    - frontend/src/superuser.js:20-27 (`signInAsBtn` exists in no HTML)
    - frontend/src/admin.js:21 (element id `invites-table-body` is wrong;
      the real id is `invite-list-body`) and admin.js never renders stats
    - cloudflareControl.html has no JS handlers bound (unlike repoControl.html)
    - frontend/src/sandboxEngine.js:64-74 splices inside forEach, skipping
      collectibles
    - frontend/src/developerWorkspace.js:649-655 wipes the success message it
      just set, because clearUpload() clears #uploadStatus
    - frontend/src/sharedInvites.js:23-42 — reissue calls a non-existent
      route, and the expiry badge uses createdAt instead of the API's
      expiresAt; `new Date('2026-09-29 14:00:00')` is Invalid Date in Safari

20) Repo hygiene — remaining tracked junk.

    STILL TRACKED, STILL WRONG:
      * logiNNotes.txt — contains a bcrypt hash.
      * assets/images/mud01.nk — an 11MB Nuke script.

    CORRECTED 2026-10-02: this item claimed "~40 editor backup files (*~,
    #file#) are tracked". They are not, and cannot be — git ls-files matches no
    file whose name contains a tilde or a hash. That line had been carried
    forward without being rechecked, which is how every stale claim in this file
    starts.

    The backups do still exist ON DISK though — 36 of them, through
    frontend/src/components and docs. Harmless to git, but they are the exact
    class of file the upload route now refuses, and they will keep appearing for
    as long as an editor writes them.

21) Guard req.body destructuring (Express 5 behaviour change).
    Express 5 sets req.body to undefined when no body parser matched, where
    Express 4 gave {}. So any unguarded `const { x } = req.body` throws a
    TypeError and returns a 500 instead of a clean 400 when a client posts
    without a JSON content-type. Affected, all outside a try block:

        backend/routes/auth.js:10           (username, password)
        backend/routes/superuser.js         (github/keys, cloudflare/keys)
        backend/routes/inviteDevs.js:42     (email, role)
        backend/server.js                   login-step1, login-step2,
                                            create-invite, register-invited

    Fix is mechanical: `const { x } = req.body ?? {};`, or use `req.body?.x`.
    backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js already does this correctly.
    Low severity (malformed requests only) but trivially avoided.

22) [DONE 2026-10-01] npm audit is CLEAN — 0 vulnerabilities.

    nodemailer 9.0.5 -> 10.0.13, done with the email work rather than before
    it, as planned: bumping a major version with no call sites would have meant
    validating it against nothing. The mail path is now exercised by tests
    against a stub relay, so the bump was checked against real use.

    Worth recording a mistake made on the way: the first attempt installed
    `nodemailer@^7`, which DOWNGRADED from 9.0.5 and left the advisory in
    place. `npm audit` still reported 1 high severity, which is what caught it.
    The fix was 10.x.


23) REGRESSION GUARD: keep the fallbacks last, and test it.
    backend/server.js section 8 holds the /api JSON 404 and the SPA catch-all.
    Express matches in registration order, so a catch-all registered too early
    silently swallows every route declared below it. That is not theoretical --
    it is exactly how login-step1, login-step2, the Google OAuth routes,
    create-invite, register-invited and setup-2fa all became unreachable and
    login broke with a "Not found" body.

    Nothing else in the test suite catches this, so there is now a check:

        npm run check:routes      # scripts/check-route-order.js

    It stubs listen(), walks app.router.stack and fails if any route or
    mounted router sits after the catch-all. Verified in both directions: it
    reports all 8 shadowed handlers against 50baa00 and passes on the current
    tree. Run it before committing any change to server.js.

24) Known-missing DOM ids, listed by npm run check:dom.
    Real gaps, not typos. Each is exempted in scripts/check-dom-ids.js with its
    reason, so a NEW missing id still fails the check while these stay visible.

      runDiagnosticsBtn  (superuser.js)
        Bound to POST /api/superuser/developer/diagnostics, which works. The
        button lived in developerInviteExp.html — a component only ever built
        into the DEVELOPER dashboard — so it was never on the superuser page
        that binds it. Needs adding to the superuser panel.

      signInAsBtn  (superuser.js)
        The entry point for impersonation. Deferred by decision: impersonation
        is how an admin reaches inside a developer workspace, and it is the
        ONLY way in — there is deliberately no admin read path.

      invite-link-input  (sharedInvites.js)
        copyInviteLink() selects it, but nothing renders a generated link with
        that id, so the copy helper does nothing. Belongs with the sharedInvites
        rework above.

    Remove an entry when the control is added or the code is deleted.

25) DEVELOPER PAGE: browse the game store, to reuse approved assets.
    A developer should be able to browse what is already in the game store —
    particularly IMAGES and MODELS — and pull it into their own work, rather
    than re-uploading a copy of something the store already holds.

    CORRECTED 2026-10-09: "IMAGES and MODELS" IS NOW WRONG. An image is NEVER in
    the game store — it lives in the IMAGE LIBRARY (items 57 and 58) — so what a
    developer browses HERE is models and other store assets, and images are
    browsed in the LIBRARY. The mechanism is unchanged and the correction is to
    the destination, not the idea: both are "browse what exists and pull it in
    rather than re-upload a copy".

    This is the consumption side of the model, and it is the same ground as
    GAME-SPACE READ below: neither exists, because both were deferred until
    the game itself is being built. Recorded separately because it is a
    DEVELOPER-facing requirement with its own shape — browsing by type,
    previewing an image or a model, and importing into a workspace — whereas
    game-space read is the access layer underneath it.

    What it needs, when the time comes:

      * a /api/game router, and the THIRD access class: workspace content is
        the owning developer only, game-store content is readable by any
        authenticated developer, release management NOT DECIDED — see item 53.
      * import into a workspace, writing workspace_assets.repository_asset_id
        and repository_base_version_id.

        CORRECTED 2026-10-15: BOTH COLUMNS EXIST, AND ONE OF THEM HAS BEEN
        WRITTEN FOR A FORTNIGHT. This said "neither has ever been written".
        repository_asset_id IS written — by the APPROVE route, so it means "this
        BECAME that" (item 59's finding). The import writes the same column at
        CHECK-OUT, meaning "this CAME FROM that". IN THE LINKED CASE THEY
        COINCIDE, which is why one column serves both directions.

        AND repository_base_version_id IS THE ONE THAT HAS NEVER HAD A PURPOSE.
        The import gives it one: THE VERSION YOU STARTED FROM. Import bigTree v3
        and it records v3 — so the store version you began at is a FACT rather
        than something guessed by comparing file sets, which cannot be done
        reliably anyway once a single file has changed.

        That is what would finally make "pick up approved work and continue"
        possible.
      * preview for the types that can be previewed: images inline (with the
        usual care — octet-stream, never the stored mime), and models via a
        glTF viewer. See docs/assettypes01 for why only some formats are
        renderable.

    Note the quota interaction: content pulled from the game store is
    EXCLUDED from the workspace limit, because it is not what the workspace is
    staging. That is already implemented, so importing approved work is free —
    which is the point.

    --- THE RETURN HALF, PUT PLAINLY. 2026-10-15. ---

    *"The round trip is not complete - the dev has to be able to search and
    download stuff from the asset store - and download to local computer"*.

    THE PRODUCER HALF NOW RUNS END TO END — upload, check, submit, approve,
    stored, provenance recorded, one copy of every byte. NOTHING CONSUMES IT.

    AND IT IS TWO STEPS, NOT ONE, WHICH IS WHY "download" NEEDS SPLITTING:

        THE STORE -> A WORKSPACE     "IMPORT" — this item. Writes
                                     repository_asset_id and
                                     repository_base_version_id, NEITHER OF
                                     WHICH HAS EVER BEEN WRITTEN.
        THE WORKSPACE -> THE DISK    "DOWNLOAD" — item 59 and docs/assettypes01
                                     §5B, which specifies it down to the manifest
                                     format and the security rules with it.

    SO "THE DEV CAN SEARCH AND DOWNLOAD FROM THE STORE" IS BOTH ITEMS, AND
    NEITHER EXISTS. Neither is hard on its own; what makes it one piece of work
    is that the second is useless without the first, and the first is useless
    until somebody wants the files on their machine.

    AND "SEARCH" WAS NOT IN THIS ITEM BEFORE. It said "browsing by type", which
    is a FILTER — the library already has a name-and-tag search whose shape this
    should copy rather than reinvent, and the tags on a store asset are a
    question this item has never asked.

    --- THE OTHER HALF: TELL THEM ON THE WAY IN ---

    NOT IMPLEMENTED, and it is the sentence this item opens with. The lookup
    exists — shaInGameStore in routes/developerWorkspace.js — but it is used in
    exactly ONE place, the quota projection, and only to avoid charging for
    content the store already holds. It never refuses and never says anything.

    SO TODAY: a developer can upload content that is already in the game store,
    and nothing tells them. Note what that does and does NOT do — there is ONE
    AND ONLY ONE COPY of any SHA-256 in the project, enforced by
    UNIQUE (sha256) on stored_files, so this writes NO new bytes to disk. The
    existing row is reused and the developer's version simply references it.
    Nothing is duplicated and no quota is charged.

    The cost is not storage, then. It is that the developer silently does
    something pointless and is never offered the better path — pulling the
    approved copy — which is what this item exists to provide.

    WHAT IT SHOULD DO: detect it and say so, naming the asset it already exists
    as. Then the developer pulls the approved copy instead of keeping a second
    one — which is the whole point of this item, and the reason the check
    belongs with the import path rather than bolted on separately.

    --- DECIDED 2026-10-02: REFUSE THE TRANSFER, POINT AT THE EXISTING FILE ---

    If a file's content is ALREADY IN THE GAME STORE — same sha256 — the upload
    is REFUSED and the developer is TOLD WHY. What they get instead is a
    reference: their own file name, pointing at the stored file that already
    exists.

    So the bytes are never transferred and never stored twice. The developer
    keeps their name for it, the store keeps its one copy, and the developer is
    told what happened rather than the system silently doing something clever.

    WHY REFUSE RATHER THAN ACCEPT QUIETLY, which is what happens today: the
    current behaviour already reuses the stored file, but says nothing. The
    developer sees a successful upload and has no idea the content they just
    sent is already approved — so they carry on iterating on a copy of
    something the game already has. Refusing costs them one round trip and
    tells them the thing worth knowing.

    A NOTE ON THE WORDING, because this is a refusal that still produces a
    result — the file entry exists afterwards, pointing at the existing
    content. It is the TRANSFER that is refused, not the operation. If that is
    the wrong reading, it is the message and the linking step that change, not
    the detection.

    THIS SETTLES THE EARLIER OPEN QUESTION. Warn-or-refuse is answered: refuse.
    The near-match prompt remains the pattern for genuinely ambiguous cases;
    this is not one, because a matching SHA is not a judgement call.

    STILL TO SETTLE WHEN BUILDING: what "already in the store" means when only
    SOME of an upload's files match. Per-file it is unambiguous — this file's
    bytes are already approved. Per-asset it is not, and the message must not
    claim more than the evidence: "these 3 files are already in the store" is
    supportable, "this asset already exists" may not be.

26) LEAK PREVENTION — stopping a build escaping into the wild.

    Put on the list as a topic, not yet a task. No build distribution exists
    yet, so there is nothing to protect; but the DESIGN of distribution should
    account for this from the start rather than being retrofitted.

    CORRECTED 2026-10-09 — THE PORTAL DOES NOT DISTRIBUTE BUILDS, so this is not
    the portal's design to make (items 60, 62, 67). The build machine produces a
    build and something puts it where it can be fetched; THE PORTAL RECORDS WHICH
    CHANGELIST IT IS AND WHO MAY SEE IT. Everything below is still worth knowing,
    and most of it now applies to whatever actually serves the bytes.

    --- AND THE ONE PART THAT IS THE PORTAL'S: DECIDED 2026-10-09 ---

    ASKED, AND THEN ANSWERED: *"Does the portal broker access to a build it does
    not serve?"* — WAY TWO, THE PORTAL BROKERS.

    WHAT THAT MEANS, CONCRETELY. A tester asks for 0.4-rc2. The portal checks the
    account is live and allowed to see candidates, ASKS THE SERVING SIDE FOR A
    TEMPORARY SIGNED LINK, and hands it over. THE FILE SERVER NEVER LEARNS WHO THE
    TESTER IS; IT HONOURS LINKS THE PORTAL SIGNED. THE PORTAL NEVER HOLDS THE
    BYTES.

        way one — the portal shows a URL. Anyone with the link has the build,
        for ever, and nothing is written down.
        WAY TWO — THE PORTAL DECIDES WHO MAY FETCH; THE FILE SERVER SERVES.

    WHY WAY TWO WON: it is the difference between a build link that circulates
    for ever and one that dies in fifteen minutes, AND IT IS THE ONLY WAY TO
    ANSWER "WHO LEAKED THIS". The attribution this item calls the highest-value
    leak defence IS the portal's download log, and way one cannot produce it.

    AND IT IS A LEGITIMATE GATE, not one of item 65's forbidden ones. Item 65 is
    about not putting a form in front of what Perforce already allows a
    DEVELOPER. A testers' build download is ACCESS CONTROL, and the portal is the
    right place for it because IT IS THE ONLY SYSTEM THAT HAS THE ACCOUNTS.

    --- WHAT IT REQUIRES, AND THE PRICE ---

      * THE SERVING SIDE MUST SUPPORT TEMPORARY SIGNED LINKS. That constrains
        where builds may live — it is no longer anywhere; it is anywhere that can
        sign. Worth knowing BEFORE choosing where the build machine puts things.

      * THE PORTAL HOLDS A SIGNING KEY. A SECRET, so it belongs with the others
        (item 42), and it is now the most consequential secret in the system: it
        is the key to every build.

      * AND THE PRICE, WHICH IS WORTH SAYING OUT LOUD BECAUSE IT UNDOES SOMETHING
        PRAISED IN ITEM 67: IF THE PORTAL IS DOWN, NOBODY CAN DOWNLOAD A BUILD.
        Item 67's appeal was that neither system needs the other to be up. THAT
        HOLDS FOR EVERYTHING EXCEPT THIS. It is a deliberate trade — control and
        attribution bought with a dependency — and it is the right way round for
        builds, which are the thing worth controlling.

    Everything else in this item — the per-user binary marks, the HWID binding,
    the visible-overlay warning — is about the RUNNING GAME and belongs to the
    build machine, not here.

    THE PREMISE: it cannot be prevented. Anyone with a copy can leak it. Every
    real technique is deterrence, attribution or damage limitation.

    Attribution is the highest-value one for a studio this size — knowing WHO
    leaked is worth more than slowing anyone down. Spec 01 already says
    "Dynamic account telemetry is embedded in client binaries for leak
    tracing", which is this. Practical forms: per-user binary edits (different
    constants, reordered functions) or steganographic marks in assets/shaders.
    A visible username overlay is worth little — it is cropped in seconds.

    Also already in spec 01, and worth keeping when distribution is built:
      * HWID binding — "session tokens are tied to Hardware ID (HWID) hashes
        upon launch". Does not stop a determined attacker; kills casual
        sharing, which is most of it.
      * short-lived user-signed download URLs — limits the window in which a
        build can be grabbed.

    Revocation ALREADY WORKS and needs nothing built: authenticateToken
    re-reads is_active on every request, so deactivating an account takes
    effect immediately rather than at token expiry. That covers both a
    developer and a tester.

    Staged content is the cheapest lever: do not put anything in an early
    build that could not afford to leak — no debug symbols, no source, no
    unreleased audio.

    DELIBERATELY NOT DRM. Denuvo-class protection is expensive, fragile,
    degrades the game, and delays rather than prevents. Wrong trade for
    pre-release builds shared with testers.

    AND THE PART THAT CANNOT BE ENGINEERED AWAY: developers have the source
    and assets on their machines because that is the job. Leak risk there is a
    trust and contract matter. Testers are the group the portal can actually
    control, which is why its access rules matter more for them.

27) DISCUSSION BOARD — SELF-HOSTED MATRIX / ELEMENT. Decided, not built.
    Full design note: docs/discussionboard01. Read that before starting.

    Requirements: open source, hosted in the UK or the EU, and no age-based
    message deletion. Slack fails on retention; Discord was chosen and then
    rejected on jurisdiction (US company, US servers, no EU residency, CLOUD
    Act exposure).

    WHAT WE WOULD RUN: Synapse + PostgreSQL + Element Web behind nginx, on a
    UK/EU machine. ~4 GB / 2 cores, EUR 5-10/month, no per-user fees.

    WHY SYNAPSE and not the lighter homeservers: Conduit's lineage is a mess
    (conduwuit discontinued 2025, work scattered across forks) and Dendrite is
    in maintenance mode. Decisively, the admin API and OIDC/SSO — the features
    the portal actually wants — exist only in Synapse. Cheap to run and unable
    to integrate is the wrong trade.

    THE POINT OF SELF-HOSTING IS INTEGRATION, not just jurisdiction. A
    third-party board means revoking portal access does NOT revoke board
    access. Self-hosting lets that be fixed: the portal's admin API calls can
    create a Matrix user on registration and DEACTIVATE it on deactivation, so
    a revoked tester loses the board the same moment they lose the portal.
    That is rung 2 of three in the note; rung 1 is the link-out that exists
    today, rung 3 is full SSO.

    THE DEPENDENCY IS NOW MET: account deactivation exists (admin panel,
    Accounts table; authenticateToken reads is_active and deactivating writes
    it, taking effect on the next request). So rung 2 has something to hook
    into when the board is built.

    WHAT EXISTS NOW, deliberately product-agnostic: frontend/discussion.html
    links out via VITE_DISCUSSION_URL and names no provider, so pointing it at
    the Element instance is one config line. Blank means the page says "not
    configured" rather than offering a dead link, which is the state today.

    STILL TO DO — all in the note's section 7 and 8: choose the machine (UK or
    EU, and it decides the whole jurisdiction argument), decide whether voice
    is needed (TURN and a lot of complexity go with it), decide room structure
    and whether roles mean anything on the board, and set retention explicitly
    since with self-hosting it is a config decision rather than a vendor's
    promise.

    Also: the manual's Discussion sections, which still describe a board we
    would have built rather than the one that exists. (They used to say this
    about the onboarding docs; those are gone and the manual carries the same
    text.)

    --- WHO CAN SEE THE BOARD, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS WHO CAN SIGN IN ---

    The board has its own membership and its own idea of which rooms you belong
    to. Being signed in to the portal does not mean being a member, and it
    certainly does not mean being a member of everything on it.

    A discussion block used to sit on the SIGN-IN FORM and the INVITATION PAGE —
    two pages seen ONLY by people who are not signed in — describing channels,
    direct messages and @mentions as though they were waiting inside. Removed
    from both. It promised the thing the portal cannot promise, to the one
    audience least able to check.

    IT IS STILL REACHABLE, and removing the links did not change that:

      * /discussion.html is served from frontend/dist like every other page, so
        a request for it gets a 200 with no session at all.
      * The board's URL is baked into the JavaScript bundle by VITE_ at build
        time, so when the board is configured the address will be readable by
        anyone who fetches the bundle — gating the page would not hide it.

    THE FIX IS NOT A PAGE GUARD. The URL has to come from an AUTHENTICATED
    ENDPOINT at runtime rather than being compiled into the bundle, and the page
    that displays it has to be served the way the manual is — outside
    frontend/dist, because express.static is mounted first and anything in that
    directory is fetchable by anyone whatever guards the route. Same reasoning
    as the manual, same shape of answer.

    --- AND DIRECT MESSAGES SHOULD BE VISIBLE WITHOUT GOING TO THE BOARD ---

    A direct message on the board should show at the TOP OF EVERY SIGNED-IN
    PAGE, as a note saying there are messages — so nobody has to remember to go
    and look. This is what the utility bar was built to have room for, and it is
    the first thing that belongs in it after the manual and the bin.

    BLOCKED ON THE BOARD EXISTING, and on the portal being able to ask it
    anything: this needs an API on the portal side that talks to the board, not
    a page that links to it. Do not build a placeholder that shows nothing —
    an indicator that is always empty is worse than no indicator, because
    people stop looking at it.


28) ADMIN AND SUPERUSER PAGES — to build. WHAT GOES ON THEM.

    The dashboards exist but are thin. What follows is the running list of
    things that either already exist with nothing calling them, or that the
    portal needs and has nowhere to show. Add to it rather than starting a new
    item.

    --- THE MENU NOW EXISTS AND NAMES ALL OF IT (2026-10-02) ---

    A menu was added to adminPanel.html, so it appears on BOTH the admin and the
    superuser dashboard — the same reason the accounts logic lives in a shared
    file. It carries every entry below that is a VIEW:

        Accounts  |  Invites  |  Reports  |  Sessions  |  Storage  |
        Health  |  Audit log

    THE TWO KINDS BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, ON PURPOSE. Accounts and Invites are
    BUILT, so they are plain anchors onto the panels already on the page — they
    scroll, and they do not pretend to be pages that do not exist. Everything
    else is a STUB: it reports itself as missing and names the TODO item it
    belongs to, via frontend/src/notBuilt.js, which wires any element carrying
    data-not-built.

    WHY STUBS RATHER THAN NOTHING. The shape of these pages is known and the work
    is recorded here, so the menu names it rather than the list living only in a
    document. And a menu entry that does nothing when clicked is indistinguishable
    from a bug — a stub tells you where to look instead of leaving you to guess.

    THEY ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TABS, and deliberately DO NOT switch panes. An
    empty pane reads as a broken page, which is worse than an honest "not built".

    DELETE THE STUB AS EACH FEATURE LANDS. When the last one goes,
    frontend/src/notBuilt.js goes with it.

    --- ALREADY BUILT, NOTHING CALLS IT ---

    ASSET CATEGORY RETAG. POST /assets/:assetId/category exists, is tested, and
    is admin/superuser only — but there is no admin view of workspace assets to
    hang a button on, so the only way to change a category today is a terminal.
    Give it a home; do not add a second endpoint.

    Read the ASSET CATEGORY block above for why it is a deliberate exception to
    the impersonation rule. That reasoning has to survive being put behind a
    button.

    BUG REPORTS — [DONE 2026-10-02]. A list with a status filter, each report
    showing its kind, who sent it and when, what they wrote, the page they were
    on as a link, and a reply address where one was left. Resolving takes an
    optional note, and the panel says when one is skipped. Anonymous reports
    are shown as information — "sent without signing in" — rather than as
    missing data.

    frontend/src/adminReports.html + adminReports.js, listed in build.js for
    BOTH dashboards the same way the accounts table is.

    --- NEEDED, AND BLOCKED ON SOMETHING ELSE ---

    WHO IS LOGGED IN. There is no data behind this today. Login is a stateless
    JWT in a cookie, so the server keeps no record of who holds a live token —
    the `sessions` table in db.js is fully defined and has NEVER HAD A ROW
    written to it by anything. Nothing writes it and nothing reads it.

    So this panel is not a UI job; it is blocked on item 10. Recording a
    session per login (and checking it in authenticateToken) is what makes
    "who is logged in", "log this device out" and real revocation possible —
    three features from one piece of work. Build item 10 first, then this is
    just a SELECT.

    Worth deciding when building: whether "logged in" means a live session row
    or "seen in the last N minutes". With a jti-per-login record those are the
    same question; without one, neither can be answered.

    --- NEEDED, NOTHING IN THE WAY ---

    DELETING FROM THE GAME ASSET STORE. Its own item — see 31 — because it is
    bigger than a control on a page: it needs a route, a confirmation that
    names what will be destroyed, and a decision about history. Listed here so
    whoever builds these pages does not build the panel without it and then
    find the asset management has nowhere to live.

    SETTINGS — [DONE 2026-10-05, see item 37]. A Settings tab on both
    dashboards, four values behind it, and a mechanism that reads them when it
    uses them rather than when it starts.

    WHERE THIS SECTION NOW STANDS: Storage and Settings are built. Sessions is
    still blocked on item 10 (nothing records a session). Health has not been
    started — it needs memory and CPU beside the disk reading that
    hostStorage() already does, and it owns the warning banner, which the
    Storage tab cannot provide from behind a tab.

    STORAGE — [DONE 2026-05-31]. Reworked after review: the storage allowance
    is now a setting, the developer table sorts, and the wording no longer talks
    about "charging". See item 38. The 2026-05-10 note below still describes the
    two figures and why they do not reconcile.

    STORAGE — [DONE 2026-05-10]. The panel is built and shows both figures,
    side by side, with the note explaining why they do not reconcile. The two
    are now LIBRARIES rather than route-local code, because each is asked on
    more than one screen and the answers must not diverge:

      * backend/lib/workspaceUsage.js — what a developer is CHARGED.
      * backend/lib/hostStorage.js    — what the DISK holds.

    AND THE DEVELOPER'S NUMBER IS THE SAME FUNCTION. The admin tab does not sum
    the tables itself; it calls workspaceUsage() per workspace, the same call
    the developer's own panel makes. Two implementations would eventually
    disagree, and the disagreement would be invisible until somebody compared
    two screens — one developer being told they have room while the admin is
    told they do not. Same function, same answer, by construction.

    The status bands (ok / warning / critical) come from shared/usageStatus.mjs
    so the disk and a quota cannot mean different things by "nearly full", and
    the CLIENT IS NOT ASKED TO AGREE — it is sent a band and displays it.

    INODES ARE SHOWN WITH THE SPACE, which was not in the original plan and
    belongs there: a content-addressed store holds one file per SHA and game
    assets include a great many small ones, so the file table can run out while
    terabytes remain. It fails identically — every write refused — and a panel
    that showed only free space would call that disk healthy.

    STILL OPEN HERE:
      * The warning has to be VISIBLE WITHOUT GOING LOOKING, which this tab is
        not: it lives behind a tab. The banner belongs in the header or the
        utility bar, and that is the Health panel's job.
      * THRESHOLDS ARE HARDCODED at 85/95 in shared/usageStatus.mjs. Item 32
        wants them admin-editable through the settings mechanism, which does not
        exist yet. When it lands, the constants move behind it and the worker
        must READ the setting rather than cache it at boot.
      * (Done, in the change below.) The blob directory path was written out in
        SIX places — this lib, jobs/daily.js, the upload route, two reset
        scripts and scripts/check-store.js — and is now defined once.

    The rest of this section is the reasoning that produced the above, kept
    because the accounting rules are easy to break later:

    PER DEVELOPER. The data exists and is already defined the way we
    agreed: workspaceUsage() sums a workspace's
    files against a quota, and workspaceStoredFileIds explicitly EXCLUDES
    anything that lives in the game store —

        AND wvf.stored_file_id NOT IN (SELECT stored_file_id FROM asset_version_files)

    So this number counts only what the developer owns: nothing shared with the
    game store counts against them, however many bytes it occupies on disk.
    Same rule on the way in — shaInGameStore() lets an upload of content the
    game already holds through without charging the quota. That is the decision,
    already implemented; the admin panel just needs to display it per developer
    and biggest-first.

    See WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE above for the full rule. One part of it
    is easy to get wrong later, so it is repeated here: a workspace is PRIVATE,
    and the game store is the only shared content in the system. Two developers
    uploading identical bytes get one copy on disk — content addressing cannot
    tell the same bytes from the same file — but that is deduplication, not
    sharing, and both are still charged, because each is staging their own copy.

    STORAGE, IN TOTAL — [DONE 2026-05-10, in backend/lib/hostStorage.js, which
    uses statfs: total, used, available and percent on the volume the blobs are
    actually on, plus the inode table. It reports bavail rather than bfree so
    the root reserve is not counted as room a process can use.]

    A DIFFERENT NUMBER, AND A DIFFERENT TOOL. The real
    question "are we about to run out of disk" is a HARDWARE question, not an
    accounting one, and the honest answer is df, not a sum of the tables. The
    per-developer figures above are what each developer is charged; they are not
    expected to add up to the disk, and they must not be presented as if they
    should. A file in the game store is charged to nobody and still occupies
    bytes. Show the filesystem: total, used, available, and percent — on the
    volume the store is actually on, whether that is a mounted disk, an object
    store, or somewhere else entirely.

    WARN WHEN IT IS GETTING FULL — STILL OPEN, AND STILL THE HEALTH PANEL'S
    JOB. The thresholds are now settings (item 37) and the bands they produce
    are live on the Storage tab, so the hard part is done. What is missing is
    the part that matters: THE ALERT IS BEHIND A TAB. An administrator has to
    go and look, which fails "visible without going looking". The banner belongs
    in the header or the utility bar, and it needs memory and CPU beside it,
    which hostStorage() does not cover.

    AND IT HAS TO BE READABLE BY CLAUDE. The admin needs to be able to discuss a
    problem and get help fixing it, which means the state of the system has to
    come out in a form that can be COPIED AND PASTED into a conversation —
    structured, complete, no truncation, stable field names, plain text or JSON.
    A screenshot is not this. Assume whoever pastes it is pasting it verbatim
    and by hand.

    THAT MAKES ONE REQUIREMENT ABSOLUTE: NO SECRETS IN IT. No password hashes,
    no tokens, no API keys, no SMTP credentials, no session cookies — nothing
    that would matter if it ended up in a chat log, a ticket, or a screenshot of
    a chat log. Redact by construction rather than by remembering: build the
    report from an explicit list of fields rather than by dumping a table and
    trimming it, because a dump grows a column the day someone adds one.

    --- ALREADY EXISTS, MAY WANT A VIEW ---

    The Accounts table (built), the invite list (built), GET /api/admin/stats
    (built: active/total users, pending invitations). The audit log is written
    by deactivation and by every category retag, feedback resolve, restore,
    purge and daily job run — and is readable by nothing at all. Worth a view
    for the same reason as the rest, and it is now the last of the
    "we already record this and nobody can see it" group.

    --- LAYOUT INCONSISTENCY — [SETTLED 2026-10-02]. The admin page is TABS,
    the same as the developer page.

    Frontend/src/adminTabs.js switches sections by id, so the panes do not have
    to be siblings in one .tab-content — which they cannot be, because these
    panels are separate components that build.js concatenates and no one of
    them can wrap the others. The nav lists EVERY section including the
    superuser-only ones, and an entry whose section is not on the page is
    removed, so the admin dashboard shows its tabs and the superuser dashboard
    shows its own from one file.

    The container oddity is also gone: devInvites.html no longer wraps itself
    in `<section class="container my-4">`; it is a plain section holding two
    cards, like the other panels.

    --- AND STILL ABSENT ON PURPOSE ---

    There is no admin read path into a developer's workspace. An admin who needs
    to see inside one does it by IMPERSONATION, which is marked, time-bounded
    and logged. Do not add a standing browse-anything view while building these
    pages, however convenient it looks next to the retag control.


29) BUG REPORT / FEEDBACK FORM — BUILT. Admins cannot read them yet.

    A form for reporting a fault or giving feedback, reachable from every page,
    signed in or not. The button is bottom-right on all eight pages.

    BUILT: shared/feedbackRules.mjs (the kinds and the limits, used by both
    sides), the feedback table, POST /api/feedback (open to everyone),
    GET /api/feedback and POST /api/feedback/:id/resolve (admin/superuser),
    optionalAuth in the auth middleware so an open route can still learn who is
    asking, and frontend/src/feedback.js — which BUILDS ITS OWN MARKUP, so a
    page gets the button from one script tag rather than a partial that has to
    be listed in build.js AND pasted into each hand-written page.

    VERIFIED, not assumed: anonymous reports store a null user; a signed-in
    report stores the actor from the token; and a body claiming
    user_id/username/role/created_at is IGNORED ENTIRELY — the row was recorded
    as the token's user with the server's clock, which is the whole point.

    NOT BUILT: no UI reads them. GET and resolve are API-only, which means
    reports currently go somewhere nobody looks. Same gap as item 28, and the
    same fix — they want building together.

    WHO AND WHEN ARE FILLED IN AUTOMATICALLY AND ON THE SERVER. The actor comes
    from the session token and the timestamp from the server clock. Neither is
    taken from the client: a client-supplied "who" is a claim, not a fact, and a
    client-supplied "when" is whatever the reporter's machine happens to think
    the time is. A report that cannot be trusted about who sent it is not worth
    collecting.

    WHAT THE CLIENT DOES supply is the one thing only it knows — the page and
    the URL the report is about. That is the "where", and without it a report
    costs a round trip to interpret. It is still untrusted input: truncated and
    escaped like any other.

    ANONYMOUS IS ALLOWED ON THE PUBLIC PAGES. Being unable to sign in is the
    single most likely thing to want to report, and by definition that person
    has no session. Those reports record no user, which is itself information —
    it says the report came from someone who could not get in.

    THREE OPEN QUESTIONS, AND ONE OF THEM WAS ANSWERED BY BUILDING IT:

    * KIND — SETTLED. A kind field was built: three options, Bug / Idea /
      Other, in shared/feedbackRules.mjs. Three and not ten because the point is
      to let someone triage a list at a glance, and past about three people
      start guessing; there is an explicit Other for anything that does not fit.
      UNLIKE the asset category, this one REJECTS an unknown value — the list is
      what makes the field worth having, and Other is always available, so being
      strict costs the reporter nothing. The two lists sit next to each other in
      shared/ and behave oppositely on purpose; both are commented.

    * A REPLY ADDRESS FOR ANONYMOUS REPORTS — SETTLED. Optional, shown only to
      someone with no session. Not required even then: making somebody hand over
      an address to report a login fault is a good way to get no reports.

    * WHO READS THEM — STILL OPEN, and it is item 28. The routes to list and
      resolve reports exist, are tested, and nothing calls them, so today they
      go somewhere nobody looks.


30) THE SHA STORE — audited 2026-10-02. One hole found, and it is not in the
    upload path.

    There is one and only one copy of any SHA-256 in the project, and the audit
    confirms the code holds that line. What follows is what was checked, so the
    next person does not have to re-derive it, and the one thing that is
    genuinely dangerous.

    --- VERIFIED SOUND ---

    * Exactly ONE place inserts into stored_files (the upload route). NOTHING
      DELETES FROM IT — there is no DELETE anywhere in the codebase — so a row
      can never be removed out from under a reference.

    * The hash is computed SERVER-SIDE from the bytes on disk, by streaming the
      temp file. It is never taken from the client, so a caller cannot claim a
      SHA that its content does not have.

    * storage_key IS the sha256, and the download route validates it as 64
      lowercase hex before building a path from it. Nothing client-supplied
      touches that path.

    * New content is created with linkSync, which FAILS if the name exists
      rather than overwriting. Two uploads of the same new content cannot
      silently produce two files.

    * The upload transaction is IMMEDIATE, so it takes the write lock at the
      start. better-sqlite3 is synchronous, so a second upload cannot interleave
      between the "is it in stored_files?" read and the insert.

    * Acceptance into the game store copies REFERENCES (INSERT ... SELECT from
      workspace_version_files), never the physical records. That is the
      mechanism behind the one-copy rule, and it is correct.

    * A row whose blob has gone missing is REPAIRED on the next upload of
      identical content, and a stray file at a new path is replaced rather than
      trusted.

    --- THE HOLE: NOTHING CHECKS THE DISK AGAINST THE DATABASE ---

    The database and the filesystem are two things and nothing enforced that
    they agree. A blob could be lost, or its contents corrupted, and the system
    would not notice until somebody tried to download it.

    check:store now does. `npm run check:store` verifies, in order of how much
    each matters: that every blob's bytes actually hash to the name it is stored
    under (content addressing intact — the one that matters most, since a file
    whose contents stopped matching its name means every reference serves the
    wrong thing); that no row is missing its blob; that no blob is without a
    row; and that the recorded file_size matches the disk. Read-only, so it is
    safe against a live database. --quick skips the re-hashing on a big store.

    IT FOUND ONE IMMEDIATELY, on its first run against the live store: a 6-byte
    orphan blob left by MY OWN cleanup after an upload test. The cleanup looked
    for a sharded path (uploads/<ab>/<sha>) and the store is FLAT
    (uploads/files/<sha>), so it deleted the row and left the file. Which is
    the whole argument for the check existing — that class of mistake is
    invisible without it, and I made it within a day.

    Suggested: run it before a backup and after any incident. Worth wiring into
    the admin page later (item 28) as the storage-health panel.

    --- STILL OPEN ---

    * THE RESET SCRIPTS ARE UNGUARDED — DECIDED 2026-10-02: REPLACE THEM WITH A
      DEVELOPMENT-MODE RESET OF THE ASSET STORE.

      The scripts do a real job — a clean store to test against — and they do it
      badly: backend/reset/resetAssetData.js deletes every asset table AND every
      file in the store, with NO confirmation, no dry run and no environment
      check. Its own comment records that a path bug once made it delete every
      stored_files row while reporting "nothing to delete", orphaning the
      physical files it had just untracked. That is the exact failure this item
      exists to prevent, and it has already happened once.

      The replacement is not a better script, it is THE SAME FACILITY AS ITEM 31
      behind a development-mode switch. It already has to:
        * be told what it is about to destroy, and agree to that specific list
        * refuse to run where it should not — and "where it should not" is now
          the point of the switch, not a path guess
        * leave an audit record

      SO THE DIFFERENCE FROM A NORMAL DELETE IS ONE THING: SCOPE, and the mode.
      Development mode resets the WHOLE store; a normal delete removes one
      asset. Both show what will go and both need agreement — the whole-store
      one just shows a great deal more.

      HARD RULE: IT MUST REFUSE TO RUN OUTSIDE DEVELOPMENT MODE. A production
      box that can be emptied by an admin clicking the wrong button is a worse
      failure than the scripts it replaces. Whatever the mode is read from — an
      explicit flag rather than NODE_ENV, so a mis-set environment cannot arm it
      — production must not be able to select it at all.

      AND IT MUST GO THROUGH THE SAME SAFETY AS GARBAGE COLLECTION: refuse to
      run if check:store reports the store inconsistent, because destroying a
      store you cannot trust is how a repairable problem becomes an unrepairable
      one. Note the symmetry — check:store tells you the store is sound,
      garbage collection removes what nothing references, and this removes
      everything. Only the last one is destructive by intent, and it is the one
      that most needs the other two to have run clean first.

    * A lost blob is only repaired by someone re-uploading identical content.
      check:store detects it; nothing repairs it. Acceptable while the store is
      small and rebuildable; worth revisiting before it is the only copy of
      anything that matters.


31) DELETING FROM THE GAME ASSET STORE — needed. NOTHING EXISTS.

    There is no note, spec, route or panel anywhere in the repo describing how
    to remove an approved asset from the store. That has been searched for
    explicitly, under every phrasing (delete, remove, withdraw, retract,
    unpublish, take down, purge), and the answer is nothing. The only mechanism
    in the repo that destroys store content is the unguarded reset script in
    item 30. So this is new ground rather than a continuation.

    THE NEED. Deleting from the store has to be possible, and most obviously
    during testing: the first assets pushed through the approval workflow will
    be junk, and today the only way to be rid of them is to wipe everything.
    Worth knowing: assets.is_active EXISTS ON THE TABLE AND IS READ BY NOTHING —
    the same dead-column state workspace_assets.asset_type was in. Soft delete
    was anticipated here and never wired up.

    THE REQUIREMENT: THE ADMIN IS TOLD WHAT IS BEING DELETED, AND AGREES.
    Not a dialog asking "are you sure?". The admin is shown what will actually
    go — the asset, its versions, its files, and what else references it — and
    agrees to that specific list. The same ask-and-confirm shape as the
    near-match prompt and the naming tiers: the server states the consequence,
    the human accepts it, the server acts. It belongs on the admin page with
    the rest of item 28.

    --- CONSTRAINTS THAT ALREADY EXIST AND WILL SHAPE IT ---

    * THE ONE-COPY RULE FORBIDS DELETING BLOBS. stored_files is shared, and a
      developer's workspace may still reference the same content. Deleting a
      store asset removes REFERENCES — asset_version_files rows — and never
      stored_files rows. It is then check:store's job to report any blob that
      nothing references any more.

    * THE DATABASE WILL REFUSE A NAIVE DELETE. There are no ON DELETE clauses
      anywhere, and an approved asset is referenced by asset_versions, by
      creative_approvals.repository_asset_id and repository_version_id, and by
      the same pair on asset_comments. Deletion is ordered and deliberate, or
      it fails.

    * docs/dbspec01 SAYS STORE ASSET VERSIONS ARE IMMUTABLE. That is a
      spec-level statement which deletion contradicts. It may well mean
      "immutable once released" rather than "immutable, full stop" — but that
      distinction has never been drawn, and this is where it has to be.

    * THE WORKSPACE ALREADY DOES THE OPPOSITE, ON PURPOSE. Its soft delete is
      documented as never touching the store tables, so an approved asset
      survives its author deleting their copy. That reasoning was about
      protecting history; deleting from the store has to be reconciled with it
      rather than quietly reversing it.

    --- DECIDED 2026-10-02: THEY ARE NOT THE SAME ACT ---

    WITHDRAWING SOMETHING THAT SHIPPED IS VERY EXTREME. It is a last resort,
    not a maintenance operation, and there should be LOTS OF BARS between the
    admin and the action — enough that the difficulty itself communicates how
    serious it is before anyone gets near it.

    REMOVING TEST DATA IS ORDINARY AND SHOULD BE NEARLY ONE STEP, especially
    when it is in the SANDBOX: nothing has been approved, nobody outside has
    seen it, and the only person affected is the developer who put it there.

    SO THE PANEL NEEDS TWO OPERATIONS OF DELIBERATELY DIFFERENT WEIGHT, not one
    delete with a severity setting. If they share a button and a confirmation
    dialog, that dialog becomes the only thing separating housekeeping from
    undoing a release — and no dialog can carry that distinction.

    --- THE SANDBOX HALF IS BUILT, AND IT FREES NOTHING ---

    Worth knowing before designing around it, because it is not what it looks
    like from the outside. A developer can already delete their own workspace
    content, and it is a SOFT delete: rows marked inactive, quota freed, and
    NOTHING REMOVED FROM DISK. And because nothing in the codebase ever deletes
    a stored_files row, the bytes stay forever — even after every reference to
    them is gone.

    So "just remove it from the sandbox" today is instant, safe, and frees no
    disk at all. For test data pushed through repeatedly that adds up, and it
    is invisible: the workspace shows empty while the store keeps every version
    of every abandoned experiment, charged to nobody.

    WHAT IS MISSING IS GARBAGE COLLECTION, not another delete button. A pass
    that finds stored_files rows referenced by NEITHER workspace_version_files
    NOR asset_version_files, and removes the row and the blob together.

    WHERE THE GARBAGE WOULD COME FROM: mostly the WASTEBIN PURGE — see the
    WASTEBIN section under WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE above. Delete puts
    the rows in the bin, the bin empties after 14 days and removes the file
    references, and this is what then frees the bytes. The two are one chain and
    are worth building together; a collector with nothing feeding it has nothing
    to collect.
    check:store already finds the mirror image of this — an orphan blob is the
    case where the FILE is present and the row is gone; this is the case where
    both are present and nothing points at either.

    SAFETY, because this is the one operation in the system that can destroy
    content outright:

      * It must run inside an IMMEDIATE transaction and RE-CHECK the references
        immediately before unlinking. A concurrent upload can find a
        stored_files row and be about to reference it; the check and the unlink
        cannot have a gap between them.

      * It should REFUSE TO RUN AT ALL if check:store reports the store
        inconsistent. Collecting garbage from a store you cannot trust is how a
        repairable problem becomes an unrepairable one.

      * Consider a grace period — only collect blobs older than, say, a day —
        so that a file written moments ago by an upload that is still in flight
        cannot be swept up by a pass running at the wrong moment.

    --- STILL OPEN, FOR THE STORE ITSELF ---

    * HARD OR SOFT — AND IT IS THE SAME ANSWER AS THE WORKSPACE. The same model
      applies, and it took the workspace note to see it: KEEP THE RECORD, DROP
      THE FILES. Releasing the store's asset_version_files rows is what frees
      the content; the collector frees the blob once nothing references it; and
      the asset, its versions, its approvals and its comments all stay readable.
      So it is not a choice between hard and soft — it is soft rows with the
      content released, exactly as in the workspace.

      (An earlier version of this note said that keeping the approval and
      comment history meant "the deletion is really a soft one and nothing is
      freed". That was wrong, and it was the same mistake made about the
      workspace: the RECORD is not what holds the bytes. The FILE ROWS are.)

    * SO WHAT DIFFERS IS FRICTION AND REACH, NOT MECHANISM. Withdrawing
      something that shipped is very extreme and wants lots of bars between the
      admin and it. Purging test data is ordinary and should be close to one
      step. Both end the same way — content released, record kept — and only the
      difficulty tells them apart. Two controls, deliberately different weight;
      see the DECIDED section above.

    * WHO — SETTLED 2026-10-02: ADMIN CAN DELETE. Deleting from the store is an
      admin capability. (It read "Not superuser-only ... and superuser can do it
      too by the standing rule below", and named the superuser-only capabilities
      as "deploying the game, creating branches". BOTH HALVES ARE NOW WRONG:
      superuser was removed entirely on 2026-10-06 (item 51), AND NEITHER
      DEPLOYING THE GAME NOR CREATING BRANCHES IS A PORTAL CAPABILITY AT ALL —
      deploying is the build machine's and branching is Perforce's (item 62).
      Corrected 2026-10-09 rather than deleted, because the bullet is a good
      example of a capability that was never the portal's to hold.)

      THE ROLE RULE, and it is worth stating once in full because it is easy to
      break silently: SUPERUSER CAN DO EVERYTHING AN ADMIN CAN DO, plus more.
      A route naming 'admin' must name 'superuser' too. The failure mode is
      not an error — it is the most privileged account in the system being
      refused. Verified 2026-10-02: no requireRole call anywhere allows admin
      without superuser. The rule is now also written at requireRole() in
      backend/middleware/auth.js, which is where someone would look.

    --- AND THE TESTING CASE IS ALREADY HALF-BUILT, BADLY ---

    The reset scripts do this job today, unguarded — see STILL OPEN in item 30.
    A proper delete facility on the admin page may well be what replaces them:
    the same need, but with a confirmation, an audit trail, and no way to run
    it against the wrong database by accident.


32) SCHEDULER AND GARBAGE COLLECTION — confirmed needed 2026-10-02.

    NOTHING IN THIS SYSTEM RUNS ON A TIMER. Every operation so far is triggered
    by a request arriving. That was a simplification while nothing needed a
    clock; it is now a gap, because the wastebin has a 14-day window and the
    store has garbage that only collection will free.

    --- THE SCHEDULER ---

    WHAT IT HAS TO RUN, roughly in the order they start to matter:

      * the wastebin purge — see WASTEBIN under WORKSPACE QUOTA AND SOFT DELETE
      * garbage collection — below
      * check:store, periodically, so a broken store is FOUND rather than
        discovered by somebody's download failing
      * later, whatever the admin page's thresholds need (item 28) — disk,
        memory and CPU are not much use sampled only when a page is loaded

    DESIGN QUESTIONS, none of them obvious:

      * WHAT MECHANISM. A setInterval in the server process is the least code
        and dies with the process. An external cron calling an authenticated
        endpoint survives restarts and leaves a record that it ran. node-cron
        sits in between. Given the eventual deployment is a self-hosted box,
        the external cron is probably the most honest: it does not depend on
        the app being up at the right moment, and a job that runs is
        distinguishable from a job that silently stopped.

      * MULTIPLE INSTANCES. An in-process timer runs the job once per process.
        Fine for an idempotent purge, wrong for anything that is not. Decide
        whether the jobs must be safe to run concurrently or whether something
        holds a lock — the answer probably differs per job.

      * WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A JOB FAILS. Silently is the wrong answer for a
        purge. A failed run should be visible — which means the admin page
        (item 28), and probably the audit log.

      * SHOULD IT ALSO RUN ON START? A box that was off for a month comes back
        with a month of backlog. Running once at startup is cheap, and stops the
        first request after a restart being the one that pays for it.

    --- GARBAGE COLLECTION ---

    Confirmed needed. It has TWO HALVES, and they are not equally ready:

      * ORPHAN BLOBS — a file on disk with no stored_files row. THIS ALREADY
        HAPPENS: it is exactly what check:store found on its first run, a 6-byte
        file left behind by an incomplete cleanup. Collectable today, and
        nothing else will ever remove it.

      * UNREFERENCED ROWS — a stored_files row whose content nothing points at,
        because every workspace_version_files and asset_version_files reference
        to it is gone. This is the one that frees real space and it cannot occur
        until the wastebin purge exists to create it.

    SAFETY, repeated from item 31 because it is the whole risk: re-check the
    references immediately before unlinking, inside the same IMMEDIATE
    transaction, with no gap for a concurrent upload to slip into; refuse to run
    at all if check:store reports the store inconsistent; and consider a grace
    period so a blob written moments ago by an in-flight upload cannot be swept
    up.

    AND IT SHOULD START REPORT-ONLY. A collector that deletes on its first
    scheduled run, having never once been watched, is how a store gets emptied
    by a bug. Run it, log what it WOULD remove, and turn deletion on once its
    output has been read a few times and matched what was expected.

    --- BUILD ORDER ---

    Two of the five are built, and they were the two everything else waited on:

      1. [DONE 2026-10-02] deleted_at on workspace_assets and
         workspace_versions, stamped on delete with COALESCE so re-deleting
         cannot restart the clock.
      2. [DONE 2026-10-02] Dropping the file rows: the file list is
         snapshotted as JSON first (name, path, size, hash — content never),
         then workspace_version_files goes. POST
         .../versions/:versionId/purge, and ONLY for versions already deleted,
         so the reversible step is never skipped by accident.
      3. [DONE 2026-10-02] The wastebin: a tab listing what is in the bin with
         a per-item countdown, and a restore for anything whose files are
         still there. A purged version is listed and marked unrestorable
         rather than hidden. The retention period is read from
         backend/lib/retention.js, where the scheduled purge will read it too.
      4. [DONE 2026-10-02] The scheduler: backend/jobs/daily.js, run from
         cron. One command, two jobs in order — the purge, then the
         collector. Documented in the manual with the cron line.
      5. [DONE 2026-10-02] Garbage collection, fed by the purge — and
         REPORT-ONLY by default, as decided. --collect turns deletion on.

    ALL FIVE ARE BUILT. What is left is not a step but a decision about when
    the machine actually starts doing this: the job must be IN CRON on the box
    before any of it happens on its own, and the collector should run plain for
    a while before anyone passes --collect.

    STILL NOT ADMIN-CONFIGURABLE. The retention period is a constant in
    backend/lib/retention.js. The decision was that an administrator changes it
    from the admin page, which needs the settings mechanism the schema still
    does not have (item 28). The value moves in ONE place when it exists —
    the bin and the job both read it — so nothing else has to change.

    NOTE WHAT STEP 2 DOES NOT DO, because the next step depends on it: it
    removes the REFERENCES, not the content. The blob is still on disk and will
    be until step 5 exists, and check:store reports the store as healthy
    throughout — correctly, because those rows DO still have their blobs. What
    is gone is the referrers, and finding those is the collector's job.

    THE FOREIGN KEY REBUILD THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE. It was only required
    if version and asset rows were going to be deleted, and they are not — the
    rows ARE the history being kept. Only the file rows go, and nothing
    references those. So the riskiest item in this plan is gone, and the design
    that removed it keeps MORE information than the one that needed it.

    Step 1 is small and unblocks everything. Steps 4 and 5 are the only
    genuinely new machinery; the rest is more of what already exists.


33) THE DEVELOPER'S ASSET HISTORY — BUILT 2026-10-02. One piece missing.

    All the assets a developer has worked on, including ones no longer live,
    each opening to show what happened to every version of it.

    WHY IT IS NOT THE LIST THAT ALREADY EXISTED. GET /assets filters to
    is_active = 1, which is right for what it is — the workspace a developer
    works IN right now — but it cannot show anything that has LEFT it. The
    moment a version was deleted or superseded, the record of what happened to
    it disappeared from sight. It was never lost; it had nowhere to be seen.

    BUILT:

      * GET /assets/history — every asset, live or not, every version, live or
        not, with the technical check result and the reason for a failure, the
        approval decision and the reviewer's comment, and whether the version
        is in the game store.

      * Navigation, where this dashboard previously had NONE: two tabs, Upload
        and History. History loads lazily on first open, because most visits
        are to upload rather than to look back.

      * The version renderer is the SAME ONE the workspace uses
        (renderVersionState), so a version reads identically in both views and
        there is no second implementation to drift. The endpoint's field names
        were changed to latest_check / latest_approval to match GET /assets for
        exactly that reason.

    VERIFIED, not assumed: an admin asking for history gets their OWN empty
    history and not the developer's, and an unauthenticated call is refused.
    That route is more tempting than most to open up, because it is read-only
    and looks harmless — but it is the developer's whole working record, so
    impersonation stays the only way in.

    STILL MISSING: THE DOWNLOAD — now a named stub. The developer dashboard has
    a "Download" entry beside Upload and History that reports itself as not
    built (frontend/src/notBuilt.js). The page also says plainly, on any version
    that is in the game store, that download is not built yet.

    It is the thing that makes "you can always download a copy" true rather than
    merely stated, and it is the same piece as item 25's import path. The
    manifest endpoint already exists and has never been called by anything; a
    per-file download is the wrong answer at fifty files, so this wants a ZIP.

    There is also a "Game store" stub, which is item 25 — browsing what is
    already approved rather than re-uploading it.

    AND THE PER-VERSION FILE RECORD IS NOT THERE YET, because nothing drops
    file rows yet. When files are dropped, the history should show what was
    uploaded from the record kept in their place — see THE WORKSPACE IS A
    STAGING AREA WITH A HISTORY.

    THE STAGING-AREA NOTICE is on the My Workspace card in the Upload tab, which
    is where a developer lands. If the navigation is ever reworked, keep it
    somewhere they actually arrive at.


34) FILE TYPE VERIFICATION — BUILT 2026-10-02. ONE function, one list.

    The asset-type field was taken off the upload page on the grounds that a
    developer should not be asked to restate what the file already says: Maya,
    Blender and Nuke are TOOLS, and the tool is a property of the file. That was
    done; the verifier that was supposed to replace it was NOT, and the plan had
    never been written down anywhere. Both halves are now done.

    ONE AND ONLY ONE FUNCTION. assetTypeOf(filename) in shared/assetRules.mjs.
    Nothing anywhere else classifies a file and nothing anywhere else keeps a
    list of extensions. It is the function the project asked for, in the place
    the project puts shared rules.

    THE LIST OF EXTENSIONS EXISTS ONCE, NOT TWICE. RESERVED_EXTENSIONS — the
    list answering "is this name a file or a folder?" — is now DERIVED from the
    type map, because both need the same vocabulary and two hand-kept copies of
    it would have drifted. One list, two questions. check:naming asserts the
    two agree, and that no extension appears under two types.

    WHERE IT RUNS: the technical check, as a real check with a pass/fail and a
    message, recorded like every other check. An UNRECOGNISED EXTENSION FAILS
    THE RUN rather than being shrugged at — it means nobody can say what the
    file is, and a check that ignores that is not checking anything.

    AND IT FILLS THE COLUMN, WHEN THERE IS ONE ANSWER. workspace_assets.
    asset_type is set from the check — but ONLY when every file in the version
    is the same type. An asset holding a .blend and forty .png textures has no
    single type, and reporting the most common would call it an Image asset when
    everyone would call it a Blender one. A confident wrong answer is worse than
    none, so it stays null and the per-file types are in the check message.
    COALESCE, so an ambiguous version cannot wipe a type an earlier one set.

    VERIFIED against a running server, three cases: one type sets the column
    ("Blender"); mixed content reports "Blender, Image" and leaves the column
    alone; an unknown extension fails the run and names the file.

    WHICH EXTENSIONS, AND WHERE THEY CAME FROM. 131 extensions across 21 types,
    drawn from what the engines and marketplaces actually accept rather than
    from a generic "file extensions" dump — Epic's Fab asset requirements and
    Unity's import list are the two most concrete published sources. Ordered
    GAME DEVELOPMENT FIRST: engines, then the tools that feed them, then the
    interchange and file formats around them.

    The order carries no behaviour; it is there so the common case sits at the
    top for whoever extends it next.

    DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED, and it is a decision worth keeping: tracker audio
    (.mod .xm .it .s3m), CAD (.dwg), and legacy web 3D (.wrl .x3d). All real
    formats, none common in production — and every extension added is another
    name that stops being usable as an asset name. check:naming asserts the
    exclusions stay excluded, so a later bulk-import cannot quietly undo it.

    --- WHAT THE REAL CHECKS MUST GROW INTO ---

    The check runs as extension-only for now. Two more are wanted before these
    can be called the real checks, and both are about the same thing: believing
    a file's NAME instead of the file.

    * NO FILE IS EMPTY — BUILT 2026-10-02. A zero-byte file passes every other
      check here: it exists, its size matches what was recorded, its path is
      unique, and its extension is recognised. And it is never a real asset —
      it is a failed export, an interrupted write, or a placeholder somebody
      meant to come back to. Verified: an empty .blend passed files_present,
      the per-file check AND the type check, and failed only this one.

    * THE CONTENTS MUST MATCH THE EXTENSION — BUILT 2026-10-02. The extension
      is a CLAIM; this is what turns it into something a reviewer can rely on,
      or says plainly that it cannot.

      FOUR OUTCOMES, NOT TWO, and the fourth is the point:

        confirmed      the bytes match a signature this extension may have
        contradicted   they match something ELSE — the name is wrong, or the
                       file is not what it claims
        blocked        refused content, whatever it is called
        unverifiable   nothing reliable to check against

      UNVERIFIABLE IS NOT A PASS. A file the system cannot vouch for is
      reported as such rather than counted as good. A tick that cannot fail
      tells a reviewer nothing, and telling them nothing while looking like
      approval is worse than saying so. The count is reported ALWAYS, including
      when something failed — dropping it on failure would hide how much of an
      upload was never actually checked.

      BLOCKED IS SEPARATE FROM WRONG, as two checks: a .png that is really a
      JPEG is a mislabelled asset, a .png that is really a Windows executable is
      not an asset at all. Burying both in one list would hide the one that
      matters. Executable signatures are checked BEFORE the extension, so a
      rename cannot get past it.

      WHAT IS NOT BLOCKED, deliberately: anything identified by "#!" or a text
      prefix. Shell and Python scripts share those markers with legitimate files
      here — a Nuke script and a Maya script are text too — so a rule built on
      them would fail real assets. Running uploaded code is prevented by nothing
      ever executing a stored file (item 35), not by this list.

      SIGNS 61 OF 131 EXTENSIONS. The rest are text formats with no reliable
      opening bytes — .obj, .ma, .nk, .dae, .gltf — and they report as
      unverifiable. A format is listed ONLY if a file of that format ALWAYS
      starts with those bytes; anything less would be a check that passes by
      accident. Structural checks (a .usda must open with #usda, an .obj has
      v/f line forms) would cover some of the gap and are a separate job.

      LIVES BESIDE assetTypeOf() in shared/assetRules.mjs. The type map and the
      signature table describe the same vocabulary, and kept apart they would
      drift — check:naming now asserts that no signature exists for an
      extension the type map has never heard of.

      VERIFIED end to end: a real PNG confirmed; a JPEG named .png contradicted
      as "looks like JPEG, not PNG"; a Windows executable named .png BLOCKED,
      with the run failed; a Nuke script reported as one of the files that could
      not be verified.


35) NOTHING UPLOADED EVER RUNS. A standing rule, not a task.

    ONLY THE FUNCTIONS WE PROVIDE MAY RUN IN A WORKSPACE. A developer uploads
    files; those files are DATA. They are never executed, never evaluated, never
    interpreted, and never served in a way that lets a browser run them. This is
    the rule the whole workspace rests on, and it is worth writing down because
    the ways to break it are all convenient.

    VERIFIED 2026-10-02, because a rule nobody has checked is a hope:

      * THE UPLOAD STORE IS NOT SERVED STATICALLY. express.static is mounted on
        frontend/dist and frontend/public only. The content directory
        (backend/uploads/files) has no route that maps onto it — a request
        cannot name a path inside it.

      * NOTHING IN THE BACKEND EXECUTES ANYTHING. No child_process, no exec, no
        eval, no new Function, no vm. Verified by searching the whole backend.
        The technical check READS files to hash and size them; that is the only
        thing anything does with a stored file.

      * CONTENT IS SERVED AS application/octet-stream WITH
        Content-Disposition: attachment AND X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff,
        and NEVER with the stored mime_type. The mime_type is there for display
        and is never a response header. Serving a developer-supplied .svg or
        .html inline from this origin would be stored XSS against whoever
        opened it, which is a worse version of the same mistake.

      * The Content-Disposition filename is built from a developer-supplied
        name, so quotes and backslashes are stripped and non-ASCII is escaped.
        A filename is not allowed to break out of its own header.

    WHAT THAT MEANS FOR ANYTHING BUILT LATER, and these are the ones that will
    be tempting:

      * NO PREVIEW THAT INTERPRETS. An image shown inline is a decision to
        re-examine, not a detail — and anything that renders SVG, HTML or a
        model in a way that can run script has broken this rule outright.

      * NO SERVER-SIDE CONVERSION. A thumbnailer, a video transcoder, an
        archive extractor: each one opens a file we did not write with a parser
        we did not write. That is the definition of the risk this rule exists to
        prevent, and it deserves its own decision rather than being added as a
        convenience.

      * NO ARCHIVE EXTRACTION without solving zip-slip first — an entry named
        ../../ is how an extractor writes outside the directory it was given.

      * NO CONFIG OR SCRIPT A DEVELOPER UPLOADS EVER RUNS. Not an .sh, not a
        .json a tool would read as configuration, not a build file. If we ever
        run a developer's build, that is a decision with a sandbox attached, not
        a feature.


36) ONE DEFINITION OF WHERE THE BYTES LIVE — 2026-10-05. Six copies, and they
    agreed only by luck.

    The string '../uploads/files' joined onto __dirname appeared in SIX places:
    backend/lib/hostStorage.js, backend/jobs/daily.js, the upload and download
    routes in backend/routes/developerWorkspace.js, both reset scripts, and
    scripts/check-store.js. All six resolved to backend/uploads/files, because
    every one of those files sits exactly two levels under backend/.

    THEY WERE RIGHT BY COINCIDENCE OF DEPTH, and the failure mode is already in
    this file. resetAssetData.js still carries the comment: it used
    '../../uploads/files', which resolves to <repo>/uploads/files — a directory
    that does not exist. So the script deleted every stored_files row, found no
    files to delete, reported "nothing to delete", and left the physical blobs
    on disk with nothing referencing them. A relative path that is right by luck
    is wrong the moment the file containing it moves. That is item 30's
    territory, and this is the same bug waiting to happen five more times.

    NOW: backend/lib/storagePaths.js owns UPLOADS_DIR, BLOB_DIR and TMP_DIR,
    resolved once from that file's own location. An absolute path cannot be got
    wrong by moving a caller. It also documents the two directories and why they
    are separate — BLOB_DIR is the permanent flat store (one file per SHA, named
    for the SHA), TMP_DIR is scratch for uploads in flight, referenced by no
    table and safe to empty.

    THE SAME SWEEP TURNED UP TWO MORE COPIES OF THE SAME DISEASE, both fixed:

      * SHA256_HEX was written out twice — in storeIntegrity.js and in the
        download route. It guards a path built from a database column, and it is
        the only thing standing between a corrupt storage_key and
        path.join(storeDir, '../../etc/passwd'). Two copies of a security check
        is one copy too many, and the one that gets forgotten is the one that
        matters.

      * The blob path was BUILT in three places and VALIDATED in only two.
        daily.js — the collector, the only thing in the system that unlinks
        content — joined the path without checking. It was covered by policy
        (the collector refuses to run unless the store passes its integrity
        check) but not by code.

    So the check and the join are now ONE CALL: storagePaths.blobPath(key)
    returns the absolute path, or NULL if the key is not a plain SHA-256. A
    caller cannot build a blob path without the key having been validated,
    because there is no other way to build one.

    VERIFIED, not assumed:

      * Every path re-resolved and compared against what the six old copies each
        produced — identical, both directories, and both exist on disk.

      * blobPath() returns null for 'AAAA…' (uppercase), a 63-character key,
        '', null, undefined and '../../etc/passwd'.

      * A REAL UPLOAD of two files through the changed route: both blobs landed
        in BLOB_DIR named for their SHA, storage_key === sha256 for both, the
        deep store check re-hashed them and passed, /tmp was left empty, and the
        download route served bytes hashing to exactly the blob's own name.

      * THE GUARD, END TO END: storage_key was temporarily corrupted to
        '../../../../etc/passwd' against a live row, and the download route
        answered 500 "Stored file record is malformed" without serving anything.
        The key was restored and the test data removed afterwards.

      * check-store.js --quick with STORE_DIR pointed at a directory containing
        a stray file reported the orphan THERE rather than checking the live
        store — the override is the reason storeIntegrity keeps taking storeDir
        as a parameter instead of using BLOB_DIR, and it very nearly got broken.

    A note for whoever touches this next: daily.js is a SCRIPT, not a module.
    Requiring it RUNS it. It is safe without --collect, but that is a property
    of its arguments, not of requiring it.


37) ADMIN-EDITABLE SETTINGS — BUILT 2026-10-05. The mechanism item 32 asked for,
    and the import trap it exposed.

    WHAT IT REPLACES. The invites panel printed "Default Expiration: 28 Days" as
    a hardcoded string while the server issued invitations from
    INVITE_VALID_DAYS = 28 in backend/lib/invites.js. Two copies of one number,
    agreeing by luck, and no way to change either from the page. The wastebin
    retention (14 days) had the same shape and was already recorded as settled
    to be admin-configurable.

    THE FOUR VALUES: invite_valid_days (28), retention_days (14),
    usage_warning_percent (85), usage_critical_percent (95).

    --- THE THING THAT DECIDES THE DESIGN ---

    Item 32 named the failure: "the worker must READ it rather than cache it at
    boot, or an admin change does not take effect until the next restart —
    which is exactly the kind of silent no-op that makes people distrust a
    settings page."

    THAT TRAP WAS ALREADY ARMED IN SIX PLACES, and this is the part worth
    remembering. Every consumer imported the number by destructuring:

        const { INVITE_VALID_DAYS } = require('../lib/invites');

    CommonJS, so that COPIES THE VALUE once, at require time. Four of those
    reads sat inside request handlers — code that runs per request, reading a
    number fixed when the server started. Converting the constant to a getter
    and leaving the import alone would have produced a settings page that saves
    successfully and changes nothing, which is precisely the failure the whole
    exercise was to avoid.

    SO THE IMPORT STYLE WAS THE FIX, NOT THE GETTER. Six files: inviteDevs.js
    (five sites), wastebin.js's expiredVersions() SQL bind,
    developerWorkspace.js's bin response, retention.js (already per-call, the
    clean one), daily.js, and the two libs that band the storage figures.

    --- WHAT IT IS ---

    * app_settings — key TEXT PRIMARY KEY, value, updated_at, updated_by. No
      surrogate id, deliberately: the key is the identity. A missing row means
      "not overridden" and the code default applies, so a fresh database needs
      no seeding.

    * backend/lib/settings.js — the registry. One entry per key with its type,
      default, bounds, label, unit and a note for the page. Every read hits the
      database; nothing is cached. Adding a setting is a line here and nothing
      else — the panel renders whatever the registry sends, so there is no
      second list of keys to go stale.

    * THE WRITE AND THE AUDIT ARE ONE TRANSACTION (settings.update /
      settings.reset, with actor and { from, to }), following the same
      reasoning as every other audited action.

    * GET/POST /api/admin/settings, POST /api/admin/settings/:key, and
      .../:key/reset — under admin.js, so requireAdmin applies and both admin
      and superuser can use them.

    * A Settings tab on BOTH dashboards, and the invites panel's window now
      filled from the endpoint.

    --- TWO DECISIONS WORTH KEEPING ---

    THE BANDING STAYS PURE. shared/usageStatus.mjs now takes the levels as
    arguments rather than reading them, because shared/ is the directory for
    code both halves of the app use and the frontend may import it — so nothing
    in there may touch the database. Callers read the setting and pass it in.

    THE RELATION IS CHECKED, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. Two independent keys with a
    relationship between them is how a system ends up with warning 95 and
    critical 85 — a band where everything is critical and nothing is ever
    warned about in time. Equal is refused as well as inverted, and the refusal
    names the field the caller actually changed: "The warning level must stay
    below the critical level, which is 80" reads correctly beside a field
    labelled Warning, where one shared sentence would not.

    --- DELIBERATELY NOT CHANGED ---

    backend/db.js hardcodes '+28 days' as the expires_at column DEFAULT in five
    places. It is DEAD IN PRODUCTION — the invite route always supplies
    expires_at explicitly — and changing a column default needs a table
    rebuild, which this schema's migration style does not do (ADD COLUMN only).
    Left in place. If it is ever wrong, it is wrong only for a hand-written
    INSERT that omits the column.

    --- VERIFIED ---

    On ONE running server with NO restart: setting invite_valid_days to 3 made
    the next invitation's stored expires_at exactly 3 days out (checked in the
    database, not just the response); setting retention_days to 2 changed what
    the developer's bin reported; a version deleted 5 days ago was NOT due for
    purge under a 14-day window, WAS due under 3, and was not due again under
    7 — so the setting reaches the SQL; setting the warning level to 12 flipped
    a 21.1%-used disk from 'ok' to 'warning' on the storage endpoint.

    Refusals checked over HTTP: 0, 999, "abc", 2.5, an unknown key, a missing
    body, and the relation in both directions. The audit entries were read back
    through the admin's own audit endpoint.

    Test data removed afterwards — settings reset to their defaults by the same
    route the panel's Reset uses, the test invitation deleted, and the audit
    rows my testing wrote deleted too, because they named the admin as the
    actor for changes the admin never made.


38) STORAGE PANEL — REVIEWED AND REWORKED 2026-05-31. Three things, one of them
    a correction to how the whole feature was described.

    THERE WERE FOUR. The fourth — storage by game version — MOVED TO ITEM 53 on
    2026-10-08, because it is a question about the GAME that happens to be
    displayed on this tab, and this item is the panel's record. The PROVISION it
    left on the tab is still described in item 53.

    --- 1. NOBODY IS "CHARGED" FOR ANYTHING ---

    The panel said "Charged" in a column heading and "Charged to developers" on
    a tile. There is no billing in this system and there is not going to be.
    The word invited exactly that misreading, on the screen an administrator
    reads to understand where disk is going.

    Now "Storage used", and "In developer workspaces". The API field was
    `charged` too, and is now `workspaces` — the label went with the figure so
    the distinction survives being read quickly. The comments in
    workspaceUsage.js that argued about "what each developer is CHARGED" were
    written in that vocabulary; they now say "holds" and "counted against".

    The accounting itself did not change. It was right; it was described badly.

    --- 2. THE ALLOWANCE IS A SETTING ---

    WORKSPACE_LIMIT_BYTES was an environment variable on the box, which is the
    same problem the invite window had: not changeable from the page, needs a
    restart. It is now workspace_limit_gb in the settings registry (item 37),
    default 5, range 1–10000.

    STATED IN GB, STORED AS GB, CONVERTED IN ONE PLACE. The setting holds
    gigabytes because that is what an administrator types and reads; the
    multiplication by 1024^3 happens once, in workspaceUsage.js, so "GB" cannot
    come to mean 10^9 in one file and 1024^3 in another. The environment
    variable was removed rather than kept alongside — two places to set one
    limit is how the number in the panel and the number refusing an upload end
    up different.

    An integer GB floor of 1 is deliberate: a sub-gigabyte allowance is not a
    real allowance, and fractional GB would mean either a float in the settings
    table or a second unit.

    --- 3. THE DEVELOPER TABLE SORTS ---

    Sortable in the browser rather than by re-querying: the list is bounded by
    the number of developer accounts, so a sort is a re-render, not a round
    trip. IF THAT EVER STOPS BEING TRUE this is the thing to move to the server,
    and the comment in front of it says so.

    SORTED NUMERICALLY, NOT AS TEXT. Sorting the formatted column would put
    "9.5 MB" above "10 GB" and "1.2 GB" below "900 KB" — which looks like a bug
    and is one. Verified with fixture sizes chosen to expose exactly that (1020
    B, 1.46 KB, 1.43 MB): the order came out numeric, the direction flipped on
    the second click, and aria-sort followed.

    AND THE SORT THE TABLE ARRIVES IN IS NOW SHOWN. The server sends rows
    largest-first, so the table is already sorted by storage before anyone
    touches it — with no marker on that heading, the first click appeared to
    sort ascending out of nowhere. The heading now carries the arrow from the
    start, so a click visibly reverses what is already in effect.

    --- AND THE DISK FIGURE, WHICH STAYS ---

    Not one of the three above, and recorded so it is not lost with them: the
    total disk figure on this tab is unchanged and still wanted. It is this
    machine, the studio server.


39) INVITATIONS FOR PEOPLE WHO ALREADY HAVE ACCOUNTS — 2026-10-05, found by
    looking at the invites panel.

    The admin invites list carried a pending invitation for val@thameslighter.net
    while that address already had a developer account. Nothing flagged it, and
    nothing prevented it.

    --- HOW IT HAPPENED ---

    Two routes to the same state, and only one of them is a mistake by a person.

    1. THE ACCOUNT WAS CREATED BY HAND. There is no CLI in this repo that makes
       users (searched: nothing under scripts/ or reset/ inserts into users), so
       it was an ad-hoc command. The invitation was issued on 10 Aug and the
       account created on 11 Aug, and nothing ever connected them. An invitation
       records the address, the role and the dates, and NOTHING about who issued
       it — so "where did this come from?" had no answer in the data either.

    2. THE REDEMPTION WAS NOT ATOMIC, and this one is a bug in the code
       regardless of the CLI. /api/auth/register-invited created the account and
       marked the invitation used as TWO separate statements. A failure between
       them — or the process dying — leaves a live account beside an invitation
       that still looks unredeemed. That is the exact shape of the row found.

    --- AND NOTHING WAS AUDITED ---

    audit_logs recorded deactivations, retags, purges, feedback resolutions,
    restores, settings changes and daily-job runs. It did NOT record a user
    account being created, or an invitation being issued or redeemed. So the one
    question the vigilance actually depends on — "how did this login come to
    exist?" — had no answer in the trail, only in a schema that did not record
    it either.

    --- WHAT WAS BUILT ---

    * THE RULE, ONCE, in backend/lib/invites.js: inviteable(db, email) and
      accountFor(db, email), case-insensitively, plus inviteRefusal() which
      names the account and says what to do instead. Called by invitation
      creation AND by both reissue paths — a reissue prolongs access for an
      address just as an issue does, so it needs the same answer. Reissue also
      re-checks, because an account can appear AFTER the invitation was created.

    * EMAILS ARE NORMALISED to lowercase on the way in. The UNIQUE constraint on
      that column is case-sensitive, so "Val@x.com" and "val@x.com" were two
      invitations for one person, and an account under one did not block an
      invitation for the other. That hole produces exactly the duplicate the
      check exists to prevent.

    * REDEMPTION IS ONE TRANSACTION, with the audit record inside it. The
      account and the invitation that authorised it appear together or not at
      all. Verified: a redemption that fails on a taken username leaves the
      invitation PENDING — the link is not burned — and records nothing, because
      nothing happened.

    * created_by ON invitations, with a guarded ADD COLUMN migration and the
      column added to the canonical CREATE TABLE. Existing rows are NULL, and
      that is the truth rather than a gap: those invitations were not recorded
      anywhere, and inventing an issuer would be worse than admitting it is
      unknown.

    * AUDITED: invitation.create (actor, role, id, window), and user.create on
      redemption (email, role, via, invitation_id, invited_by). The actor on a
      redemption is NULL — nobody with a session acted, the person registering
      had no account yet — so the link back to the administrator is invited_by
      in the details.

    * THE LIST NOW JOINS ACCOUNTS, and the panel flags a row whose address
      already has a login: amber row, warning icon, "Already has an account: X".
      REISSUE IS NOT OFFERED on those rows, because the server refuses it and a
      button whose only outcome is an error is worse than no button. Revoke is
      still offered, so a stale row can be cleared.

    --- VERIFIED ---

    Inviting val@thameslighter.net, VAL@Thameslighter.NET and the same with
    surrounding whitespace: all three 409 with the account named. A fresh
    address: 200, stored lowercased, created_by set, audited. Redemption:
    account and status 'used' and the audit row, all present. Failure: rolled
    back entirely.

    --- STILL OPEN, AND THE REAL GAP FOR "SPURIOUS LOGINS" ---

    THE CHECK ABOVE CANNOT SEE A CLI-CREATED ACCOUNT, and neither can any audit
    trail, because the insert never goes through the application. Prevention
    stops invitations being issued wrongly; it does not detect an account that
    appeared without one.

    What would: an "accounts not accounted for" check — every account whose
    existence no audit entry explains. The seed accounts (admin/superuser,
    created by db.js at first run) would need a bootstrap entry of their own so
    they read as accounted for, and the accounts that predate this work
    (including val) have no trail at all and would show as unaccounted until
    somebody records why. That is a small piece of work and a decision about
    what "accounted for" means, which is why it is not done here.

    The stale row for val@thameslighter.net is LEFT IN PLACE and now flagged.
    Revoking it is one click in the panel, and it is the user's record to clear
    rather than mine.


40) ACCOUNTS NOT ACCOUNTED FOR — BUILT 2026-10-05. The safeguard item 39 said was
    missing.

    Item 39 stopped invitations being issued for people who already have a login,
    and made redemption atomic and audited. None of that could SEE an account
    created by a CLI, because the insert never goes through the application. This
    is the check for that.

    --- THE MECHANISM IS A COLUMN WITH NO DEFAULT ---

    users.created_via, holding one of 'bootstrap' (made by db.js when the
    database was empty), 'invitation' (redeemed an invitation), 'google' (first
    sign-in through Google), or 'manual' (made by hand, outside the application).

    NO DEFAULT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK. Every path in the application sets
    it explicitly. A row inserted by something that does not know about the
    column — an ad-hoc command, a script written in a hurry — lands as NULL.
    NULL therefore means "nothing in this application created this account",
    which is exactly the question worth asking about a login nobody expected.

    A DEFAULT WOULD DEFEAT IT COMPLETELY: every insert would look explained.

    --- THERE ARE EXACTLY THREE WAYS AN ACCOUNT IS MADE ---

    Checked, not assumed: INSERT INTO users appears in db.js (the bootstrap seed)
    and in two prepared statements in server.js. Nothing else in the tree creates
    users — no CLI under scripts/, no impersonation route. All three now record
    their route, and the Google path was not audited at all before this; it is
    now, in the same transaction as the insert, with the same null actor a
    redeemed invitation gets (nobody signed in acted — a person arrived through
    Google and had an account made for them).

    --- THE BACKFILL IS A JUDGEMENT, RECORDED ---

    The three accounts that existed when the column was added (admin, superuser,
    val) are marked 'manual'. That is a deduction rather than a guess: db.js's own
    seed inserts 'admin' with the address admin@lowexpectations.com and the live
    row has admin@lowexpectations.zone, so the seed did not make it, and the
    application has no other way to make an account. Something outside the
    application made all three, which is what 'manual' means.

    The alternative was to leave them NULL and let the check report them for
    ever. That is how a check that always has something in it becomes a check
    nobody reads — and the point of this one is to be believed on the day it
    speaks. Marked, with the reasoning in the migration comment.

    --- WHERE IT SHOWS ---

    The admin Accounts tab, on both dashboards. A "Created" column on every row
    saying how that account came to exist, and a warning above the table that
    appears ONLY when something is unaccounted for — naming the accounts, when
    they were added, and what to do about it. Silent when everything is
    explicable, because a warning that is always on screen is a warning nobody
    reads.

    An unaccounted row is badged in the status warning colour with an icon,
    never left as a blank cell: a blank reads as a rendering fault rather than as
    a finding.

    --- WHAT IT CANNOT DO ---

    A determined hand can write a created_via alongside the row and the check
    will believe it. It cannot be otherwise without a signature. What it catches
    is the ordinary case, which is an ad-hoc command that does not know the
    column exists — and that is precisely how the account this was written for
    got here.

    Possible follow-ons, not built and not obviously wanted yet: warning an
    administrator when an unaccounted account SIGNS IN (there is no notification
    channel — SMTP exists but is optional and used for invitations), and putting
    this in the manual.

    --- VERIFIED ---

    Baseline: no accounts unaccounted, banner hidden. Inserted an account the way
    a CLI does — no created_via — and the endpoint returned it in `unaccounted`
    and the panel badged the row and showed the banner. Redeemed a real
    invitation and confirmed the account records 'invitation' and the count stays
    zero. Removed the probes and confirmed the banner container is back to
    d-none in the DOM, not merely assumed to be.

    FOUND WHILE BUILDING IT, AND WORTH RECORDING: the migration was first written
    at the TOP of initDb(), before the users table is created — so on a FRESH
    database, PRAGMA table_info(users) returned empty, ALTER TABLE ran against a
    table that did not exist, and db.js threw at startup. Every existing database
    would have been fine and every new one broken. Caught by check:schema, which
    builds a fresh scratch database by design. The migration now sits immediately
    after the CREATE TABLE.


41) URGENT NOTIFICATIONS — the test button is built. The rest is a discussion.

    The admin page can show a warning, but only to somebody looking at the admin
    page. A disk at 95% and climbing needs to reach a person who is not at their
    desk. This records the design, the decisions taken so far, and the two open
    questions. NOT to be confused with the discussion board (item 27), which is
    decided but not built — so this discussion is here rather than on it.

    --- DECIDED ---

    * WHO: EVERY ACTIVE ADMIN AND SUPERUSER. All of them, not one — an alert
      that reaches one person reaches nobody when that person is on holiday, and
      the point of an urgent message is that it arrives. Deactivated accounts
      are skipped: mailing an address nobody is watching is the wrong thing to
      do with the message that has to arrive.

    * EMAIL FIRST, AND CC RATHER THAN BCC, so everyone on the message can see
      who else is on it and talk to each other. BCC would hide exactly the thing
      that makes it collaborative, and would let two people investigate the same
      disk without ever finding out. (In the mailer this is `cc`, an array; the
      first recipient goes in To, the rest in Cc, because a message with an
      empty To is treated as suspicious by some spam filters.)

    * THREE LEVELS, and what separates them is not how bad something is but
      WHETHER IT INTERRUPTS SOMEBODY. info and warning belong on the admin page;
      urgent leaves the building. Defined in backend/lib/notify.js.

    * WHAT COUNTS AS URGENT, given so far: disk over the critical threshold AND
      RISING; the production server being down; a security breach. "And rising"
      is the important part — a disk sitting at 95% is stable, a disk at 95% and
      climbing will fill. So an alert fires ON ENTRY into the condition, carries
      the trend, and then stays quiet while it persists rather than messaging on
      every check. That is the difference between an alert somebody acts on and
      one they mute. NOT YET BUILT: whether to also send a daily reminder while
      something stays critical — my inclination is yes, once a day, but it is a
      decision rather than an obvious one.

    * "THERE WILL BE OTHER SITUATIONS MARKED URGENT" is why the design is a
      registry of conditions with a severity each, the same shape as the
      settings registry, rather than a fixed list. Adding one should be a line.
      Not built yet.

    --- BUILT: THE TEST BUTTON ---

    Settings tab, both dashboards. It shows who would be told and whether
    anything can be sent BEFORE the button is pressed, so "email is not
    configured" is read while calmly setting things up rather than discovered
    during the incident it was supposed to report.

    The message it sends is SHAPED LIKE A REAL ALERT — same subject prefix, same
    level, same recipient list — because a test that sends something prettier
    than the real thing proves less than it appears to. backend/lib/notify.js
    holds the recipient rule, the levels and the message; mailer.js gained an
    optional `cc`.

    IT REPORTS WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED, and all three outcomes were verified
    against a throwaway SMTP sink rather than assumed:

      * email configured, sent  -> sent:true. The sink confirmed the envelope
        carried BOTH recipients and the headers read To: superuser, Cc: admin —
        so the collaboration requirement is verified at the SMTP level, not just
        in the API response.
      * email not configured    -> sent:false, reason not_configured, with the
        sentence to put on screen. This is the state on THIS machine: SMTP_HOST
        is empty, so invitations silently fall back to showing the link and
        alerts would go nowhere.
      * the mail server refused -> sent:false with the REAL reason,
        "connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:2525", not a vague failure.

    A 200 with sent:false is the ordinary "it could not be sent" answer and the
    panel treats it as information rather than as an error — reporting it as an
    error would report the wrong thing.

    --- OPEN 1: SMS ---

    Wanted, and the user's words were "either phone txt or whatsapp". NEITHER IS
    POSSIBLE WITHOUT A THIRD-PARTY SERVICE. Nothing in this codebase talks to
    any gateway, so this is a new integration rather than a setting: an account
    with Twilio, Vonage, MessageBird or a WhatsApp Business provider;
    credentials; a sending number; and a cost per message.

    Worth deciding before building: SMS to one number or several, whether
    WhatsApp is really wanted (it is a different API from SMS and, for a
    Business account, a different approval process), and whether every urgent
    message should go to a phone or only the ones nobody is awake for.

    --- OPEN 2: THE SERVER CANNOT REPORT ITS OWN DEATH ---

    "The production server has gone down" is the one condition on the list that
    the portal can never send, because the thing that would send the message is
    the thing that is dead. It needs something OUTSIDE the portal watching it: a
    health endpoint, and a monitor that notices when it stops answering. Either
    a third-party watcher (healthchecks.io and similar) or a job on a different
    machine. Cron runs the daily job here, but cron does not report its own
    failures.

    This is a real piece of work and it is not the same piece as the rest. It is
    recorded here so it is not assumed to be covered by the alerting above,
    which it is not.

    --- ALSO OPEN ---

    "Security breach" is not yet a thing that can fire. It has to become
    concrete conditions first. One exists — an account appearing that nothing in
    the portal created, item 40 — and others would be repeated failed sign-ins,
    or a new admin or superuser appearing. Each is buildable; the phrase alone
    is not a trigger.


42) BEFORE THIS GOES LIVE — IMPORTANT. Security items that must not be forgotten
    between now and deployment.

    --- PASSWORDS ---

    THE ADMIN AND SUPERUSER PASSWORDS MUST BOTH BE CHANGED BEFORE GO-LIVE.

    Checked on 2026-10-05: the live `admin` account still opens with
    the seed password, the value in db.js. The `superuser` account does NOT
    match that string, so it was set to something else by hand — which means its
    password is written down nowhere and must be reset deliberately rather than
    assumed.

    The rule going forward, stated once:

        A PASSWORD IS NEVER ECHOED — NOT TO THE CONSOLE, NOT INTO A LOG, NOT IN
        AN API RESPONSE, NOT IN AN ERROR MESSAGE. It exists as a bcrypt hash in
        the database and as the plain text the person typed, and nowhere else.

    db.js breaks this twice today: it contains the literal the seed password
    and it prints it to the console at first run ("🔑 Initial superuser created:
    username="admin", password=<the seed password>"). Both go.

    --- AND THE SEED GOES WITH THEM ---

    The right fix is not to hide the password, it is to stop db.js creating a
    superuser at all. A database that seeds itself with a known credential on
    the most privileged account is the weakest thing in the system, and it
    exists only so that a fresh install has a way in.

    scripts/create-superuser.js replaces it (item 43). A fresh database then has
    no accounts and no way in except by somebody with shell access to the host —
    which is exactly the intended property, and it is better than a default
    password nobody remembers to change.

    NOT DONE NOW, deliberately: the live admin account is left alone until
    go-live, per the decision on 2026-10-05. Do not forget it.


43) PRIVILEGED ACCOUNTS ARE CREATED OUT OF BAND — decided 2026-10-05.

    ADMIN AND SUPERUSER CANNOT BE CREATED THROUGH THE WEB INTERFACE AT ALL. They
    are created only by somebody with superuser rights on the host machine,
    running a script from a scripts/ directory that the web application cannot
    reach. The web app never has a path to grant those roles.

    WHY: it limits PERSISTENCE, not impact. A stolen superuser session can still
    change settings, deactivate people and read everything. What it cannot do is
    plant a lasting privileged account — which is the difference between an
    incident you clean up and one you keep finding again.

    WHERE THE SYSTEM STANDS TODAY, checked rather than assumed:

      * superuser — ALREADY correct, by accident. The strictly-below rule in
        backend/lib/roles.js means nobody can assign it, including a superuser.
      * admin — THE GAP. assignableRolesFor('superuser') returns
        ['user','developer','admin'], so a superuser can invite an admin through
        the panel today.
      * No route anywhere changes an existing account's role, so there is no
        promotion path to close. That helps.

    BUILT 2026-10-05. All three:

      * THE WEB REFUSES ADMIN AND ABOVE, whoever is asking. roles.js now has
        WEB_ASSIGNABLE_ROLES = ['user','developer'], and canAssignRole checks it
        BEFORE rank — so a superuser asking for admin is refused like anybody
        else. A REFUSAL IN THE APP, not a hidden dropdown: verified by posting
        role=admin and role=superuser straight at the API as a superuser, both
        403, while role=developer still succeeds.

        THE TWO REFUSALS ARE SAID DIFFERENTLY, and that matters. "The web cannot
        grant that role at all" and "you are not senior enough" are separate
        facts — a superuser told "you are not senior enough to invite an admin"
        would reasonably look for somebody more senior, and there is nobody,
        which is the whole point. webAssignable() exists to tell them apart.

      * scripts/create-superuser.js — see item 45.

      * THE ACCOUNTS CHECK IS ROLE-AWARE, and asks TWO questions now rather than
        one, because they fail differently:

          1. DOES ANYTHING ACCOUNT FOR THIS ACCOUNT AT ALL? A NULL created_via
             means nothing in this application made it.
          2. IS ITS ORIGIN ALLOWED FOR ITS ROLE? A privileged account created
             through a web path is a violation even though its origin is
             recorded — which question 1 cannot see, because the row looks
             perfectly explicable.

        Only privileged accounts have a restricted origin: a developer made by
        invitation is entirely normal, and flagging that would bury the two that
        matter. The panel names each account and gives the matching explanation,
        because "needs explaining" means different things in the two cases.

        The three accounts that exist are NOT flagged: their created_via is
        'manual', which is a host-machine origin, and that is a legitimate way
        for an admin to have come about.

        Verified by planting both kinds — an admin with created_via='invitation'
        (flagged as privileged-from-web) and a developer with no origin at all
        (flagged as unrecorded) — seeing both described correctly, and removing
        them.

    ONE INVARIANT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE, but which item 45 records: the rank rule
    still means nobody can invite a peer, so the web can never make a superuser
    even if the role list were wrong. The two defences are independent.

    ONE INVARIANT THIS CHANGES, worth writing down: today NOBODY can create a
    superuser, so there is exactly one for ever and no backup. A script that can
    create one allows a second — better for recovery, and a deliberate change to
    a property that was previously guaranteed by the role rule.

    ONE COST TO ACCEPT DELIBERATELY: with a single machine, no admin can be
    created while whoever needs one is away from the studio computer. That is
    the point of the rule; it should be a decision rather than a surprise on the
    day somebody is travelling.

    ALSO OPEN — WHAT "AT THE MACHINE" MEANS. Two different things could enforce
    it and they are not equivalent:

      (a) SHELL ACCESS TO THE HOST, via the scripts directory. This is the real
          control: an attacker holding only a web session cannot reach it.
      (b) THE REQUEST COMING FROM THE MACHINE, via a loopback check in the app.
          Worth having as a second layer, but it is not the control on its own —
          and note the app currently binds to every interface (item 44), so
          there is nothing distinguishing a local request today.

    Recommended: (a) as the control, (b) as belt and braces.


44) WHAT THE SERVER LISTENS ON — decided in principle 2026-10-05, to implement
    with deployment.

    TODAY: app.listen(PORT, callback) with no host argument, so Express binds
    EVERY INTERFACE — ss shows *:5000. The .env value HOST=localhost does NOT
    control this; it is only used to build the OAuth callback URL, so it looks
    like a restriction that is not one. Meanwhile the Vite dev server binds
    loopback only ([::1]:5173), so the API is the exposed surface and the
    frontend is not.

    THE SHAPE IT SHOULD HAVE:

      * Express binds 127.0.0.1:5000 — LOOPBACK ONLY, not reachable from the
        network at all.
      * A reverse proxy (nginx, Caddy) faces the world on 443, with 80
        redirecting to it, terminating TLS and forwarding to loopback.
      * Vite's dev server never runs in production. It is a development tool.
      * The firewall denies inbound 5000 from anywhere but the machine itself.

    WHY A PROXY RATHER THAN BINDING THE APP TO THE LAN: the application process
    should not be the thing facing the network. The proxy is where TLS lives,
    where rate limiting lives, and — relevant to item 43 — where "admin routes
    only from the studio" can be enforced on requests that never reach Node.
    The app also cannot serve https on its own here.

    WHAT IS NOT SERVED, and must stay that way: the database file and the
    uploads directory. Only frontend/dist and frontend/public are mounted as
    static, which is correct — worth knowing it is deliberate.

    OPEN QUESTION: does anything OTHER than this machine reach the portal today?
    If a tester on a laptop does, binding to loopback is not enough on its own
    and the proxy has to exist first. If nothing does — only the Vite setup on
    this box, whose proxy targets localhost:5000 — then the loopback change is
    safe to make at any time and breaks nothing.

    TIMING: this is deployment configuration rather than code, so it can wait
    until just before deployment EXCEPT for the item below, which is worth doing
    early because it is the recovery path rather than a hardening measure.


45) THE SEED IS GONE — done 2026-10-05. scripts/create-superuser.js replaces it.

    db.js no longer creates any account. It used to seed a superuser called
    "admin" with the password the seed password whenever the users table was
    empty, and print that password to the console.

    TWO THINGS WERE WRONG AND ONLY ONE WAS THE WEAK PASSWORD. The literal was in
    the repository, on the most privileged account in the system — and it was
    PRINTED, where anybody at the terminal or reading a captured log could read
    it. The deeper problem is that it existed at all: a database that hands
    itself a known credential the moment it is created is the weakest thing in
    the system, and it was only there so a fresh install had a way in.

    A FRESH DATABASE NOW HAS NO ACCOUNTS AND NO WAY IN, and that is the intended
    property. Verified by building a scratch database the way check:schema does
    and counting the users: zero, and no password printed.

    --- WHAT REPLACES IT ---

    scripts/create-superuser.js. Runs on the studio machine, needs a shell, and
    is not reachable from the web — scripts/ is not served; only frontend/dist
    and frontend/public are mounted as static.

      node scripts/create-superuser.js                  create a superuser
      node scripts/create-superuser.js --role admin     create an admin
      node scripts/create-superuser.js --reset <name>   set a new password
      node scripts/create-superuser.js --list           who is on the system

    Only admin and superuser can be created here. Developers and testers come
    through invitations, which is what the invitation system is for — so the
    privileged path is the one that needs a shell.

    --- THE PASSWORD IS NEVER ECHOED, AND THAT DECIDED THE DESIGN ---

    Not to the console, not into a log, not into an audit record, not into a
    shell history file. It exists as a bcrypt hash in the database and as the
    characters the person typed, and nowhere else.

    WHICH IS WHY IT IS PROMPTED FOR AND NOT PASSED IN. An argument lands in
    `ps` output and in the shell's history; an environment variable is readable
    from /proc by the same user and is inherited by child processes. So there is
    no --password flag, no environment variable, and no "generate one and print
    it" mode — a password printed to a terminal is a password in a scrollback
    buffer and possibly a screenshot. A run without a terminal is REFUSED with
    an explanation rather than compromised, verified by piping input in.

    THE PROMPT IS SWITCHED TO NO-ECHO BEFORE IT IS PRINTED, not after. My test
    harness found this: input arriving before echo is switched off is echoed by
    the terminal itself. Nobody types that fast, but a PASTE arrives all at once
    and does not care how brief the window is.

    Verified through a real pty rather than assumed: the created account holds a
    $2b$ bcrypt hash that matches what was typed and the plain text appears
    nowhere in the terminal output; --reset changes it and rejects the old one;
    a mismatched confirmation changes nothing; and every action is audited with
    action user.create / user.password_reset, actor NULL, and details carrying
    via:'console', the OS user and the hostname — so a console action names the
    human and the machine, which is the most that can honestly be claimed when
    nobody signed in.

    --- AND IT CLOSED A HOLE I WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE OPENED ---

    There is NO PASSWORD-CHANGE FACILITY ANYWHERE IN THE APPLICATION — the only
    auth routes are login, logout and invitation registration. So removing the
    seed without --reset would have left a forgotten password with no recovery
    path, and would have made the go-live requirement in item 42 ("both
    passwords must be changed") impossible to satisfy. --reset is that
    mechanism. Anyone who still wants an in-app password change should say so;
    it is a separate piece of work and is not needed for go-live now.


46) MAKING AN ADMINISTRATOR — reworked 2026-10-06. Invite, do not hand over a
    password.

    Item 43 said privileged accounts come from the machine. That was right, but
    the PROCEDURE was wrong: the script created the account and typed a password
    at the machine, which somebody then had to convey to the other person.
    Awkward, and it made a third party the author of a password they should
    never know.

    THE BETTER PATTERN, and it is the ordinary one — role mapping, or
    pre-authorised provisioning, the shape nearly every SSO integration uses:
    the grant happens out of band, and it MATERIALISES through the normal
    account-creation flow. Here the machine issues an invitation that carries
    the privileged role, and the person redeems it and chooses their own
    password.

    WHY IT IS STILL SAFE, and this is the part that matters: what preserves the
    property is that the web interface cannot issue a privileged invitation at
    all. WEB_ASSIGNABLE_ROLES refuses admin and superuser to every web request,
    so an admin-role invitation can only have come from the console. The grant
    still requires machine access; only the moment it lands has moved.

    --- WHAT WAS BUILT ---

      * --invite --email them@example.com [--role admin|superuser]
        Issues an invitation with the privileged role and sends it. Prints the
        link when mail is not configured, the same degrade the panel does.

      * --promote <username> --role admin|superuser
        For somebody who ALREADY has a login. AN INVITATION CANNOT DO THIS:
        redeeming one creates an account, and invitations are unique per address
        (email TEXT UNIQUE) as are accounts — so a developer cannot be invited
        again. Without this, promoting an existing person meant editing the
        database by hand. Leaves their password alone; takes effect on their
        next request.

      * --role admin, unchanged, for the first account on a new installation
        (nobody exists to invite) and for when the mail path is broken. Now
        documented as the exception rather than the way.

      * invitations.source ('web' | 'console'), added with a guarded migration,
        defaulting to 'web' — which is true of every invitation that existed
        before it. RECORDED RATHER THAN INFERRED from the role: the inference
        would be correct today and silently wrong the day the role rule changes.

      * THE REDEMPTION RECORDS THE TRUE ORIGIN. createUser's created_via is
        bound rather than hardcoded: a console-issued invitation produces an
        account whose created_via is 'console', not 'invitation'. Without that,
        the accounts check would flag every legitimately-granted administrator
        as "created through the website" — the exact alarm it exists to raise,
        fired at the wrong people.

      * The invitations list and panel show "issued on the studio machine", so
        an administrator does not have to wonder who created a privileged row.

    --- VERIFIED END TO END ---

    Issued an admin invitation from the console: the row records
    source='console', role='admin', created_by NULL, and an audit entry with
    via:'console', the OS user and the hostname. Redeemed it through the API the
    way the person does: the account came out as admin with created_via
    'console', the password was the one THEY set, the invitation went to 'used',
    and the accounts check stayed QUIET — which is the point, because a
    console-granted administrator is exactly what it should accept.

    Promoted an existing account: role changed, password untouched, audited with
    from/to. Refusals checked: inviting an address that already has an account
    (which now points at --promote), inviting an address already invited, and
    --promote without a role.

    --- STILL OPEN ---

    DEMOTION. There is no --demote. Removing somebody's privileged role means
    editing the database, which is the gap --promote was built to close on the
    other side. Deactivating the account is the available answer today, and it
    is a different thing.

    AND NOTHING RE-CHECKS. Once granted, the role is a stored value — the script
    is not consulted on later logins, and there is no list to remove somebody
    from. That is simpler than the allow-list variant considered on 2026-10-06
    and was chosen deliberately, but it means revoking a grant is a manual act
    rather than a consequence of editing a file.


47) THE LOGIN PATH — audited 2026-10-06, while discussing whether a banned list is
    needed. It is not; this is. FOUR FINDINGS, one of them an open door.

    --- 1. `000000` IS THE SECOND FACTOR FOR EVERY ACCOUNT ---

    backend/server.js, in /api/auth/login-step2:

        if (user.two_factor_secret) { ...verify TOTP... }
        else if (code === '000000') { verified = true; }

    "Fallback demo code before initial 2FA setup."

    EVERY ACCOUNT IN THE DATABASE HAS is_2fa_enabled = 1 AND NO SECRET:

        admin      admin      2FA enabled: 1 | has a secret: false
        superuser  superuser  2FA enabled: 1 | has a secret: false
        val        developer  2FA enabled: 1 | has a secret: false

    So the `else if` is not a fallback — it is the ONLY way through step 2, and
    the fixed string 000000 is the actual second factor for everybody. The flag
    is set automatically on first login (enable2FA is called when it is 0), so
    the system reports "2FA enabled" while there is nothing behind it.

    WITH NO RATE LIMITING ON LOGIN AND THE ADMIN PASSWORD IN GIT HISTORY, this
    is a complete chain to full administrator access: guessable username,
    password from the repository, fixed code.

    NO AMOUNT OF NETWORK FILTERING TOUCHES THIS. To the application it is a
    successful, valid login. That is the answer to "do we need a banned list" —
    not for this.

    TO FIX, and it needs a DECISION because removing the fallback without a rule
    locks every account out of step 2:

      * Delete the 000000 branch. There is no version of production where it
        belongs, and a "demo" bypass on the login path is a backdoor whatever it
        is called.
      * Decide the real rule. The line directly above, commented out, already
        had it right:
            //const requires2FA = user.is_2fa_enabled === 1
            //                    || ['admin', 'superuser'].includes(user.role);
        REQUIRED for admin and superuser, optional below that. It was replaced
        with `const requires2FA = true;`, which asks everybody for a code that
        nobody actually has.
      * Accounts with no secret then need a way forward: either skip step 2, or
        force enrolment on first sign-in. Enrolment is the better answer for
        privileged accounts and is more work.
      * is_2fa_enabled must MEAN something. Setting it on login because there is
        no secret is how the flag came to be true while being false.

    --- 2. NO RATE LIMITING ON LOGIN ---

    The only rate limiting anywhere is in routes/feedback.js, for anonymous
    reports. /api/auth/login accepts unlimited attempts. bcrypt at cost 12 makes
    each attempt slow, which is a speed bump and not a control.

    Needs: per-source and per-account limits, with backoff. Careful with
    per-account lockout — it is a denial-of-service against the account holder,
    so prefer slowing down over locking out, at least for privileged accounts.

    --- 3. FAILED LOGINS ARE NOT RECORDED ---

    audit.record is called for account creation, settings, invitations,
    deactivations, purges and job runs. Nothing records a failed sign-in. So the
    one question that matters during an incident — "is somebody trying?" — has
    no answer in the data.

    AND IT IS THE MISSING TRIGGER for item 41: "repeated failed sign-ins" was
    named there as a condition that would make "security breach" a real
    notification rather than a phrase. Recording them is what makes it fire.

    --- 4. THE HANDLER TELLS AN ATTACKER WHICH ACCOUNTS ARE REAL ---

    routes/auth.js, /login:

      * A missing user returns 401 "Invalid username or password" — good.
      * A DEACTIVATED user returns 403 "Account has been deactivated" — which
        confirms the account EXISTS and says what state it is in. That is an
        enumeration oracle. Distinguishing "deactivated" from "wrong password"
        is useful to the account holder and useful to an attacker; the holder
        can be told by email instead.
      * The comment says "Prevent Timing Attacks & Verify Account Exists", and
        the code does the opposite: it returns immediately when no user is
        found, so a missing username answers in microseconds while a wrong
        password waits for bcrypt — roughly 100ms. That difference is
        measurable and enumerates usernames. A dummy hash comparison on the
        no-user path is the standard fix.

    --- AND A PREREQUISITE, NOT A FINDING ---

    `trust proxy` IS NOT SET, and nothing reads CF-Connecting-IP. Behind
    Cloudflare, req.ip is a Cloudflare address — see item 48, where that also
    explains why an app-side IP blocklist would be actively wrong.

    The consequence today is smaller but real: the ANONYMOUS feedback limiter in
    routes/feedback.js keys on req.ip, so behind Cloudflare every anonymous
    report would share one bucket — one person could exhaust it, and an attacker
    would be hidden among legitimate reporters.

    Setting it must wait until the origin is only reachable through Cloudflare
    (item 48), because otherwise X-Forwarded-For is client-controlled and the
    "fix" becomes a spoofing hole that is worse than the problem.

    --- ORDER ---

      1. The 000000 branch, plus the rule that replaces it.
      2. The two privileged passwords (item 42 — the likeliest real vector now).
      3. Rate limiting and failed-login auditing.
      4. The enumeration and timing leak.
      5. trust proxy, once the origin is protected.


48) HOSTING BEHIND CLOUDFLARE — where each control belongs. Decided 2026-10-06.

    The question was whether the portal needs a banned list. The answer is no,
    and the reasoning is worth keeping because it is not "we can live without
    one" — it is that AN APP-SIDE IP BLOCKLIST WOULD BE ACTIVELY WRONG HERE.

    --- WHY NOT IN THE APPLICATION ---

      * THE APP CANNOT SEE THE ATTACKER. There is no `trust proxy` and nothing
        reads CF-Connecting-IP, so behind Cloudflare req.ip is CLOUDFLARE'S
        address. A blocklist built on it would block Cloudflare — which is
        everybody — or nothing at all, depending on which way the comparison
        fell. Either way it is not a control.
      * IT IS LEAKY. VPNs, mobile carriers, IPv6 rotations, botnets. An address
        block stops the lazy and inconveniences nobody else.
      * IT PRODUCES FALSE POSITIVES WITH REAL COSTS. An office or a carrier NAT
        is one address for many people, so a block can lock out the team while
        the attacker moves on.
      * IT DUPLICATES WHAT SITS IN FRONT OF IT, WORSE. Cloudflare has the
        reputation data, the bot scores and the ASN information. Anything the
        app could write, Cloudflare already does earlier and better.

    --- AND THE THING IT CANNOT DO AT ALL ---

    A banned list does nothing about item 47's 000000 backdoor, because that is
    a VALID LOGIN. Network-layer filtering cannot distinguish an attacker who
    has the password from the person who owns it. Fixing the login path is not
    something a blocklist can substitute for.

    --- WHERE THE CONTROLS BELONG ---

    FIRST, AND BEFORE ANY OF THE REST: PROTECT THE ORIGIN.

    If the origin accepts connections from anywhere, every Cloudflare rule is
    bypassable by connecting straight to the IP and sending the right Host
    header. So:

      * firewall the origin to Cloudflare's published address ranges, or
      * run the origin behind a Tunnel so it has no inbound port at all.

    Until this is done, everything below is decoration. It is also the
    prerequisite for `trust proxy` in item 47 — after this, and only after
    this, X-Forwarded-For can be trusted, because nothing else can reach the
    app to forge it.

    ALSO WORTH SETTLING WHILE IT IS BEING SET UP: the app currently binds every
    interface (item 44). With a firewall or a Tunnel in front, it should bind
    loopback only.

    THEN, AT CLOUDFLARE:

      * RATE LIMITING on /api/auth/* — the app has none, and this is the
        cheapest place to add the first layer.
      * TURNSTILE on the login form. This is the real answer to credential
        stuffing, and it is the piece that scales.
      * WAF MANAGED RULES, on by default.
      * COUNTRY OR ASN RULES if the team is geographically known. For a studio
        this gets most of the benefit of a blocklist for none of the
        maintenance — and it is a blocklist, just one somebody else maintains
        and updates.
      * CLOUDFLARE ACCESS IN FRONT OF /app/admin AND /app/superuser. This is the
        strongest control available here and it is the right shape for the rule
        in item 43: the admin pages become reachable only by identities that
        passed a second, independent check before the request ever reached the
        application. Worth comparing against item 47's 2FA work — they overlap,
        and Access may be the cheaper half.
      * "UNDER ATTACK" as the break-glass switch.

    AND A BANNED LIST OF ACCOUNTS ALREADY EXISTS: deactivation. It is immediate
    (authenticateToken re-reads is_active on every request) and audited.

    --- WHAT IS NOT BUILT AND IS NOT PLANNED ---

    No IP blocklist in the application. If one is ever wanted, it belongs at
    Cloudflare, where the real client address is known — and the reasoning above
    is the reason to write it there rather than here.


49) THE TESTER'S PAGE — rebuilt 2026-10-06. It is generated now, like everything
    else.

    IT WAS THE ONLY HAND-WRITTEN PAGE BEHIND THE SIGN-IN. index.html,
    shopfront.html and discussion.html are hand-written by design — nobody is
    signed in when they load, and they carry no components. user.html was not,
    and that is exactly why it had drifted:

      * its own navbar where every other signed-in page has the shared header,
        including a sign-out button carrying the same id the shared header uses;
      * a Discussion button duplicating the one in the utility bar loaded beside
        it, on the same line;
      * a card badged "Not built yet" for a report-a-problem form that had been
        working for weeks, and a closing paragraph repeating the same false
        claim.

    Nothing generated it, so every change to the rest of the app left it behind.

    NOW: frontend/src/components/userLanding.html plus an entry in build.js,
    with the shared header and the utility bar as components. It looks like the
    pages either side of it because it is made of the same parts.

    --- AND IT ASKS THE SERVER WHO YOU ARE ---

    The old page drew its "Your account" panel from localStorage.user_info.
    That value is written at sign-in and NEVER EXPIRES while the session cookie
    behind it does — so hours in, the page would greet you by name, print your
    role, and head a panel called "Your account" with details that were no
    longer true of anybody. It looked certain and it was stale.

    So there is a new endpoint, GET /api/auth/me, which reads the row on the
    request. The panel is the server's answer or it says it cannot reach one.
    The greeting still uses the stored profile, because a greeting is a courtesy
    rather than a claim.

    VERIFIED: with a stored profile and NO session cookie — exactly the stale
    state the old page would have drawn a full panel for — the page now lands on
    the sign-in page instead. apiFetch handles the 401, the same way it does
    everywhere else.

    --- TWO ROUTE BUGS, FOUND WHILE WIRING IT UP ---

    Neither was reachable, which is why both had survived. Nothing links to
    either.

      * GET /app/user SERVED shopfront.html — the protected route for a signed-in
        tester sent them to the sign-in page.
      * GET /app/developer REQUIRED superuser, so a developer asking for the
        developer dashboard would have been refused. Now
        requireRole('developer','admin','superuser'), following the rule in
        middleware/auth.js that naming 'admin' means naming 'superuser'.

        CORRECTED 2026-10-09: that rule is GONE, and middleware/auth.js says so
        itself ("THAT RULE IS NOW MOOT: 'superuser' was removed on 2026-10-06").
        So the line above describes a route guard as it was on the day of the
        fix, not as it is. Kept rather than rewritten, because it is a historical
        record of a fix — but it read as a present-tense claim about the code,
        which is how a removed role goes on being named in new work.

    Both verified after the fix: /app/user serves the tester page, and a
    developer gets 200 from /app/developer.

    --- THE TWO-FACTOR ROW, AND WHY IT READS has_2fa_secret ---

    The row was briefly removed and then put back, because the honest answer
    turned out to be available. It reads whether a SECRET EXISTS, not
    is_2fa_enabled — that flag is set automatically on first sign-in while no
    secret exists, so it reads 1 for every account in the system, and the page
    was confidently reporting "Two-factor: Set up" for accounts with no
    authenticator at all.

    GET /api/auth/me returns two_factor_secret IS NOT NULL AS has_2fa_secret,
    and never selects the secret itself. The panel says "Not set up" for every
    account until item 47 is done — which is the truth, and more useful on
    screen than hidden behind a flag that lies. When 47 is finished this is
    already the right place to show it.

    NOT DONE HERE: there is no way for anyone to SET UP two-factor from this
    page, and an alert() still greets a tester on sign-in in
    frontend/src/auth.js while every other role is redirected silently. Both are
    small and neither was asked for.


50) THE SUPERUSER'S POWERS — inventory. Removed from the website 2026-10-06.
    NOTHING IS SILENTLY DROPPED: every capability that existed has an entry
    here, with where it is going and whether that is done yet.

    WHY IT WAS REMOVED. `/api/superuser` held seven endpoints, and three
    different problems were mixed together in them. Two took a long-lived API
    token from a BROWSER FORM and put it into `process.env` at runtime — the
    ability to take over the edge or the repository, one stolen session away, in
    a request that cannot be audited after the fact. One triggered a production
    DEPLOY from a cookie. The rest were operations that belong on the machine.
    None of it needed to be reachable from the internet, and the page that
    hosted it was the only reason any of it was.

    AND A SUPERUSER LOST NOTHING BY THIS. They pass requireAdmin, so every admin
    page was already theirs.

    --- THE INVENTORY ---

    | was | where it goes | done? |
    |-----|---------------|-------|
    | POST /github/keys    | `.env` on the host. A secret is configuration, and this app already refuses to start without JWT_SECRET — these should match. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /cloudflare/keys| `.env` on the host, same reasoning. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /github/deploy  | `scripts/`, run on the machine. A release must not answer to a cookie. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /github/sync    | `scripts/`, same place as deploy. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /cloudflare/purge        | `scripts/`, or the Cloudflare dashboard. See note below. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /cloudflare/toggle-devmode | Same. | NOT DONE |
    | POST /developer/diagnostics   | THE HEALTH TAB (item 28), for admins. See note below. | NOT DONE |
    | "Sign in as another user" (loginModal) | Nowhere. Deleted. Real impersonation is item 5, still deferred. | DONE — deleted |
    | requireSuperuser middleware   | Nowhere. Redundant with requireRole('superuser'). | DONE — deleted |
    | The superuser dashboard, /app/superuser | Nowhere. A superuser uses the admin dashboard. | DONE — deleted |

    SO THE HONEST STATUS IS: the powers are withdrawn, not relocated. Right now
    a deploy, a cache purge or a token change has no home in this system at all —
    they would be done by hand on the machine or in the Cloudflare dashboard.
    That is not a gap in the plan, it is the state of it, and it is written here
    rather than assumed.

    --- NOTES ON TWO OF THEM ---

    DIAGNOSTICS IS NOT REALLY A SUPERUSER POWER, and it is the one worth keeping
    most. It reports memory, uptime, node version and BOOLEANS for whether
    tokens are configured — deliberately no secrets, which is why it is good. It
    is exactly the readout item 28's Health panel needs, alongside the disk
    figures that already exist. Recommend it lands there, for admins, rather than
    in a script.

    PURGE AND DEVMODE ARE A GENUINE CHOICE. Purging the cache affects everyone
    who uses the site; devmode bypasses the cache. Both are one `curl` against
    the Cloudflare API from the machine, so a script is cheap and keeps the rule
    simple — nothing that changes the live site answers to a cookie. The
    argument the other way is that they are operational rather than
    provisioning, and that being able to purge from a phone during an incident
    has real value. NOT DECIDED. If they do stay web-reachable, they belong on
    the ADMIN page (a superuser passes it), never behind a superuser-only door.

    --- AND THE TWO THAT WERE NEVER REACHABLE AT ALL ---

    Worth recording, because it was only found while removing them: the
    diagnostics endpoint and the "sign in as another user" dialog were both
    unreachable. Their buttons — runDiagnosticsBtn and signInAsBtn — were bound
    in superuser.js and declared in NO component, so nothing could open them.
    The id checker had been listing both as known gaps, which is what that
    mechanism is for. The superuser page shipped with two features no user could
    reach.


51) THE ROLE MODEL — SETTLED AND BUILT 2026-10-06. Four roles, and superuser is
    gone from the website AND the schema.

    THE LADDER IS user < developer < admin.

        user       a tester. No extra privileges.
        developer  works in a private workspace.
        prod       PRODUCTION — a craft role, not a seniority. Decides creative
                   approvals, comments, manages categories and boards.
        admin      administers the system. TOP OF THE LADDER; nothing above it.

    'SUPERUSER' WAS REMOVED ENTIRELY — not just from the website. It had 0
    accounts, no routes and no page, and the one property it provided (a peer
    cannot act on a peer, so the last administrator cannot be locked out) already
    holds at admin level because isStrictlyBelow is strictly below. Keeping a
    role nobody holds and nothing requires is the definition of the bloat this
    project keeps cutting.

    CONSEQUENCE, WORTH KNOWING: nobody can deactivate an administrator from the
    web — including another administrator. A rogue admin is dealt with on the
    machine. That is consistent with every other privileged act here, and it is a
    deliberate decision rather than an oversight.

    --- AUTHORITY IS NOW SEPARATE FROM ORDERING ---

    prod sits between developer and admin in ROLE_RANK, and that rank confers
    NOTHING. It is there for ordering lists. A rank comparison alone gave prod
    the ability to invite developers and deactivate them — it is numerically
    above a developer — and it did, until this was separated out.

    So backend/lib/roles.js has AUTHORITY_ROLES = ['user','developer','admin'] and
    hasAuthorityOver(a, b), which refuses any role not on that list before it
    compares ranks. Invitations and deactivation both go through it. A craft role
    added later is OUT of that list by default.

    THE GENERAL LESSON, since it will come up again: a ladder is a good model for
    "how senior is this person" and a poor one for "what is this person's job".
    prod is the second kind, and forcing it into the first produced two wrong
    permissions in one commit.

    --- WHAT IT TOOK ---

      * TWO TABLE REBUILDS, because SQLite cannot alter a CHECK. users.role
        (nine foreign keys point at it) and invitations.assigned_role. The users
        rebuild runs with foreign_keys OFF *outside* the transaction — a pragma
        inside one is a no-op — and is followed by a foreign_key_check. Both
        migrations now compare the WHOLE role list rather than looking for one
        value, so the next change needs no new migration written.
      * The users rebuild was DRY-RUN ON A COPY first, and the copy was used to
        prove the new CHECK accepts the four roles and rejects 'superuser' and
        'wizard'. The first attempt at this, earlier the same day, went straight
        at the live database; that was the wrong order and this one was not.
      * requireRole lists lost 'superuser' (23 sites); requireAdmin accepts admin
        only; the mailer's role labels, the tester page's labels, the header's
        role prefixes and the notification wording all follow.
      * scripts/create-superuser.js became scripts/accounts.js — a file named
        after a role it can no longer create. CREATABLE_ROLES is now
        ['admin','prod'].
      * The `superuser-module` CSS class became `panel-module` across 16 sites in
        9 files plus the stylesheet. It carried real rules, so a missed rename
        would have silently un-styled a card.

    VERIFIED: the four roles accepted and everything else rejected at the schema
    level; a prod account gets 403 from every /api/admin route and from
    generate-invite, while still signing in and reading its own account; the
    admin login flow works end to end; all five checks pass; and a
    comment-stripping sweep of all 65 website files finds no live reference to
    superuser anywhere — every remaining mention is a comment explaining that it
    is gone.

    THE OLD ITEM 51 TEXT BEGINS BELOW, because the reasoning about creative
    approval still applies to prod.

51b) WHO MAY DECIDE CREATIVE APPROVALS — decided 2026-10-06.

    TODAY IT IS AN ADMIN POWER, AND IT SHOULD NOT BE. The approve and reject
    routes are:

        POST /creative-approvals/:approvalId/approve   requireRole('admin','superuser')
        POST /creative-approvals/:approvalId/reject    requireRole('admin','superuser')

    So the only person who can say an asset is good enough for the game is
    somebody who can also deactivate accounts, change the invite window, read
    the audit log and change every setting. Approving work is a judgement about
    the WORK. Administering the system is a different job, and this conflates
    them: it means an art lead cannot review models without being made a full
    administrator.

    THE DATA MODEL ALREADY DISAGREES WITH THE PERMISSION MODEL. creative_approvals
    has requested_by and decided_by as separate columns, and the request route is
    separate from the decide route — the developer submits, somebody else judges.
    That separation is right and it is already built. What is missing is a
    permission that says "may judge creative work" without saying "may run the
    system".

    DECIDED: creative approval moves to a role or attribute called **prod**,
    which may also add comments. NOT admin, NOT superuser. (Superuser was
    removed ENTIRELY — schema, routes and page — on 2026-10-06; item 50 is the
    inventory and item 51 is the removal. CORRECTED 2026-10-09: this said it
    "keeps its role in the schema", which stopped being true when it was
    removed, and a decision record that leaves a removed thing half-present is
    how somebody builds against it.)

    --- SETTLED: A ROLE. ONE ROLE PER ACCOUNT, ALWAYS. ---

    Decided 2026-10-06: an account holds exactly ONE role and never more than
    one. So 'prod' is a role in the ladder, not an orthogonal attribute. The
    schema already enforces the constraint:

        role TEXT CHECK(role IN ('user','developer','prod','admin')) NOT NULL

    --- WHAT THAT DECISION EXPOSES, AND IT NEEDS SETTLING FIRST ---

    A LADDER AND "NOT ADMIN" CANNOT BOTH BE TRUE, and this is the whole of it.

    A role ladder means a higher rung passes every check a lower one passes: an
    admin is also a developer. That is exactly WHY "an admin may not decide
    creative approvals" is not expressible as a rung — if prod sits below admin,
    admins inherit prod's powers by definition, and if prod sits ABOVE admin then
    prod inherits account deactivation and every settings power, which is worse.

    SETTLED 2026-10-06, in four parts:

      * AN ADMINISTRATOR MAY NOT APPROVE CREATIVE WORK. Prod only. Superuser
        neither — that role has no website presence at all (item 50).
      * PROD HAS NO ADMIN POWERS.
      * PROD CANNOT DEACTIVATE ANYONE.
      * PROD HAS NO WORKSPACE. It does not upload, version or hold anything.

    THAT LAST ONE UNDOES THE ARGUMENT FOR PUTTING PROD ABOVE DEVELOPER, which
    was mine and it was wrong. I had argued a creative director is usually also
    a maker, so prod must sit above developer to keep their workspace. If prod
    has no workspace, it needs nothing from the rung below it — and putting it
    above developer would GIVE it a workspace through every route that says
    "at least a developer".

    SO PROD INHERITS NOTHING, and that is the cleanest statement of what it is:
    a CRAFT ROLE, not a seniority. It reviews and comments. Everything it may do
    comes from named capabilities, not from a rung, and the developer routes
    simply do not name it.

    SO THE LADDER IS NO LONGER ENOUGH FOR THIS ONE THING, BY CONSTRUCTION. A
    rung cannot express it: admin is above prod, so an admin would inherit
    prod's powers if prod were merely a rung, and prod above admin would inherit
    deactivation and every settings power instead. Creative approval therefore
    becomes a NAMED CAPABILITY — a role is a bundle of permissions, and 'admin'
    simply does not contain "decide creative approvals".

    THIS DOES NOT MEAN RE-EXPRESSING ALL 23 requireRole() CALLS. Only the
    genuinely NON-HIERARCHICAL things need capabilities. Everything else — may
    you upload, may you see the admin pages, may you deactivate this account —
    is a real seniority question and stays a rank comparison. Two mechanisms,
    each where it fits, rather than one stretched over both.

    --- AND THE QUESTION THAT DECIDES WHETHER THE ROLE IS USEFUL AT ALL ---

    HOW DOES PROD SEE THE WORK IT IS JUDGING?

    It cannot today, and not by accident. A developer's workspace is PRIVATE —
    the rule is deliberate and is stated in the manual: "There is no read path
    into a developer's workspace. Not for administrators, not for superusers."
    The only mechanism that answers it is IMPERSONATION, which is marked,
    time-bounded and audit-logged — and is item 5, decided but not built.

    So a creative director asked to approve a model currently cannot open the
    model. The approval route takes an approvalId and decides on it; the files
    live in a workspace nobody may read.

    THIS IS THE REAL BLOCKER, not the permission plumbing. Three ways out, and
    they are genuinely different decisions:

      * BUILD IMPERSONATION (item 5 as designed) and let a reviewer act as the
        developer, marked and logged. Honours the existing rule exactly, and is
        the largest piece of work.

      * A REVIEW VIEW: when a version is submitted for approval, expose that
        version's files to the reviewer and nobody else. A deliberate, narrow
        exception to workspace privacy — the submission is the developer
        offering the work for judgement, so showing it to the judge is arguably
        the point rather than a leak. Needs deciding whether that is a real
        exception or a hole in the rule.

      * NOTHING FOR NOW: build the permission model, leave the review workflow
        unusable, and record that honestly.

    NOT DECIDED, and it should be decided before building any of the rest —
    a role that can approve things it cannot see is not a role.

    --- WHAT BUILDING IT ACTUALLY TAKES ---

      1. ROLE_RANK gains 'prod'. WHERE IS NOT OBVIOUS NOW. It inherits nothing
         either way, so its number matters only for the questions rank still
         answers: who may invite whom, who may deactivate whom, and how people
         sort in a list. Placing it between developer and admin is the least
         surprising for the last of those; deactivation is moving to
         admin-and-above regardless (see below), and invitation authority wants
         the same treatment — prod should not be inviting people, for the same
         reason it should not be deactivating them.

      2. THE USERS TABLE HAS TO BE REBUILT, and this is the part to be careful
         with. The role column is:

             role TEXT CHECK(role IN ('user','developer','admin','superuser'))

         SQLite cannot ALTER a CHECK, so adding 'prod' means create-copy-drop-
         rename — the same procedure db.js already uses for invitations. On
         users, though, NINE foreign keys point at it, so the rebuild has to
         run with foreign_keys OFF outside a transaction, then a
         foreign_key_check afterwards. It is the most important table in the
         system and the change is not reversible by editing a line back.

      3. A capability layer: a role-to-capabilities map and a requireCapability
         helper, alongside requireRole rather than replacing it. First (and for
         now only) capability: creative.approve. prod has it; admin does not.

      4. The two approval routes change from requireRole('admin','superuser') to
         the capability check.

      5. ROLE CHECKS BECOME RANK COMPARISONS where they are lists. With a fifth
         role, "name every role that qualifies" is a trap — middleware/auth.js
         already documents the failure mode ("a route that lists 'admin' and
         forgets 'superuser' does not error, it just refuses the most privileged
         account"). requireAtLeast('developer') makes a new rung safe to add.
         This is the change that stops prod being silently locked out of the
         developer routes.

      6. FRONTEND: ROLE_LABELS, the role badge map, the header prefix, and the
         invite dropdown — prod must not be invitable from the web, for the same
         reason admin and superuser are not (item 43).

      SETTLED: prod may not deactivate anyone, and may not invite anyone.
      Deactivation and invitation authority both become admin-and-above rather
      than "strictly below me", because they are administrative acts and prod is
      not an administrative role.

    --- AND TWO SMALLER CONSEQUENCES OF A NEW RUNG ---

    WHATEVER IS DECIDED, A NEW RUNG BREAKS THE "NAME EVERY ROLE" CONVENTION.
    middleware/auth.js already documents the problem: "a route that lists 'admin'
    and forgets 'superuser' does not error, it just refuses the most privileged
    account in the system." That convention was survivable with four roles and a
    note; with a fifth it is a trap. If prod is a rung, the role checks want to
    become rank comparisons — requireAtLeast('developer') rather than a list of
    names — so that inserting a rung needs no changes anywhere. That is the
    change that makes the ladder safe to extend, and it is worth doing at the
    same time as adding the rung rather than after.

    AND A CREATIVE DIRECTOR MUST KEEP THEIR WORKSPACE. Somebody who judges models
    is usually also modelling, so prod must sit ABOVE developer — otherwise
    making them prod takes away their uploads, versions and history, because
    every developer-facing route is an allow-list that would no longer name their
    role. This is an argument for the rung, not against it: in a ladder, prod
    above developer inherits the workspace automatically.

    THE DEACTIVATION QUESTION COMES WITH IT, AND IS NOT DECIDED. Rank currently
    decides who may deactivate whom (isStrictlyBelow). A prod above developer
    would be able to deactivate developers — which a creative director almost
    certainly should not be able to do. Two answers:

      * deactivation becomes admin-and-above regardless of rung, or
      * deactivation becomes a capability too, alongside creative.approve.

    The first is simpler and probably right: deactivating somebody is an
    administrative act, like every other thing admins do, and prod is not an
    administrative role. It would also be the second thing the ladder gets
    wrong, which is worth noticing: the ladder is a decent model for "how senior
    is this person" and a poor one for "what is this person's job".

    --- AND COMMENTS, WHICH ARE NOT BUILT AT ALL ---

    asset_comments IS AN UNUSED TABLE. Nothing reads it and nothing writes it —
    no route exists. Meanwhile the manual promises testers can "download,
    comment, report", and two of those three work. So there is a promise with
    nothing behind it. (The first of the three is a RELEASE CANDIDATE rather than
    "a build" — corrected 2026-10-09, see the invitations section.)

    Decided here: prod may comment. STILL OPEN: whether testers and everyone else
    may as well. The recommendation on 2026-10-06 was YES — a tester's entire
    purpose is to give feedback, author_id already records who said it, and
    gating comments at developer-and-up would let a tester report a bug but not
    say "this jump feels wrong", which is the more useful half.

    WHERE THE OUTPUT GOES IS ALSO UNBUILT. Nothing renders comments, so building
    the ability to write them without somewhere to read them makes no sense.
    Both belong with whatever page shows a version's review state — the history
    view already reports the latest approval and the latest technical check.

    --- WHAT IS NOT IN QUESTION ---

    technical_check_runs.started_by — the developer runs it on their own version,
    and only their own. The routes resolve a version with
    `WHERE wv.id = ? AND dw.developer_id = ?`, so one developer cannot act on
    another's workspace, and an admin has no workspace to act through. That is
    correct and stays.

    app_settings.updated_by — admin and superuser, chosen on 2026-10-06 when the
    settings mechanism was built. Not part of this item.


52) WHAT PROD CAN DO — added 2026-10-06, on top of item 51.

    PROD IS A CREATIVE/COMMUNITY ROLE. It is not a seniority and it inherits
    nothing from the rung below it. Its powers, so far:

      * decide creative approvals            (item 51 — NOT admin, NOT superuser)
      * add comments                         (item 51)
      * CREATE ASSET CATEGORIES              (new)
      * CREATE DISCUSSION BOARDS/CHANNELS    (new)

    And it definitely does NOT have:

      * a workspace — it does not upload, version or hold anything
      * any admin power — accounts, settings, audit log
      * the ability to deactivate anyone
      * the ability to invite anyone

    --- AND THE REASON IS THE RULE, NOT THE LIST ---

    Stated 2026-10-06: PROD HAS NO BACKEND TECHNICAL EXPERTISE. Accounts and
    settings are not withheld from prod because somebody decided they should
    not have them — they are withheld because prod is a CREATIVE role and those
    are ENGINEERING surfaces.

    THAT GIVES A TEST FOR ANY FUTURE PROD CAPABILITY, which is worth more than
    the list it replaces:

        IS THIS A DECISION ABOUT THE WORK, OR ABOUT THE SYSTEM?

      Categories are the work — what kind of thing is this asset, and what kinds
      do we recognise. Boards are the work — how the team talks about it.
      Approving and commenting are the work. All prod.

      Accounts, settings, the audit log, retention windows, disk thresholds,
      tokens, deployments — the system. None of it prod, however convenient it
      would be to let the person who cares most about the taxonomy also fix a
      setting.

    The rule also settles the shape of the capability names: everything prod
    holds is content-shaped (creative.approve, comment, category.manage,
    board.manage), and nothing prod holds is system-shaped. If a proposed
    capability needs backend knowledge to use safely, it is not prod's.

    --- ONE ADJACENT QUESTION, NOT DECIDED ---

    BUG REPORTS. Reading and resolving feedback is admin-gated today
    (requireAdmin on the list and resolve routes). Is that systems work or
    community work?

    Arguably a prod should be able to READ reports — "this jump feels wrong" is
    feedback about the work, and the person closest to the work is the useful
    one to see it. Resolving them is more administrative. NOT DECIDED, and it is
    the next place the content/system line has to be drawn.

    --- CREATING CATEGORIES CHANGES WHERE THE LIST LIVES ---

    Asset categories are currently a CONSTANT, not data:

        shared/assetRules.mjs → ASSET_CATEGORIES
        ['Character','Environment','Prop','Vehicle','Weapon','FX','Audio','UI','Other']

    in the one shared file both halves of the app use, with normaliseCategory()
    beside it. That was a deliberate decision on 2026-10-02 and it is why the
    client and the server cannot disagree about what a category is.

    IF PROD CAN CREATE THEM, THE LIST BECOMES DATA, and that has consequences
    worth knowing before it is built:

      * the client can no longer import the list — it has to FETCH it, so the
        category dropdown depends on a request that can fail, and needs a
        sensible state when it does;
      * the hint-versus-check distinction has to survive: a category is a HINT,
        so an unknown value is KEPT rather than rejected (unlike feedback kinds).
        A dynamic list must not quietly turn that into a strict check;
      * renaming or removing a category that assets already carry needs deciding
        — the column is free text on purpose, so old values keep working, but the
        dropdown would stop offering them;
      * and where the list lives moves from shared/ to the database, which is the
        same move the settings made (item 37) and for the same reason.

    NOT BUILT. Recorded so the constant is not quietly converted into a table
    without these decisions being taken.

    --- CREATING BOARDS IS FOR A FEATURE THAT DOES NOT EXIST ---

    "BB" is the discussion board, item 27: self-hosted Matrix/Element, decided
    but NOT BUILT. There is a discussion.html stub and nothing behind it.

    So "prod can create boards" is a capability for an unbuilt feature, and it
    cannot be built before the board is. Recorded here so the role's shape is
    complete — when the board arrives, creating channels on it is prod's job,
    not an admin's.

    WORTH NOTING: the board is the THIRD thing now waiting on item 27, after
    "Talk to the team" on the tester page and the Discussion link in the utility
    bar. The role model is running ahead of the feature.

    --- WHERE THIS LEAVES THE ROLE MODEL ---

    prod is turning into the CREATIVE/COMMUNITY role: it judges work, says
    things about it, and organises it — categories and boards. That is a
    coherent job, and it is NOT any part of administering the system, which is
    exactly the separation item 51 is about.

    It also means the capability list is growing past one entry, which is worth
    noticing: creative.approve, comment, category.manage, board.manage. Still
    small enough for a map in code, and worth watching — if it reaches a dozen,
    that is the point to reconsider roles-as-capability-sets properly rather
    than bolting another name onto the map.


===========================================================================

AFTER THE GAME EXISTS — work that cannot be done, or cannot be finished, until
there is a game. Kept in its own section because the website and the game are
functionally different things: the portal is built, and the game is not, and
several items only LOOK like website work until you try to write them.

===========================================================================

NOTHING HERE IS BLOCKED ON THE WEBSITE. Each one is blocked on the game existing
— a build, a version, a release, or content that has been through the store. If
an item below could be done today, it should not be in this section.

A) HOW DOES A REVIEWER SEE THE WORK THEY ARE JUDGING? — the blocker found while
   building the prod page (2026-10-06).

   A prod can decide a creative approval. It cannot open what it is deciding.

   A developer's workspace is PRIVATE — that is a deliberate rule, stated in the
   manual: "There is no read path into a developer's workspace. Not for
   administrators, not for superusers." The only mechanism that answers it is
   IMPERSONATION (item 5), which is marked, time-bounded, audit-logged, and NOT
   BUILT.

   The prod page says this on screen rather than letting a reviewer hunt for a
   bug. Three ways out, and they are genuinely different decisions:

     * Build impersonation (item 5 as designed). Honours the rule exactly.
       Largest piece of work, and the only one that does not change the rule.
     * A REVIEW VIEW: when a version is submitted for approval, expose THAT
       version's files to the reviewer and nobody else. The submission is the
       developer offering the work for judgement, so showing it to the judge is
       arguably the point rather than a leak — but it IS an exception to a rule
       that was stated firmly, and exceptions to privacy rules are how privacy
       rules end.
     * Not at all: keep deciding on metadata. Workable for a small studio where
       the reviewer and the maker sit together, and useless otherwise.

   Not decided. It has been postponed twice — first while designing prod, then
   while building its page — and the page now carries a visible note about it, so
   the postponement is at least honest.

B) DEPLOYING THE GAME. What the removed superuser page could do: triggering a
   release via GitHub. Withdrawn with that page and currently with NO home at
   all — see item 50.

   CORRECTED 2026-10-09, AND THE CORRECTION IS THAT IT SHOULD NOT COME BACK.
   Under the two-system decision (item 62) DEPLOYING IS THE BUILD MACHINE'S JOB
   AND THE GAME IS IN PERFORCE — so a portal that triggers a deploy duplicates
   both, and GitHub was only ever right while the game was assumed to live in
   this repository (see the note on game/ below). WHAT THE PORTAL DOES INSTEAD:
   RECORDS THE RELEASE — a name, a changelist, who built it, when — and nothing
   more. Item 67.

   Kept rather than deleted because it is the clearest example of a capability
   that was withdrawn AND should stay withdrawn, rather than waiting to be
   rebuilt.

   It is recorded here rather than in item 50 because item 50 is an inventory of
   what was removed, and this is a piece of work in its own right.

C) STORAGE BY GAME VERSION. The Storage tab has a marked "not built" section for
   it. It needs a game version to measure, and nothing in the schema records
   which content belongs to one — db.js anticipates a `releases` table that was
   never built. THE MODEL IS DECIDED: item 53 — named releases, membership by
   query. When versions exist: the bytes per version, so hosting elsewhere
   can be bought rather than estimated, and so a version quietly growing out of
   proportion is visible before it is a problem.

   THE ARITHMETIC — CORRECTED 2026-10-09, BECAUSE IT SAID THE OPPOSITE OF THE
   TRUTH ABOUT THE THING IT MEASURES.

   It said: "content is stored once and referenced by everything that needs it,
   so a file in two versions is one file on disk and the per-version figures will
   not sum to the total."

   THAT IS TRUE OF THE ASSET STORE AND FALSE OF BUILDS. Builds are NOT
   content-addressed and must not pretend to be (item 60): each carries its own
   cooked copy of a texture inside its own paks. SO PER-VERSION FIGURES FOR
   BUILDS WILL SUM ROUGHLY TO THE TOTAL, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT WAS
   WRITTEN — and somebody would have gone looking for a double-counting bug that
   does not exist, in the one place the note told them it would be.

   The one-copy arithmetic DOES hold for the store, and if this figure is ever
   computed over STORE CONTENT grouped by which release uses it, then it holds
   and the original wording is right. WHICH OF THE TWO IT MEASURES IS PART OF
   WHAT IS NOT BUILT, and it decides the answer.

D) OPTIMISATION — where the bytes actually went. The follow-on to (C), and the
   thing to build when a build is too big to host. Needs (C) first.

E) TESTERS PLAYING A RELEASE CANDIDATE. The tester page's first card, badged
   "not built yet", and it is accurate — but CORRECTED 2026-10-09, because it
   said "there is no build to download and no route that would serve one", and
   under the two-system decision there never will be (items 60, 62). A portal
   route serving builds is out of scope, not missing.

   WHAT IS ACTUALLY WANTED is narrower and clearer: A RELEASE CANDIDATE under
   test — named, numbered, tied to a changelist — with the portal RECORDING which
   candidate it is and who may fetch it. The bytes come from wherever builds
   live. The manual promises testers "download, comment, report", and two of the
   three exist.

F) COMMENTS (item 51) cannot be finished either. The table is unbuilt, the write
   route does not exist, and NOTHING RENDERS A COMMENT — and comments are about
   versions of work. "This jump feels wrong" has nowhere to attach until there is
   something being tested.

G) THE DISCUSSION BOARD (item 27) is not game-dependent, and is NOT in this
   section for that reason — but note it is now the third thing waiting on
   itself, after the tester page's "Talk to the team" card and the utility bar's
   Discussion link. Prod creating boards (item 52) is a capability for it.


===========================================================================

THE GAME PROJECT — structure created 2026-10-06. Separate from the website, and
kept in this file for now because there is one TODO rather than two.

===========================================================================

The game lives in `game/` at the repository root. UNREAL IS ALREADY INSTALLED
and is not to be installed, configured or otherwise touched — see the note at
the end of this entry.

RAISED 2026-10-09, AND IT NEEDS AN ANSWER: THE GAME IS IN *THIS* REPOSITORY.
`git ls-files game/` lists the .uproject and ten .gitkeep files, so the portal's
git repository is currently versioning the game project's structure — which is
exactly what the two-system decision (item 62) says PERFORCE should do. The
.gitkeep work was right when it was done and there was no decision; now there is
one, and two systems both versioning the same tree is the duplication this design
exists to avoid.

THE LIKELY ANSWER IS THAT THE GAME LEAVES THIS REPOSITORY — a website and a game
are two projects with different histories, different sizes and different
tools — with Perforce holding it from the first commit, which is what item 62's
action list already assumes. NOT DECIDED, and recorded here because it is a
consequence of a decision rather than a thing anybody would think to look for.

--- WHAT THE PROJECT IS CALLED ---

    game/LowExpectations.uproject

RENAMED from MyOpenWorld.uproject on 2026-10-06. The game is Low Expectations,
and the project name is not decoration: it becomes the C++ module name, so it
appears in every .Build.cs, every target file and every include path once any
code exists. It was free to change on the day it was done and gets more
expensive from here.

An Unreal project takes its name from the FILENAME — there is no name field
inside the .uproject — so the rename was the whole change.

--- THE STRUCTURE, AND WHY MOST OF IT WAS NOT IN THE REPOSITORY ---

    game/
      LowExpectations.uproject
      Config/
      Content/
        _Core/            shared: master materials, base classes
        Art/              source art the other folders build from
        Environment/      the world's own art: landscapes, biomes, kits
        Features/         gameplay features, one folder each
        World/            the levels and their World Partition data
        Developers/       per-developer scratch, never shipped
      Plugins/
      Source/

EVERY FOLDER WAS EMPTY, AND GIT CANNOT TRACK AN EMPTY DIRECTORY. So the studio's
structure existed on this machine and nowhere else — the only thing in version
control was the .uproject. A .gitkeep now sits in each, which is what puts the
structure into the repository. Each carries one line saying what belongs in it,
so the taxonomy is documented where somebody will actually see it rather than
only here.

`Plugins/` did not exist at all and was created. It is where content that OTHER
projects should be able to reuse belongs, as opposed to Content/ which is this
game's.

--- THE CONTENT TAXONOMY, AND WHAT IS MISSING FROM IT ---

The six folders above were already there and are a considered structure: `_Core`
with the underscore so it sorts first, `Developers` for per-artist scratch, and
a split between the world's art, gameplay features, and shared art. NONE OF THAT
WAS INVENTED HERE — it was on disk, unversioned, and is now recorded.

WHAT THE CANONICAL SET WOULD ALSO HAVE, AND THIS DOES NOT: `Maps/`, `Audio/`,
`UI/`, `FX/`. The reading is that `Features/` absorbs the gameplay ones and
`World/` holds the levels — but IF `World/` IS NOT WHERE THE .umap FILES GO,
that needs deciding now rather than after somebody puts a level somewhere
arbitrary. Undecided.

--- TWO THINGS LEFT DELIBERATELY UNDONE ---

SOURCE/ HAS ONLY ITS KEEP-FILE. The C++ scaffolding is
LowExpectations.Target.cs, LowExpectationsEditor.Target.cs and
LowExpectations/LowExpectations.Build.cs — and two of those pin engine settings:

    DefaultBuildSettings = BuildSettingsVersion.V5;
    IncludeOrderVersion  = EngineIncludeOrderVersion.Unreal5_5;

Those must match the engine actually installed, and they are NOT to be guessed:
a wrong value fails the build with an error that reads like something else.
Unreal writes them correctly when the first C++ class is added, using whatever
version is there. Let the editor do it.

EngineAssociation IS EMPTY in the .uproject:

    { "FileVersion": 3, "EngineAssociation": "", "Category": "", "Description": "" }

That is what pins which engine opens the project, and nothing currently pins it.
It is filled the first time the project is opened with an engine chosen — a job
for the editor, not for a script.

--- AND ONE THING THE GITIGNORE GETS WRONG ---

`.gitignore` ignores `game/Build/`. `Build/` is where Unreal keeps AUTHORED
packaging assets — the project icon, and the FileOpenOrder lists that stop the
cooker reordering packages between builds. It is not a generated folder like
`Intermediate/` or `Saved/`, it is small, and it is worth keeping.

Untouched so far because nothing is in it yet. It should be un-ignored BEFORE
somebody puts an icon there and loses it to a clean.

--- AND THE STANDING RULE, WRITTEN DOWN BECAUSE IT WAS ASKED FOR TWICE ---

UNREAL, BLENDER, MAYA AND NUKE ARE NOT TO BE INSTALLED, LAUNCHED, CONFIGURED OR
FIDDLED WITH. They are installed and working. Nothing about the work in this
repository requires touching them, and an agent investigating them is risk with
no upside. If something seems to need one of them, ask.


53) RELEASES — DECIDED 2026-10-08, NOT BUILT. Named releases, and membership you
    query rather than a field you maintain.

    THE DECISION, in the user's words: *"they should be a named release and my
    naming is going backwards"* — the naming being production / development /
    experimental, which are CHANNELS. A channel is a small fixed set of tracks.
    A NAMED RELEASE is a specific build with a date: "0.4 Experimental, 12 Nov".
    Channels cannot express a history, and a game version has one.

    AND MEMBERSHIP IS DERIVED BY QUERY, not stored on the asset:
    *"you are right about finding the constituents of a game with a query — so
    lets keep that"*.

    --- THIS WAS ALREADY DECIDED ONCE, IN THE SCHEMA ---

    db.js carries the note and this is a restatement of it, not a new idea:

        "A single 'recommended version' pointer cannot express the intended
         model — an asset can hold several production versions at once, and
         PRODUCTION STATUS IS DERIVED FROM RELEASE MEMBERSHIP, not from a
         pointer. Removed in the phase that introduces `releases`."

    That phase never came. `asset_recommended_versions` is still sitting in the
    schema, unused and orphaned — see the note about recommended_version_id in
    the PRE-EXISTING section near the top of this file. It is the visible
    symptom of a model that was designed and never built.

    --- WHY NOT A FIELD ON THE ASSET SAYING WHICH CHANNEL IT IS IN ---

      * THE SAME VERSION IS IN SEVERAL AT ONCE. In development now, experimental
        next week, production after that. A field holds one value and would need
        rewriting every time — by hand, so it would not get rewritten.
      * IT WOULD BE A SECOND SOURCE OF TRUTH beside the membership, and the two
        would drift.
      * MEMBERSHIP IS MANY-TO-MANY: a release holds many asset versions, and one
        asset version is in many releases. That is a table, not a column.

    So "is this asset in production?" is a QUERY. A stored flag that answers the
    same question is a flag that can be wrong.

    --- FOUR LAYERS, NOT THREE ---

        source_assets/     candidates; may never be used      the studio    never ships
        workspace          private staging, iterations        ONE developer never ships
        game store         APPROVED content                   the studio    ships
        releases           which of it is in which build      the studio    that build

    IN THE STORE IS NOT THE SAME AS IN THE GAME, and that was the gap this
    started from: content can be tech-checked and creatively approved and sit
    unplaced for months. That is normal — studios approve deliberately, before
    they need something — so approved-but-unplaced BELONGS in the store. The
    store means "approved, ownership has passed to the game", which is a fact
    about the content and not about which build happens to use it.

    The two axes are worth holding onto, because they are independent:

        by VISIBILITY   private to one developer  →  visible to the studio
        by DESTINY      never ships  →  ships in a named release

    A workspace is private and never ships. The store is shared and ships. That
    is why reference material cannot live in a workspace however convenient it
    would be: it is studio material in the one place the system deliberately
    hides from everyone else.

    --- WHAT IT UNBLOCKS: STORAGE BY GAME VERSION ---

    MOVED HERE FROM ITEM 38 on 2026-10-08. It was recorded as the fourth point of
    the storage panel rework, which is where the PROVISION was made — but "bytes
    per release" is a question about the game that happens to be displayed on
    that tab, and item 38 is the panel's record.

    What was asked for: how many bytes each game version takes, so the storage
    needed to host a build elsewhere can be READ OFF rather than estimated, and
    so a version growing out of proportion is visible before it is a problem.
    Later, tools to break that down and find what is worth optimising.

    THE STORAGE TAB CARRIES A MARKED "NOT BUILT" SECTION for it rather than
    nothing at all. That section says what it will show, why it cannot yet, and
    the one thing that will be true when it can: content is stored once and
    referenced by everything that needs it, so a file counted under two versions
    is ONE FILE ON DISK and the per-version figures WILL NOT SUM TO THE TOTAL.
    That belongs on the screen rather than only in this file, because it is the
    first thing somebody will report as a bug.

    AND THE PLACEHOLDER IS NOT A REASON TO BUILD ANY OF IT. The instruction was
    to make provision in the UI and write the backend once a real game version
    exists — this item waits for the GAME, not for that panel. The tempting
    mistake is the reverse: building a releases model to fill a placeholder, and
    finding out afterwards that it answers the wrong question.

    (It was also, until an hour ago, telling the next person not to build a
    releases model at all — which was correct while the model was undecided and
    became false the moment this item decided it. Recorded because the two
    sentences were each right and together contradicted each other, which is how
    a decision record starts arguing with itself.)

    --- OPEN, AND NOT OBVIOUS ---

      * WHO MANAGES RELEASES? The TODO used to say admin/superuser, and superuser
        is gone (item 51). By the test used for prod — is this a decision about
        the WORK or about the SYSTEM? — deciding what is in a build is a decision
        about the work, which points at prod. But making a release is arguably an
        administrative act. NOT DECIDED, and the three places that asserted
        admin/superuser have been corrected to point here rather than left saying
        something false.
      * A RELEASE IS A HISTORICAL RECORD. What shipped, when. That argues for
        immutability once created — you do not edit history — and for it being
        audited like everything else that records a decision. Not settled.
      * VERSIONS OR ASSETS? The db.js note says "an asset can hold several
        production versions at once", so membership is at the VERSION level, not
        the asset. Worth confirming when it is built, since it changes the join.
      * WHAT IDENTIFIES A RELEASE? ANSWERED 2026-10-09 by item 67: A NAME, A
        PERFORCE CHANGELIST, WHO BUILT IT AND WHEN. It said "a name and a date at
        least. Whether it also carries a build number, a git tag or a changelog
        is undecided" — and A GIT TAG IS WRONG, because the game is versioned in
        Perforce, not git (items 62, 67). The changelist is the tag.
        A changelog is still open, and probably belongs to the release as prose
        rather than as a mechanism.

    --- AND WHAT IS NOT IN QUESTION ---

    It is still deferred. Everything here is recorded so that when the game
    exists the model is waiting rather than being re-derived — which is what
    happened on 2026-10-08, when a discussion about where candidate images live
    arrived at a decision the schema had already made.


54) THE IMAGE PIPELINE — a run-through against the code, 2026-10-08. What exists,
    what does not, and the two places the CODE CONTRADICTS WHAT WAS ASKED FOR.

    The run-through was ten steps of a developer working with a directory of
    images. They resolve to three answers: built, absent, or built in a shape
    that disagrees with the request.

    CORRECTED LATER THE SAME DAY — see item 57. The run-through sent images
    through approval and into the game store, and an image NEVER GOES THERE. An
    image lives in the IMAGE LIBRARY; only an ASSET reaches the store, and an
    image is in the game by being PART of one. Steps 4-5 below are the ASSET
    pipeline, which is the one that was verified — not the image one.

    --- THE TABLE ---

      1  upload a directory of images            EXISTS — WRONG IDENTITY MODEL (A)
      2  submit the directory to a tech check    EXISTS
      3  pass some files, fail others, with
         reasons shown in the UI                 HALF — the reasons exist, the
                                                 per-file verdict does not (B)
      4  submit the passed images for approval   EXISTS, but approval is per
                                                 VERSION, not per file (B)
      5  approved images reach the asset store   WRONG — see item 57. What
                                                 reaches the store is an ASSET.
                                                 An image reaches the LIBRARY.
      6  labelling what the images depict        MISSING
      7  thumbnails                              MISSING — no image library at all
      8  search by content                       MISSING, depends on 6
      9  pull images into a workspace, download  HALF — download exists, IMPORT
                                                 does not (C)
     10  the image inside magicTree_v01 with no
         second copy in the store                THE GUARANTEE EXISTS, THE PATH
                                                 DOES NOT (C)

    --- (A) THE FOLDER NAMES THE ASSET — RESOLVED 2026-10-08 ---

    SETTLED, and it settles by separating THREE THINGS that were being
    conflated. The user's words: *"the dev should be asked if there is a
    name(s) that go with the images — which would be a step on the content
    labelling — so that they could be retrieved as a batch — and could appear in
    the source asset display listings."*

        THE CONTAINER NAME   the folder or zip the files arrived in.
                             TRANSPORT. It names nothing, and it is never
                             retained in the workspace, the store or a listing.

        THE ASSET NAME       what a thing is called in the game store. AN
                             IMAGE NEVER BECOMES A GAME ASSET — it becomes PART
                             OF one (item 57), so this question belongs to the
                             asset the developer builds, not to the images.

        THE NAME(S)          a COLLECTION LABEL over a set of images, asked for
                             at the LABELLING step — where a human is already
                             going through them deciding what they depict. Its
                             two purposes are retrieval as a batch and appearing
                             in the source-asset listings.

    SO THE DEVELOPER IS NOT ASKED TO NAME ANYTHING AT UPLOAD. The folder or zip
    is how their files got here and nothing more, which is what "the directory is
    just convenient" meant.

    AND THE NAME IS NOT IN COMPETITION WITH TAGS. They answer different
    questions and both are wanted:

        TAGS    what is IN this image      trees, river, boat       → search
        NAME    what this BATCH is          "wales trip 2025"        → retrieval

    WHAT ALREADY EXISTS, AND IT IS MOSTLY THE MECHANISM: POST
    /assets/check-name is a preflight that derives a name, returns it, and the
    client SHOWS IT TO A PERSON — it renders "From the folder X" — before
    anything is stored. The human can already override it with upload_name or
    confirmed_asset_name, and the server honours both.

    WHAT IS WRONG WITH IT IS THE DEFAULT, NOT THE MECHANISM: the container name
    is PRE-FILLED, so a meaningless photos_from_wales is what the human is
    invited to accept. A pre-filled field is a strong suggestion even when the
    label above it says "from the folder".

    --- AND THE OLD TEXT, KEPT BECAUSE IT IS WHAT THIS REPLACED ---

    The folder names the asset, and that was just said to be wrong ---

    deriveUploadRoot() and classifyFolderName() take the uploaded DIRECTORY's name
    as the asset name and read a version marker out of it: bigTree_v04/ becomes
    asset "bigTree". Deliberate, and documented in docs/uploadmodel01.

    So dropping a directory of loose images in today creates ONE ASSET NAMED AFTER
    THE DIRECTORY, holding every image — the opposite of what is wanted. The
    directory is transport; it should not become an identity.

    THIS IS A CHANGE TO THE DOCUMENTED UPLOAD MODEL, NOT A FIX. And it is the same
    "what counts as one asset" question that was left open in the discussion of
    2026-10-08 — for loose images the answer is clearly one image per asset, but
    a PBR set (basecolour + normal + roughness) is one asset with three files,
    and both have to be expressible.

    --- (B) APPROVAL IS PER VERSION, SO "PASS SOME, FAIL OTHERS" HAS NOWHERE TO LAND ---

    THE INFORMATION IS ALREADY THERE. The technical check knows which files
    failed: files_not_empty lists the empty ones BY NAME, and the content check
    collects blocked, contradicted and unverifiable file lists. Seven checks, all
    stored per run in technical_check_results with a message and details.

    WHAT IS MISSING IS THE VERDICT. There is no per-file status anywhere, and
    DELETE FROM workspace_version_files appears NOWHERE IN THE ROUTES — a file
    cannot be removed from a version once uploaded. So "resubmit the whole
    directory, or continue with the ones that passed" cannot be expressed: to
    continue with a subset you would need a NEW VERSION containing only those
    files, or a per-file model that does not exist.

    AND creative_approvals POINTS AT workspace_version_id. Approval is of a whole
    version. Per-file rejection is not a UI problem; it is a data-model problem,
    and it is the larger of the two here.

    --- (C) STEPS 9 AND 10 — THE IMPORT PATH HAS NEVER BEEN WRITTEN ---

    DOWNLOADING FROM A WORKSPACE WORKS. Driven and verified: the bytes served
    hash to the blob's own name.

    IMPORTING DOES NOT EXIST. workspace_assets.repository_asset_id and
    repository_base_version_id are in the schema and HAVE NEVER BEEN WRITTEN by
    anything — they are only ever SELECTed, in the approval queue. So a developer
    cannot pick an image out of the store and pull it into a workspace, which is
    the whole of steps 9 and 10 and the missing half of "pick up your own approved
    work and continue". See the note around line 1236 in this file, which records
    the same gap from the other direction.

    WHAT DOES ALREADY HOLD is the part that matters most: if magicTree_v01
    contains a file whose bytes are already in the store, NO SECOND COPY IS MADE.
    UNIQUE(sha256) and one blob per SHA is the heart of the store. The content is
    referenced, never duplicated. The path to BUILD such an asset is what is
    missing, not the guarantee.

    --- AND THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION, ANSWERED FROM THE MANUAL RATHER THAN MEMORY ---

    ASKED: at step 4, when the passed images are submitted for approval, is that
    where the developer ceases to own them?

    NO. TWO DIFFERENT MOMENTS, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS:

      * AT SUBMISSION the version AND THE ASSET are LOCKED — "so what a reviewer
        looks at cannot change underneath them. While it is pending you cannot
        change or delete the asset, and you cannot re-run the check." Locked, but
        still the developer's.

      * AT APPROVAL ownership passes — "Your version is moved into the game's
        asset store… OWNERSHIP PASSES TO THE GAME AT THIS POINT — but you can
        always download a copy."

    Which matches what was said when the model was set: "if the version is
    accepted the developer ceases to own the directory/files but can download a
    copy to work on if they want."

    AND NOTHING REACHES THE STORE BEFORE APPROVAL. The approve route is what does
    INSERT INTO assets / asset_versions / asset_version_files and links the
    workspace version back with repository_version_id. Before that the store holds
    nothing of the developer's.

    --- WHAT THIS ADDS UP TO ---

    THE PIPELINE EXISTS FROM UPLOAD THROUGH TO THE STORE. What does not exist is
    everything that treats an image as an INDIVIDUAL THING: per-file verdicts,
    per-file approval, tags, thumbnails, content search, and pulling store content
    back into a workspace.

    OF THOSE, TWO ARE DECISIONS AND THE REST IS WORK:

      * (A) needs the asset-identity question settled — one image per asset, a
        folder per asset, or both. Raised 2026-10-08 and still open.
      * (B) needs a decision about whether approval can be partial at all. If a
        file cannot be approved on its own, the answer to step 3 is "resubmit a
        corrected version", which is simpler and may be right — but it should be
        chosen rather than assumed.

    AND NONE OF IT IS GAME-DEPENDENT, which is why this is not in the AFTER THE
    GAME EXISTS section. Thumbnails, tags, per-file verdicts and the import path
    can all be built before there is a game to put anything in.


55) ARCHIVES — UNZIP ON UPLOAD. Decided 2026-10-08, NOT BUILT.

    So a developer does not have to push gigabytes through a browser. The case is
    real and already on this machine: source_assets holds a 3.3G zip beside two
    loose photographs.

    --- THE TWO DECISIONS TAKEN ---

      * NOTHING IS KEPT. The archive is inflated and then DELETED. No zip is
        stored in the workspace, the store, or anywhere else — it is transport,
        exactly like the directory name in item 54, and keeping it would double
        the bytes on disk for content already stored.

        THE HISTORY TRAIL STILL RECORDS IT. "Submitted as wales_photos.zip, 412
        files" is what documents what was presented, which is what the history is
        for. The bytes go; the record stays.

      * THE ARCHIVE'S NAME NAMES NOTHING, same as a folder name. See item 54,
        finding (A): what a batch is called is asked for at the LABELLING step,
        not at upload, and it is a collection label rather than an identity.

    --- UNZIP IS A FEEDER, NOT A SECOND UPLOAD PATH ---

    THE PIPELINE CAN BE REUSED RATHER THAN DUPLICATED. The upload handler works
    from a list of file paths plus their names (tempPaths.map(f => f.path), with
    originalname driving naming). So extraction writes into the existing temp
    directory and then hands the files to THE SAME upload logic — and every
    existing check applies unchanged: content-versus-extension, zero bytes,
    executables, backup files, the store write, deduplication, the history entry.

    BUILDING A SECOND PATH INSTEAD is how two upload pipelines end up
    disagreeing about what is acceptable, which is the thing shared/ exists to
    prevent for the checks themselves.

    --- AND THIS IS THE RISKIEST CODE IN THE UPLOAD AREA, SO SAY WHY ---

    Extraction WRITES FILES WHOSE NAMES CAME FROM OUTSIDE. Everything else in the
    upload path works on files a browser already put somewhere with a name the
    server chose to accept. This takes a name from inside an attacker-controlled
    archive and turns it into a path. The classic failure is called ZIP SLIP and
    it has a name because it is common:

      * PATH TRAVERSAL. An entry named ../../../etc/passwd, or an absolute path,
        or ..\..\ with Windows separators. Join it onto a directory and write,
        and the write lands outside it.
      * SYMLINKS. An entry that IS a symlink, followed by a second entry that
        writes THROUGH it — which defeats the naive "is the joined path inside
        the target directory?" check, because it is.
      * ZIP BOMBS. A few kilobytes that expand to terabytes, whether nested
        archives or one hugely compressed file. Also thousands of tiny entries,
        which exhaust the INODE TABLE — and this store's inode headroom is
        already something the Storage tab watches (item 38/48).
      * NAMES THAT BREAK THE EXISTING RULES. classifyFolderName, isBackupFile and
        the structural-character rules were written for names a browser sends. An
        archive can contain anything, including names that break the asset
        naming rules or collide with each other.

    READ THE CENTRAL DIRECTORY FIRST, BEFORE WRITING A SINGLE BYTE. A zip
    declares the uncompressed size of every entry up front, which buys three
    things at once:

      1. CHECK THE WORKSPACE QUOTA FIRST, from the declared sizes, and refuse
         before extracting — rather than extracting 2 GB and then discovering it
         does not fit. (Against a 5 GiB quota this matters; see item 51 for the
         allowance.)
      2. REFUSE ON COMPRESSION RATIO. The bomb defence.
      3. REFUSE ON ENTRY COUNT. The inode defence.

    THEN: extract into the temp directory, and REBUILD EVERY PATH RATHER THAN
    TRUSTING IT — reject absolute paths, reject any segment that is '..', reject
    symlinks outright rather than following them, normalise separators. Then hand
    the results to the existing upload logic.

    DECLARED SIZES CAN LIE, which is why the quota check cannot rest on them
    alone. Recommended: check the declared total (cheap, catches accidents) AND
    stop the extraction when the bytes actually written exceed the limit (cannot
    be lied to). Both, not either.

    --- NOT DECIDED ---

      * WHICH FORMATS? Zip only, or tar.gz and others? Zip is the one a person
        produces by right-clicking a folder, which is the case here. Others widen
        the attack surface for no obvious gain.
      * THE TRANSIENT DISK SPIKE. A 3.3 GB zip plus its inflated contents is
        roughly 7 GB at peak. Fine on the volume this runs on, but it is the
        number that decides whether the quota check must also require free space
        before it starts.
      * ANYTHING AN ARCHIVE CONTAINS MUST NOT BE ABLE TO RUN — the standing rule
        in item 35. Extraction is the one new place where an untrusted name
        decides a path, so it is the place to be most careful about it, not the
        place to relax.

    --- AND IT DEPENDS ON ITEM 54 ---

    Extraction only makes sense once it is settled what an uploaded set of files
    BECOMES. Item 54's finding (A) settled the naming half on 2026-10-08; the
    other half — whether loose images are one asset each, or a set is one asset —
    is still open, and it decides whether an archive inflates to one asset or
    many.


56) WHAT KIND OF UPLOAD IS THIS? — the type decides TWO things. 2026-10-08,
    NOT BUILT.

    Raised while working through the "uselessName containing 50 images, 10 in a
    subdirectory called meansNothing" case. The user's words: *"for other
    directory structure like a maya blender or nuke project the directory
    structure must be preserved otherwise the app will not be able to find the
    assets they need — so maybe selecting the correct type is very important —
    and should be made very clear."*

    --- IT DECIDES STRUCTURE, AND IT ALSO DECIDES GRANULARITY ---

        AN APPLICATION PROJECT   Nuke, Blender, Maya, Unreal, Houdini…
                                 THE TREE IS LOAD-BEARING. The app resolves its
                                 assets by path, so flattening breaks it. ONE
                                 asset — the project — with its structure intact.

        AN ARBITRARY COLLECTION  files, possibly in directories that may or may
                                 not mean anything. Structure is convenience, so
                                 it collapses to LABELS. MANY files, each its own
                                 thing and individually checkable — AND THEY LAND
                                 IN THE IMAGE LIBRARY, NOT THE STORE (item 57).
                                 Creative approval is not their gate.

    So the type is not only "do we keep the tree" — it is the answer to what one
    asset IS, which is the half of item 54 that has been open since the image
    pipeline run-through.

    AND WITHIN A COLLECTION THERE IS A SECOND QUESTION: do the directory names
    mean anything? Keep them as labels, rename them, or leave blank. From the
    same conversation: the images then sit in ONE FLAT SPACE, grouped by labels
    that were the directory names — or by nothing.

    --- THE CONSEQUENCE HAS TO BE SAID OUT LOUD, BECAUSE GETTING IT WRONG IS INVISIBLE ---

    Flatten a Nuke project and EVERY FILE IS STILL THERE. Nothing errors at
    upload. The project simply cannot find its assets later, and by then the tree
    is gone. So the prompt has to say what happens, not just offer a choice — a
    dev who does not know what a Nuke script does with paths has no way to choose
    well.

    --- THE SUGGESTION IS ALREADY FREE ---

    ASSET_TYPE_EXTENSIONS in shared/assetRules.mjs maps APPLICATION to
    extensions — Unreal, Unity, Blender, Maya, 3ds Max, Cinema 4D, Houdini,
    ZBrush, Substance, NUKE, Modo, LightWave — and assetTypeOf() ALREADY RUNS PER
    FILE AT UPLOAD. The asset_types_recognised check reports what it found:
    "N file(s) recognised: Nuke, image."

    So "this contains 3 .nk files — is this a Nuke project?" is answerable today
    with no new machinery. THE SUGGESTION IS FREE; ONLY THE ASK AND THE
    CONSEQUENCE NEED BUILDING. Which is the pattern this project prefers anyway:
    suggest from what the machine can read, let a human confirm, and never make
    them restate something the file already says.

    --- AND THIS IS NOT THE REMOVED asset_type FIELD, WHICH MATTERS ---

    The old asset_type asked the developer to choose between Concept, Texture,
    Model, Animation, Audio — and also Maya, Blender, Nuke, Unreal. It was REMOVED
    deliberately: see the note in this file, "asking a human to restate something
    the machine can read is how a field becomes wrong". A .blend IS Blender.

    THIS ASKS A DIFFERENT QUESTION AT A DIFFERENT LEVEL:

        old asset_type   which tool made THIS FILE     answerable from the
                                                       extension
        this             what is THIS UPLOAD           NOT answerable — 50 JPGs
                                                       are 50 JPGs
        old consequence  a wrong label                 this consequence  A PROJECT
                                                       THAT NO LONGER OPENS

    RECORDED EXPLICITLY so that somebody finding the removal note does not
    "restore" it. They are not the same field and the second is not a reversal of
    the first.

    --- WHAT IT TOUCHES ---

      * relative_path IS LOAD-BEARING TODAY. The upload stores it, the download
        manifest rebuilds `${relative_path}/${filename}`, and logical_paths_unique
        stops two files claiming the same path. So a project upload needs all of
        that; a collection upload discards it once the labels are taken.
      * THE ASSET COUNT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TWO, which reaches the technical
        check, the approval step, and the store write.
      * AND IT IS WHY THE UNZIP QUESTION (item 55) cannot be answered until this
        is: an inflated archive becomes ONE asset or MANY depending on the answer.


57) THE IMAGE LIBRARY — a THIRD SPACE. BUILT 2026-10-09. 2026-10-08.

    ADDS TO 54 AND 56, and CORRECTS BOTH. The user's words: *"Images cannot go
    into the game without being part of an asset but they can go into the image
    library in the source assets."*

    --- WHAT THAT CHANGES ---

        image   →   THE IMAGE LIBRARY (in source assets)      the studio
        asset   →   THE GAME STORE                            the game

    AN IMAGE IS GAME CONTENT ONLY AS PART OF AN ASSET. It is not approved INTO
    the game on its own — it is USED BY something that is. A texture, a
    reference, a photograph: none of them belong in the store standing alone.

    So item 54's run-through described the wrong pipeline for images. What it
    verified — upload, check, approve, store — is the ASSET pipeline, and it was
    driven end to end and works. The IMAGE pipeline is different and shorter:
    upload, check, LIBRARY. Creative approval is not its gate, because nothing
    about an image in a library "ships".

    AND IT SIMPLIFIES WHAT WAS OPEN. Item 56 asked whether loose images are one
    asset each or a set is one asset. NEITHER: they are not assets at all. They
    are library items, and the asset is something a developer BUILDS, possibly
    containing them.

    --- AND THE LIBRARY DOES NOT EXIST ---

    CHECKED, not assumed: the word "library" appears in the portal only in
    comments about modules being libraries. THERE IS NO LIBRARY SPACE, no table,
    no route, no page. The portal models exactly two spaces — the private
    workspace and the game store — and this is the third one, which has been
    noticed twice now (once while working out where candidate images live, again
    here) without being named.

    SO THE THIRD SPACE IS NAMED: THE IMAGE LIBRARY, holding source material in
    source_assets/, visible to the studio, from which developers draw.

    --- WHAT IT NEEDS, AND WHAT OF IT IS ALREADY DECIDED ---

      * STORAGE. Content-addressed like everything else, so an image that later
        becomes part of an asset is THE SAME BLOB — nothing is copied when it is
        used, and using it twice costs nothing. The one-copy rule already covers
        this; only the bookkeeping is new.

      * A GATE, AND PROBABLY A LIGHT ONE. A library image should still be a real
        file of the type it claims — the tech check already does that and is not
        creative. WHETHER ANYTHING ELSE IS REQUIRED TO ENTER THE LIBRARY IS NOT
        DECIDED.

      * LABELLING (item 54, step 6), THUMBNAILS (step 7) AND CONTENT SEARCH
        (step 8) are all LIBRARY concerns, not store concerns. That is what they
        were always for: you search a library for material, you do not search a
        finished build.

      * THE COLLECTION NAME from item 54's finding (A) is a LIBRARY concept too —
        retrieval as a batch is a library job.

      * AND PULLING AN IMAGE INTO A WORKSPACE IS NOW AN IMPORT FROM THE LIBRARY,
        which is NOT the same as the import already in the schema.
        workspace_assets.repository_asset_id references assets — THE GAME STORE —
        so the schema's one import path is store→workspace. Library→workspace has
        NO schema support at all, and it is the path steps 9 and 10 need.

    --- OPEN ---

      * IS THE LIBRARY SHARED, OR PER-DEVELOPER? DECIDED 2026-10-09: SHARED,
        STUDIO-WIDE. One library; everyone browses it and draws from it. The
        simpler table AND the simpler permissions, and it matches how
        source_assets is described. (The alternative — per-developer, or a
        private tier promoted later — would have meant the same image sitting in
        two libraries, and "the library" ceasing to be one thing to browse.)

      * WHAT MAY LIVE IN IT? DECIDED 2026-10-09: ANY FILE, WITH AN IMAGE-FIRST
        UI. The table is the same either way and the technical check already
        verifies file types (item 34), so allowing video, audio and PDFs costs
        nothing now — and reference material for an open-world project is not
        only pictures. THE PAGE LEADS WITH IMAGES, because thumbnails, labelling
        and content search only mean anything for images.

        WHICH MEANS THE NAME IS SLIGHTLY WRONG AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. "The image
        library" is what it is called and what it is FOR first; it is not a
        restriction on what it may hold. Recorded because a name describing the
        first use is how a second use gets refused.
      * HOW DOES AN IMAGE GET INTO AN ASSET? The developer pulls it into their
        workspace and builds with it, and the asset carries a REFERENCE — so the
        asset's file set includes a blob that also lives in the library. That is
        what content addressing is for, and it means an asset can contain
        library content without owning a copy. Worth confirming when built.
      * AND THE QUESTION THE WHOLE THING TURNS ON: DOES AN ASSET THAT CONTAINS A
        LIBRARY IMAGE OWE ANYTHING TO THE LIBRARY? By the one-copy rule, no —
        the blob is shared and neither owns it. But that does mean deleting from
        the library cannot remove a blob an asset depends on, which is the same
        guarantee that protects the game store. Worth stating so nobody
        "tidies up" the library and breaks a build.


58) IMAGES ARE NOT GAME ASSETS — A STANDING RULE, not a task. 2026-10-08.

    AN IMAGE NEVER GOES INTO THE GAME ON ITS OWN. It goes into the IMAGE LIBRARY,
    and it is in the game only by being PART OF something that ships.

    Stated by the user as the thing to make unmistakable: *"images are not game
    assets — they are components in a library"*.

        image   →   the image library (in source assets)    shared    does not ship
        asset   →   the game store                          the game  ships

    THIS IS NOT A STEP IN A PIPELINE, IT IS A RULE ABOUT WHAT THINGS ARE. An
    image is a component. An asset is a unit of game content a developer
    assembles, which may USE components, and which is checked, reviewed and
    approved into the store. Neither is a stage of the other.

    --- AND THE WORD "ASSET" IS OVERLOADED, WHICH IS HOW THIS GOT MUDDLED ---

    An "asset" in this portal means a unit of game content a developer assembles
    and submits. That is close to, and not the same as, what an engine means by
    it. A texture file can be a UTexture inside Unreal and still be a LIBRARY
    COMPONENT here until it is part of something that ships.

    The run-through in item 54 walked straight into this: it sent images through
    approval and into the store, and described the ASSET pipeline while calling
    it the image one. The pipeline it verified is real and it works — it is just
    not the pipeline images travel.

    --- WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT ---

      * CREATIVE APPROVAL IS NOT AN IMAGE'S GATE. Nothing about an image in a
        library "ships", so there is nothing for creative approval to be about.
        Images are checked — a real file of the type it claims — and filed.

      * LABELLING, THUMBNAILS AND CONTENT SEARCH ARE LIBRARY CONCERNS. You
        search a library for material; you do not search a finished build. Those
        three steps of the item 54 run-through relocate, and so does the
        COLLECTION NAME from finding (A) — retrieval as a batch is a library job.

      * AND THE ONE-COPY RULE MAKES THIS ALMOST FREE. An asset that uses a
        library image REFERENCES the same blob rather than copying it, so using
        an image costs nothing on disk and using it twice costs nothing twice.
        Which also means: DELETING FROM THE LIBRARY CANNOT REMOVE A BLOB AN ASSET
        DEPENDS ON. The same protection the game store already has. Recorded
        because the failure is a tidying-up that breaks a build.

        CONFIRMED BY THE USER, 2026-10-08: *"an asset using a library image
        references the same blob — NO DUPLICATION ANYWHERE"*.

        AND IT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN A CONVENTION, checked rather than
        assumed:

          * stored_files HAS sha256 UNIQUE AND storage_key UNIQUE. One row per
            SHA, and the blob on disk is named for that key. THERE IS NO SECOND
            PLACE A COPY COULD GO.

          * EXACTLY TWO THINGS REFERENCE A BLOB TODAY —
            workspace_version_files.stored_file_id and
            asset_version_files.stored_file_id, both foreign keys into
            stored_files. A library is a THIRD REFERENCER OF THE SAME TABLE, the
            same shape as the other two.

          * THE UPLOAD LOOKS CONTENT UP BY SHA FIRST and writes only when it
            finds nothing, so re-submitting known content adds a reference and
            no bytes.

        So the library cannot introduce duplication without changing
        stored_files, and that is the single place the rule lives. Which is why
        adding a library is additive: it is another row pointing at content that
        already has exactly one row.

    --- WRITTEN DOWN IN THREE PLACES, BECAUSE IT KEEPS BEING ASSUMED WRONG ---

      * THIS RULE — the decision, and why the word is overloaded.
      * ITEM 57 — the library itself: a third space, which does not exist yet.
      * THE MANUAL, docs/manual/40-developing.md — "An image is not a game
        asset", the second section on the page, because a developer needs it
        BEFORE their first upload rather than afterwards.

    The manual section exists because the older wording — "a staging area for
    game assets… approved work goes into the game's asset store" — implied that
    everything uploaded becomes a game asset, which is exactly the thing that is
    not true.


59) THE ROUND TRIP — check out, adjust, check in. AND THE HASH NEGOTIATION.
    2026-10-09.

    HOW CHANGE HAPPENS, and the user's correction that made the model smaller:
    *"if it is worked on - adjusted etc - that will be done by the dev checking
    it out - downloading those pieces on their local machine which don't already
    exist and then uploading it again"*.

    SO THE GAME DOES NOT TRANSFORM ANYTHING. Adjustment is WORK, work happens in
    a workspace, so adjustment is A NEW VERSION. There is no fifth layer: the
    four of item 53 stand — library, workspace, store, releases — plus THE
    DEVELOPER'S MACHINE, which is a real place content lives and NOT a layer we
    model, because we only ever learn what is on it by being told.

    --- THE LOOP IS NOT SYMMETRICAL, AND THAT IS THE FINDING ---

    CHECK-IN WORKS, and it preserves identity. developerWorkspace.js:4258 —
    "If this workspace asset was explicitly derived from an existing repository
    asset, use it." So an approval on a linked workspace asset adds a NEW VERSION
    to that store asset rather than making a new one.

    CHECK-OUT DOES NOT EXIST. workspace_assets.repository_asset_id is written in
    EXACTLY ONE PLACE — the approve route, :4374. The upload route never sets it.
    So the column means "this BECAME that", recorded after the fact: it is
    ANCESTRY, not DERIVATION. Nothing anywhere pulls store content into a
    workspace.

    WHICH PRODUCES A LIVE FAILURE. A developer who adjusts a store asset and
    re-uploads it fresh: the upload makes a NEW unlinked workspace asset;
    approval looks for a store asset of the same name (:4319), finds one, and
    THROWS — "A repository asset with this name already exists, but this
    workspace asset is not associated with it" (:4323). And assets.name is
    UNIQUE, so this is guaranteed for any name already in the store.

    So the loop only closes FOR THE DEVELOPER WHO ORIGINALLY MADE IT, while their
    workspace asset survives — their re-upload lands on the linked asset. Anyone
    else, which is the normal case, has no way in and a hard stop on the way out.
    That is the gap, and everything else here depends on closing it.

    --- STAGING FIRST, DECIDED ---

    Check-out STAGES INTO THE WORKSPACE, and the developer views it and confirms
    before anything reaches their machine. The user's words: *"it stages first
    into the workspace where the dev can view and check they want to proceed with
    downloading to the local machine"*.

    Three jobs at once:

      * they SEE THE FILE LISTING, sizes and types BEFORE pulling gigabytes;
      * the workspace asset is created WITH ITS ANCESTRY RECORDED — which is the
        link the eventual upload needs, and therefore REPAIRS THE FAILURE ABOVE;
      * nothing is downloaded if they decline.

    So staging is not a nicety on top of the round trip. IT IS THE MISSING HALF
    OF IT.

    DOES A STAGED CHECK-OUT COUNT AGAINST THE WORKSPACE LIMIT? NO, AND THAT IS
    ALREADY THE RULE. The user's principle: the quota is *"only the things that
    are still owned by the dev - and not shared"*. Studio-owned content is not
    the developer's to be charged for.

    AND IT IS ALREADY IMPLEMENTED, in the precise form. workspaceUsage.js:93:

        AND wvf.stored_file_id NOT IN (
            SELECT stored_file_id FROM asset_version_files
        )

    THE EXCLUSION IS BY BLOB, NOT BY LINK — a stored file appearing in any store
    version is not charged, whatever workspace asset points at it. Which gives
    more than was asked for:

      * A FULL CHECK-OUT COUNTS ZERO. Every blob is already in the store. A 3 GB
        project stages against a 5 GB quota and the quota does not move.

      * AN ADJUSTED CHECK-OUT COUNTS ONLY WHAT WAS ADDED. The untouched 3 GB
        stays free; the one changed texture exists nowhere else, so it counts.
        That is the quota measuring material that exists BECAUSE OF THIS
        DEVELOPER, and it is taken as the reading.

      * AND THE EXISTING DESIGN ALREADY ANSWERED THE HARD CASE: two developers
        independently uploading identical bytes are BOTH charged, because that is
        not sharing — neither can see the other's work — it is deduplication.
        Consistent with the rule above, and the one place "shared" could be read
        two ways, so the existing reading stands.

    --- AND THE ONE GAP, WHICH IS A TRAP ---

    THE LIBRARY DOES NOT EXIST, SO LIBRARY BLOBS ARE NOT EXCLUDED. When it is
    built, item 57's material MUST JOIN THAT `NOT IN` — otherwise pulling library
    images into a workspace charges quota for STUDIO-OWNED CONTENT, the precise
    opposite of the rule.

    AND IT WILL GO WRONG RATHER THAN BE CAUGHT, because workspaceUsage.js:35 says:

        "The GAME STORE is the only shared content in the system, which is why
         it is the only thing the exclusion above is about."

    True today. FALSE THE DAY THE LIBRARY EXISTS. That sentence is an instruction
    to the next person, and it currently instructs them to leave the exclusion
    alone. Whoever builds item 57 must change it in the same breath.

    --- HASH NEGOTIATION — the piece that makes the round trip affordable ---

    THE USER ASKED FOR THIS EXPLICITLY. The dev downloads only *"those pieces on
    their local machine which don't already exist"*, and the same idea applies in
    both directions.

    THE READ SIDE ALREADY EXISTS. GET /assets/:id/versions/:id/manifest and
    .../files/:fileId/content. WHAT IS MISSING IS THE NEGOTIATION: the server
    does not know what is on the developer's disk, so THE CLIENT MUST SAY SO.

        THE MANIFEST IS THE INTERFACE, AND HASHES ARE THE CURRENCY.

      * CHECK-OUT: the server offers the version's manifest — every file with its
        relative_path and its SHA-256 — and the client pulls only the blobs it
        does not already hold. It already knows, because it can hash its own
        files.

      * CHECK-IN: the client announces the hashes it HAS; the server asks for
        only the blobs it LACKS. WITHOUT THIS, adjusting one texture in a 3 GB
        Nuke project re-uploads 3 GB: the store dedupes the STORAGE so nothing is
        wasted on disk, but every byte crosses the wire again.

    This is the exchange git and rsync already use, and it falls out of content
    addressing for free. NOT a new concept — a negotiation on top of routes that
    exist.

    AND IT IS WHAT MAKES ITEM 55 (unzip on upload) MATTER LESS: the reason to
    send an archive is to avoid sending many bytes, and negotiation is the better
    answer to the same problem.

    --- BRANCHING, DECIDED ---

    An adjustment BRANCHES: it produces a NEW STORE ASSET, not a new version of
    the one it came from. The user's decision — *"I think it should branch"* —
    and the argument is THE ASYMMETRY OF THE FAILURES:

      * version-in-place gone wrong — "bigTree" SILENTLY STOPS MEANING what it
        meant, and somebody else's reference or a release query quietly picks up
        a different thing. SILENT.

      * branching gone wrong — an extra asset nobody uses. NOISE.

    When one failure is silent and the other is merely untidy, take the loud one.

    Two more reasons, both about the store being SHARED:

      * TWO PEOPLE ADJUSTING THE SAME SOURCE IN DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS IS NORMAL,
        and in-place versioning forces v4/v5 as though 5 succeeded 4. They are
        SIBLINGS. That is a lie stored in the data, and unfixable later.

      * NAMES ARE HOW PEOPLE REFER TO THINGS. A store where bigTree means one
        fixed thing is usable; one where it drifts is not. Immutability is what
        makes "approved" mean anything at all.

    AND BRANCHING NEEDS NO NEW CONCEPT — IT FIXES THE DIRECTION OF ONE ALREADY
    THERE. repository_asset_id is written only at approval today, so it means
    "became". Under branching it is written at CHECK-OUT and means "came from",
    which is what a column with that name on a WORKSPACE asset obviously ought to
    mean.

    BUT IT FORCES PROVENANCE. A branch must record that it came from bigTree v3 or
    the thread is lost. And it must be MANY-TO-MANY, because both directions are
    real:

        one source  → several branches     bigTree v3 → mossyTree, deadTree
        several     → one asset            tree + hut + fence → village

    So a TABLE, not a column — the same reasoning item 53 used to reject a
    `recommended_version` field on the asset.

    --- OPEN ---

      * DOES EVERY ROUND TRIP BRANCH? A one-character fix would then make a new
        asset. The suggestion is that THE DEV CHOOSES — update in place, or
        branch — DEFAULTING TO BRANCH for the failure-mode reason above. NOT
        SETTLED.

      * AND VERSIONING CONTINUES WITHIN A BRANCH, so the two coexist: branch at
        the asset level, versions within. What decides which one a given change
        is, is the question above.

      * WHERE THE STAGED VERSION GOES when the dev uploads an adjusted one: does
        it supersede the staging, or sit beside it? Not decided.


60) GAME VERSIONS ON THE DEVELOPER PAGE — IMPORTANT. 2026-10-09.

    The user's words: *"we should put the latest game versions to download on the
    devs page - TODO - Important"*.

    WHY IT MATTERS, from the scenario that produced it: *"most devs will have a
    copy of the game - inprogress dev whatever - and they will upload their asset
    into the game to see how it works before uploading it to the website"*.

    SO THE PORTAL HONOURS A WORKFLOW THAT ALREADY HAPPENS. Developers test in a
    local game before submitting. The portal's pipeline therefore BEGINS AFTER
    LOCAL VALIDATION — tech check and creative approval are about STANDARDS AND
    OWNERSHIP, not about whether the thing works. The developer already knows.

    DEPENDS ON ITEM 53. A downloadable game version IS a release — or rather a
    RELEASE CANDIDATE, which is the thing actually wanted; see below — so this
    cannot be built before releases exist.

    CORRECTED 2026-10-09: it said this is "the DISTRIBUTION half of item 53",
    WHICH IS WRONG. The portal does not distribute game versions (items 62, 67).
    Item 53 defines what a release IS; WHAT REACHES A DEVELOPER'S DISK IS
    PERFORCE'S OR THE BUILD MACHINE'S JOB, and the portal's half is the RECORD.

    --- AND WHAT IS ACTUALLY DOWNLOADED IS A RELEASE CANDIDATE ---

    CORRECTED THE SAME DAY, on the user's instruction: *"This should be a
    download of a pre-release candidate for shipping"*. So the thing on the page
    is not "the game" and not "a build" — IT IS A RELEASE CANDIDATE, AND SAYING
    SO REMOVES THE VAGUENESS THAT MADE THIS ITEM UNFINISHABLE:

      * it has a NAME AND A NUMBER — 0.4-rc2 — so it can be referred to at all;
      * it is A SPECIFIC CHANGELIST, so it is reproducible;
      * it is what a tester is ASKED to try, which is why testers exist;
      * AND IT IS THE THING THAT BECOMES THE RELEASE, or is discarded.

    THE CHAIN IS CANDIDATE -> TESTED -> RELEASED, and the portal records all
    three states.

    --- AND IT IS A KIND OF RELEASE — SETTLED 2026-10-09, WITH A WRINKLE ---

    The user: *"I think it is a kind of release - what do game studios sy"*. THE
    INDUSTRY AGREES. THE LADDER, AS STUDIOS ACTUALLY USE IT:

        first playable -> vertical slice -> ALPHA -> code freeze -> BETA
        -> RELEASE CANDIDATE -> GOLD -> [CERTIFICATION] -> launch -> patches

    ALPHA: features functional, content rough, still buggy.
    BETA: FEATURE COMPLETE — all content in, only bug fixing remains. Closed or
    open.
    RELEASE CANDIDATE: the near-final build, ONLY CRITICAL FIXES ALLOWED.
    GOLD / "going gold": the final approved build, handed over to manufacture.
    CERTIFICATION: for console, the platform holder (Sony, Microsoft, Nintendo)
    approves it against technical compliance rules — IT CAN FAIL AND A FAILED
    SUBMISSION RESETS THE CLOCK. Most titles go through at least two rounds.

    SO A CANDIDATE SITS EXACTLY WHERE THE USER PUT IT: the last state a release
    is in before it becomes the release.

    --- AND THE WRINKLE, WHICH DECIDES THE SCHEMA ---

    STUDIOS CUT RC1, RC2, RC3, AND THE ONE THAT PASSES BECOMES GOLD. A version
    routinely has SEVERAL candidates; the failed ones are DISCARDED, not shipped.
    "Going gold" is PROMOTING ONE OF THEM.

    WHICH MEANS AN EARLIER INSTINCT IN THIS ITEM WAS TOO QUICK. It said "one
    record with a status, because it is the same build either way" — and if
    0.4-rc1 is rejected and 0.4-rc2 is cut, THAT IS TWO BUILDS AND ONE RECORD
    CANNOT BE BOTH.

    THE CLEAN ANSWER, AND IT NEEDS NO SECOND TABLE: THE CHANGELIST IS THE
    IDENTITY OF A BUILD. So one table, with the stage as a label:

        NAME        CHANGELIST    STAGE
        0.4-rc1     15320         candidate
        0.4-rc2     15407         candidate
        0.4         15407         shipped

    0.4-rc2 AND 0.4 ARE THE SAME BUILD — SAME CHANGELIST — AT TWO STAGES. So
    "which candidate became the release" is a CHANGELIST MATCH rather than a
    column, and a rejected candidate is simply LEFT AT candidate FOREVER, which
    is a truthful thing to display rather than a gap.

    AND IT FITS EVERYTHING ALREADY DECIDED: the boundary is a changelist number
    (item 62), and item 53's "membership derived by query" is the same style.

    --- WHAT IT NEEDS ---

      * ACCESS: developers and above. Not users. A game build is studio material.

      * AND IT MUST BE A RELEASE, NOT A WORKING COPY. A developer's local game has
        their own unapproved work in it. Handing out builds must hand out CLEAN
        ones, or one person's half-finished experiment lands in another person's
        test and confuses everybody. This is the single most important constraint
        here.

      * WHICH ONES? "The latest" is what was asked for. The open question is
        whether older releases stay available — a developer testing against the
        wrong version needs the right one, and pinning to a version is the normal
        reason to want an old one. Probably: keep all named releases, offer the
        latest prominently.

      * SIZE. Game builds are enormous. CORRECTED 2026-10-09: this said they are
        "a NEW kind of content in the portal — the store holds assets, this holds
        BUILDS", WHICH THE REST OF THIS ITEM CONTRADICTS eight paragraphs later,
        where it concludes the portal does not store or serve them at all. IT WAS
        RIGHT THE SECOND TIME. The portal holds NO build content, so there is no
        disk to fill and nothing to de-duplicate — what it holds is a RECORD, and
        a record is bytes of no consequence.

        (Kept as a correction because it is a live example of the failure this
        file keeps naming: two sentences written at different moments, each true
        of what it was describing, together arguing with each other.)

    --- AND HOW BIG, AND HOW IT GETS THERE. ANSWERED 2026-10-09. ---

    ASKED: *"So how big is a normal openworld game? Once the game has been
    downloaded once - it only needs the differences - both plus and minus - so
    the local machine does not fill up with useless stuff... should we make a
    script that the dev can run on their local machine? Or what else can you
    suggest"*.

    SIZES — THREE TIERS, AND ONLY THE FIRST IS A PUBLISHED FIGURE:

        SHIPPED GAME          60-120 GB     Elden Ring ~60, Cyberpunk ~70-80
                                           (108 with expansions), RDR2 ~120
                                           (150 to install). Cooked, compressed.
        PACKAGED DEV BUILD    ~150-250 GB   debug symbols, less compression.
                                           ESTIMATE, not a searched figure.
        THE FULL PROJECT      300 GB-1 TB+  source assets, PSDs, FBX, DDC,
                                           Intermediate, Binaries. WHY NOBODY
                                           SYNCS THE WHOLE THING.

    SO THE FIRST QUESTION IS WHICH ONE A DEVELOPER NEEDS, and it differs: an
    artist testing an asset wants to run it, a tester wants a build.

    THE DELTA INSTINCT IS RIGHT, AND CONTENT ADDRESSING MAKES IT BETTER THAN
    EXPECTED:

      * DIFFERENCES BOTH WAYS, and THE MINUS MATTERS MORE THAN PEOPLE THINK.
        Leaving stale files behind breaks the game in ways that look like code
        bugs.

      * AND RENAMES ARE FREE. A file that moved has the same SHA, so it is a
        RELINK, not a download. Only changed CONTENT costs bandwidth — and an
        enormous share of change between builds is files moving.

    --- BUT DO NOT WRITE THE CLIENT. THREE REASONS. ---

      1. IT ALREADY EXISTS, TWICE. "We know what the dev has, send only the
         differences, plus and minus, don't leave junk" IS A LITERAL DESCRIPTION
         OF `p4 sync`. For build artifacts it is a literal description of
         `rsync --delete`.

      2. A TOOL THAT DELETES FILES IS THE MOST DANGEROUS THING TO WRITE. "Delete
         everything not in the manifest" eats a developer's work the first time
         the manifest is wrong, and Perforce and rsync have had decades of bugs
         shaken out of exactly that logic.

      3. THE PORTAL IS NOT A FILE SERVER. Node and Express serving 100 GB has no
         range requests, no resumability and no CDN. Works in testing, falls over
         in use.

    --- WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, IN ORDER ---

      1. PERFORCE FOR THE PROJECT (item 62). p4 sync gives deltas, deletions,
         resume, verification and locking, and there is NOTHING TO BUILD. The
         developer page links to a CHANGELIST; the portal never touches the bytes.

      2. `rsync -a --delete` FOR PACKAGED BUILDS. If builds sit on a server with
         SSH, that ONE COMMAND is the entire delta mechanism — incremental,
         resumable, deletes what is gone. Almost free to adopt.

      3. UNREAL'S OWN PATCH PAKS if small GAME patches are wanted rather than
         whole builds. Packaging in Patch mode produces paks containing only
         changed assets. THE ENGINE HAS ALREADY SOLVED DELTA FOR COOKED BUILDS.

      4. A SCRIPT, IF ONE IS WANTED, IS A WRAPPER AROUND `p4` OR `rsync` AND
         NOTHING MORE. CORRECTED 2026-10-09: it said "ask the portal for the
         manifest, then invoke p4 or rsync", AND THE PORTAL HAS NO MANIFEST TO
         GIVE. A manifest of build contents is a thing PERFORCE ALREADY KNOWS,
         because a build IS a changelist; a second copy from the portal would be
         the duplication item 67 forbids. (The MANIFEST is real and belongs to
         the ASSET store — item 59's hash negotiation — which is a different
         thing entirely, and the word doing double duty here is what made this
         read as sensible.)

    --- AND ONE CORRECTION TO THE FRAMING ---

    "IF WE KNOW WHAT VERSION THE DEV HAS ALREADY DOWNLOADED" — DO NOT TRACK THAT
    SERVER-SIDE. A client's state is a bad thing to mirror: they reimage a
    machine, work on two machines, delete a folder — and the server's belief is
    silently wrong, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHEN A DELETE-EVERYTHING-NOT-IN-THE-MANIFEST
    TOOL DOES DAMAGE.

    ASK THE CLIENT INSTEAD. It hashes what it has and says so; the server replies
    with what is missing. Self-correcting, no state to drift, and it is THE SAME
    HASH NEGOTIATION ALREADY DESIGNED FOR CHECK-OUT (item 59). Perforce does
    this, rsync does this, git does this — and it works because THE CLIENT IS THE
    AUTHORITY ON THE CLIENT.

    SO ITEM 60 CHANGES SHAPE: THE DEVELOPER PAGE LISTS AND LINKS RELEASE
    CANDIDATES AND RELEASES. It does not store or serve them. THE PORTAL KEEPS
    THE RECORD — which changelist, who built it, when, and what state it is in —
    and the transfer is somebody else's solved problem.

    (CORRECTED 2026-10-09: this said the portal keeps "the MANIFEST AND THE
    RECORD ... what changed". The RECORD is right; THE MANIFEST IS NOT. What
    changed between two builds is what a changelist already is, and a portal copy
    of it would be a second answer that drifts. Item 67.)

      * AND WHO PRODUCES A RELEASE CANDIDATE? CORRECTED 2026-10-09: it asked "WHO
        UPLOADS A BUILD? Somebody has to build the game and put it somewhere",
        WHICH PRESUMES THE PORTAL RECEIVES IT. NOBODY UPLOADS A BUILD TO THE
        PORTAL. The BUILD MACHINE produces it and something serves it; the portal
        records that it exists and who may fetch it. WHO RUNS THE BUILD MACHINE
        is still NOT DECIDED — see item 53.

    --- AND ONE THING THAT IS MISSING WHATEVER IS DECIDED HERE ---

    NOTHING RECORDS WHICH GAME VERSION AN ASSET WAS TESTED AGAINST.

    A developer validates against a specific build, submits, and the fact is
    lost. If the asset is later used in a different version, nobody knows it was
    only ever seen against the first. That is "IT WORKED ON MY MACHINE" with no
    way to check it, and the fix is small: ONE FIELD ON THE SUBMISSION — the game
    version it was tested against — offered from the same list this item puts on
    the developer's page.

    Cheap now, impossible to reconstruct later.


61) ALL AUTHORING HAPPENS OUTSIDE THE ENGINE. 2026-10-09.

    A PREMISE, not a task. Stated by the user when asked how much artist work
    happens in Unreal: *"all done outside and then imported"*.

    So the engine is a CONSUMER, not an authoring space. Work is made in Blender,
    Maya, Nuke and the like, and imported. Nothing is authored in Unreal that has
    no source outside it.

    WHAT IT SETTLES:

      * THE STORE IS AUTHORITATIVE. The source and its tree is the real thing, and
        the engine's copy is derived from it. Provenance runs one way.

      * AND THE EXPORT-BACK PROBLEM DISAPPEARS. There is nothing to export back,
        because nothing was authored there. The earlier worry — that engine-side
        work would be stranded with no route to its source — does not arise,
        PROVIDED THIS PREMISE HOLDS.

      * SO THE PORTAL RECORDS THE STORE->GAME LINK AND NOTHING MORE. Modelling the
        engine's internals here would duplicate what it already knows and drift.
        The link is worth keeping precisely because the engine's own import
        metadata is local, absolute-path, and dies with the machine.

    ASKED AND NOT YET ANSWERED: DOES THIS INCLUDE THE WORLD ITSELF? Level layout,
    lighting, foliage placement and set dressing are normally engine-side work in
    an open-world project, and are not "imported" in the way a mesh is. If this
    premise covers them, the pipeline is entirely one-way and unusually clean. If
    it does not, the engine holds authored work with no source in the store, and
    that work needs its own provenance — the game repository's history, not ours.

    --- CORRECTED THE SAME DAY, BECAUSE THE PREMISE IS TOO STRONG ---

    THE USER'S COUNTEREXAMPLE, put to me directly: *"if I am working on my local
    machine I have bigTree originally created in blender - and the wip game - and
    I import bigTree and make changes to big tree in the game - for instance - i
    apply some matrix transform - and then want to export it back - how should
    this be done"*.

    SO "ALL DONE OUTSIDE" IS TRUE OF CREATION AND NOT OF ADJUSTMENT. Things are
    MADE outside. They are also CHANGED inside, and that change needs a route
    back to the source.

    AND THERE ARE TWO CHANGES BEHIND ONE PHRASE, with opposite answers:

      * A - PLACEMENT. The tree is moved in the world and the ASSET is untouched.
        What changed is the LEVEL. There is no asset round trip: it is layout, it
        is world data, and the store is not involved. The engine would export a
        placement list — which asset, which matrix — and the DCC would consume
        that.

      * B - BAKED. The transform is applied INTO the mesh, so the asset HAS
        changed and the source is stale.

      AND B IS THE LUCKY ONE: A PURE TRANSFORM IS THE ONLY ASSET CHANGE THAT
      ROUND-TRIPS PERFECTLY, because there is nothing to lose — no materials, no
      UV authoring, no modifier stack, no procedural history. It is geometry.
      FBX carries it faithfully both ways.

      FOR ANYTHING THAT IS NOT A TRANSFORM — retopology, a repainted material,
      edited UVs — EXPORT-BACK IS LOSSY AND THE CHANGE SHOULD BE MADE IN THE DCC.
      Not a rule the portal imposes: the engine's representation cannot express
      what was done in a form the DCC accepts back.

      AND THE THIRD POSSIBILITY WORTH RULING OUT: a matrix applied to fix an
      IMPORT problem (Blender and Unreal disagreeing about forward axis) belongs
      in the EXPORT SETTINGS, applied consistently — not in the engine, and not
      in a round trip. It is the most common reason a matrix appears there.

    WHAT THE PORTAL DOES, IN ALL OF IT: NOT THE EXPORT. Unreal and the DCC do
    that, and the portal must not parse a .uasset. THE PORTAL RECORDS THAT A NEW
    SOURCE ARRIVED AND WHICH STORE VERSION IT BRANCHED FROM — item 59's loop,
    unchanged. The engine is simply another place a change can originate.

    --- AND THE NORMAL WAY, WHICH SETTLES IT ---

    ASKED: *"what is the normal way - It must be a common problem"*. IT IS COMMON,
    AND THE INDUSTRY ANSWER IS THAT IT IS NOT ROUND-TRIPPED AT ALL.

    THE STANDARD PIPELINE IS ONE-WAY: DCC -> export -> engine, and the DCC FILE IS
    THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. When the source changes you export again and hit
    REIMPORT in Unreal, which re-runs the import with the original settings.

    WHICH EXPLAINS UNREAL'S IMPORT METADATA. The source path and timestamp exist
    PRECISELY to support Reimport, and they work perfectly well for one artist on
    one machine. They are only useless as a STUDIO-WIDE RECORD — a different job,
    which is the one we were asking of them.

    SO THE RULE IS: THE ENGINE IS DOWNSTREAM. Asset changes are made in the DCC
    and flow one way. Level layout, lighting and set dressing are ENGINE work and
    STAY THERE. You do not edit an asset in the engine and carry it back.

    AND THE MATRIX CASE IS ALMOST ALWAYS AN AXIS OR SCALE PROBLEM — Blender and
    Unreal disagree about forward axis — whose normal fix is A SETTING APPLIED
    ONCE, in the FBX export and Unreal import options, so nothing ever needs
    correcting. A matrix applied by hand to every import is the SYMPTOM; the cure
    is upstream, in the export settings.

    --- AND THE ROUND TRIP ALREADY EXISTS: IT IS PERFORCE ---

    IN A NORMAL STUDIO bigtree_v01.blend LIVES IN PERFORCE. The artist checks it
    out, edits it in Blender, checks it in, and reimports in Unreal. THE ENGINE
    NEVER ROUND-TRIPS — THE SOURCE ROUND-TRIPS THROUGH VERSION CONTROL.

    WHICH IS EXACTLY ITEM 59'S LOOP. The store plays Perforce's role: check out,
    edit, check in, branch. The same shape, because it is the same problem, solved
    decades ago by keeping the source in a depot and reimporting.

    SO THERE IS NOTHING TO INVENT. The user's own instinct — check it out, adjust,
    upload it again — IS the normal way. The only question is WHERE the adjustment
    happens: IN THE DCC.

    AND GENUINE ENGINE->DCC ROUND TRIPS ARE SPECIFIC TOOLS, NOT A GENERAL
    CAPABILITY: Houdini Engine (procedural, live both ways), USD (increasingly the
    direction of travel), Datasmith (scene interchange, mostly CAD inward), and
    scene/instance export for LAYOUT. All exceptions. None is what a texture or a
    tree does.

    --- WHICH MEANS ITEM 61'S PREMISE SURVIVES ---

    "All done outside and then imported" is right about ASSETS, and the counter-
    example does not overturn it: the normal way is to make the change in
    the DCC and reimport, not to edit in the engine and export back. The premise
    is too strong only in that it does not SAY the engine can be edited — it can,
    and the answer is that you should not need to.

    OPEN: whether case A (placement) belongs to the store at all, or is purely
    world data the portal never sees. The reading is the latter.


62) THE GAME PROJECT AND VERSION CONTROL — A SCOPE QUESTION. 2026-10-09.

    RAISED BY THE USER: *"I think that the lighting etc are done in Unreal - and
    that the unreal game can be downloaded worked on - say the lighting and then
    the lighting update uploaded to the dev portal - what do you think? Is
    perforce free? should we be using it?"*

    SO THE PREMISE OF ITEM 61 IS NOW SETTLED: lighting and the world ARE authored
    in the engine. Item 61 already carries the correction.

    BUT WHAT IS DESCRIBED IS NOT THE ASSET STORE. IT IS VERSION CONTROL FOR THE
    GAME PROJECT, which is a different system with a different tool.

    --- WHY THEY ARE DIFFERENT ---

                     THE STORE                    THE GAME PROJECT
        churn        on submission                all day, every day
        contents     curated approved sources     a huge tree of binary .uasset
        conflicts    rare, one dev one asset      constant, two artists one level
        needs        approval, provenance,        LOCKING, branching, merging,
                     one-copy                     deltas

    AND THE PROPERTY THAT DECIDES IT IS LOCKING. Two people editing one level is
    not a merge conflict to resolve, it is CORRUPTION — you need "I have this
    checked out, nobody else may touch it". That is Perforce's core feature, and
    it is exactly what a CONTENT-ADDRESSED store cannot give, because content
    addressing is MERGE-BY-CONTENT and Unreal's binaries do not merge at all.

    --- RECOMMENDATION ---

      * THE PORTAL RECORDS RELEASES AND DOES NOT DISTRIBUTE THEM. It does NOT
        version the project either. Item 60 puts release candidates and releases
        on the developer page AS RECORDS; item 53 defines what a release is.
        Growing the portal into a VCS means building locking, branching, binary
        deltas and histories — badly, and against a tool that has done it for
        twenty years.

        (CORRECTED 2026-10-09: this said the portal "DISTRIBUTES GAME VERSIONS",
        which the very next bullet and item 60 both contradict.)

      * THE BOUNDARY IS A CHANGELIST NUMBER. A release records which Perforce
        changelist it was built from. The portal knows THAT a build exists and
        where it came from; Perforce knows how it got there.

      * AND IT IS THE "NORMAL WAY" ANSWER ONE LEVEL UP. The source round-trips
        through version control and the engine reimports — item 61. This is the
        same sentence about the PROJECT: the project round-trips through version
        control, and the portal records which build came out.

    --- PERFORCE, ANSWERED ---

    FREE AT THIS SIZE. Helix Core is free for small teams — UP TO 5 USERS AND 20
    WORKSPACES — and paid above that. Checked 2026-10-09 against Perforce's own
    pages; licensing changes, so it is worth rechecking before committing.

    LICENCES ARE PER NAMED USER, NOT PER IP OR PER CONNECTION. Asked directly —
    *"are the perforce licenses per ip address?"* — and the answer is no. Five
    standard accounts is five accounts wherever those people are, which is what
    makes it work for a remote team at all.

      * A STANDARD USER — can sign in, make a workspace, submit — CONSUMES ONE.
      * OPERATOR USERS and SERVICE USERS DO NOT CONSUME LICENCES, but NEITHER CAN
        DO FILE WORK, so neither can be a build machine. See below.
      * YOUR OWN ADMIN ACCOUNT COUNTS — unless it is made an OPERATOR user, which
        is the one economy this licence model offers.

    AND WHAT THE TWO FREE TYPES ACTUALLY ARE, since it was asked:

      * OPERATOR USER — for ADMINISTERING THE SERVER, human or automated. No
        licence. Restricted to a fixed command list: admin stop/restart/
        checkpoint, counter, dbstat, dbverify, diskspace, configure, info,
        journal*, login, log*, lockstat, monitor, passwd, ping, serverid, verify,
        user. NOTABLY ABSENT: sync, submit, edit, add — AN OPERATOR CANNOT TOUCH
        FILES AT ALL. Because the list is that narrow, giving it `super` is
        considered safe.

      * SERVICE USER — for ONE SERVER TALKING TO ANOTHER: remote depots,
        replicas, multi-server setups. No licence. It exists so each installed
        service has its own identity, which makes logs readable and forces remote
        services to hold valid tickets. Not a human, not a client.

      * IN ONE LINE: OPERATOR ADMINISTERS THE SERVER; SERVICE IS SERVER-TO-SERVER.
        NEITHER DOES DEVELOPMENT, AND NEITHER CAN BE A BUILD MACHINE.

    AND THE ONE PLACE THE OPERATOR TYPE SAVES MONEY, which Perforce says plainly:
    organizations whose admins do not use versioning features may economise via
    this type. SO IF WHOEVER RUNS THE SERVER ONLY ADMINISTERS IT AND NEVER SYNCS
    OR SUBMITS, MAKE THEM AN OPERATOR AND THEY STOP CONSUMING A LICENCE.

    AND THE USER LIMIT IS PROBABLY NOT THE ONE THAT BITES — THE SECOND ONE IS.
    20 WORKSPACES, AND WORKSPACES MULTIPLY WHERE USERS DO NOT: every machine a
    person uses is another workspace. A developer with a laptop and a desktop is
    TWO. Five people is easily ten before any build machine.

    AND THE BUILD MACHINE TAKES FROM BOTH POTS — A WORKSPACE AND A STANDARD USER
    ACCOUNT. IT CANNOT BE AN OPERATOR OR A SERVICE USER: it syncs a changelist
    and runs commands, which is FILE WORK, and both free types are barred from
    file work. (An earlier version of this note said otherwise. It was wrong.)

    SO FIVE STANDARD USERS REALISTICALLY MEANS FOUR HUMANS PLUS THE BUILD
    MACHINE — or five humans if the admin is an operator and no build machine
    sits on the server. SIX IS WHERE THE FREE TIER ENDS.

    IF YOU GO OVER, YOU ARE NOT CUT OFF: the unlicensed server CAPS THE DEPOT AT
    1,000 FILES rather than refusing to run. Which for a game project is a hard
    stop in practice, since a thousand files is nothing. The free tier is real
    only while inside 5 users and 20 workspaces.

    AND IT IS BUILT INTO UNREAL: the editor's source-control menu speaks Perforce
    natively — check out, check in, history, reconcile — with no plugin. For an
    Unreal project that is a strong argument by itself.

    THE ALTERNATIVE: git with git-LFS, which is already in use here and free
    without user limits. Small studios do run Unreal on it, but it has NO REAL
    FILE LOCKING and Unreal binaries in git are painful at scale.

    --- AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY LOOKS LIKE, ASKED AND ANSWERED ---

    ASKED: *"if all the developers are working remotely they would each have
    their own copy of perforce on their local machine - and the studio would also
    have a copy?"*

    NOT QUITE, AND THE MENTAL MODEL IS GIT'S. PERFORCE IS CENTRALISED: there is
    exactly ONE authoritative copy — the depot, on the server. Developers do not
    have copies of it. They have WORKSPACES: a mapping saying which parts of the
    depot appear where on their disk, plus the files they have synced and their
    own edits. NO HISTORY, NO BRANCHES — just files.

    AND A WORKSPACE SYNCS A SUBSET. An artist syncs the art folders, a programmer
    the source, and nobody syncs 200 GB of textures to edit a level. Native to
    Perforce, and a large part of why studios use it over git for a project.

    --- TWO OPERATIONAL FACTS THIS TURNS UP, BOTH ASSUMED BY ITEM 60 ---

      * WHERE THE SERVER LIVES. NOT IN THE STUDIO. If it is a machine in the back
        room, the studio's internet connection becomes every developer's
        bottleneck and a single point of failure for the company. FOR A REMOTE
        TEAM THE SERVER WANTS TO BE CLOUD OR DATACENTRE HOSTED — Perforce sells
        this, or it runs on a VPS. Developers work over the internet and CAN work
        offline, but SUBMITTING REQUIRES THE SERVER, because the server arbitrates.

      * AND A BUILD MACHINE, WHICH ITEM 60 DEPENDS ON AND DOES NOT MENTION.
        Somewhere there is a machine with its own workspace which syncs a known
        changelist and builds the game. THAT IS WHAT PRODUCES THE RELEASES on the
        developer page. Not a developer's copy, not the server — and without it
        "download the latest game version" has nothing to download.

    --- AND THE BIT THAT TIES BACK ---

    LOCKING REQUIRES A CENTRAL AUTHORITY. You cannot have it with distributed
    copies agreeing among themselves, which is why git is bad at it and Perforce
    is built around a server. THE CENTRALISATION IS NOT AN IMPLEMENTATION DETAIL
    — IT IS THE FEATURE.

    WHICH IS ALSO WHY THE PORTAL CANNOT BE THE ANSWER HERE. The portal is
    content-addressed, and content addressing is MERGE-BY-CONTENT: no arbiter, no
    lock. Adding one means building the thing Perforce already is.

    --- IF WE GO WITH PERFORCE, THESE ARE THE ACTIONS — ASKED FOR 2026-10-09 ---

    The user asked whether the ACCOUNTS WE NEED and the fact that this has to be
    INSTALLED AND CONFIGURED IN UNREAL are written down. They were not, so:

      [ ] DECIDE ONE SYSTEM OR TWO (below). Nothing here happens until that is
          answered.

      [ ] A SERVER, HOSTED OFF-SITE. Not a machine in the studio: for a remote
          team, on-prem makes the studio's uplink everyone's bottleneck and a
          single point of failure. Perforce's own cloud, or a VPS.

      [ ] THE ACCOUNTS, AND THE COUNT MATTERS. Five standard users on the free
          tier:
            * ONE PER DEVELOPER who does file work.
            * THE BUILD MACHINE — a STANDARD user, unavoidably. It syncs a
              changelist and builds, which is file work, and the two free types
              are barred from file work.
            * THE ADMIN — a standard user, UNLESS whoever runs the server only
              administers it and never syncs or submits, in which case make them
              an OPERATOR user and they consume no licence. This is the one
              economy the licence model offers and it is worth taking.
            * NOBODY needs a service user unless we run replicas or remote
              depots. Do not create one "just in case" — it is for
              server-to-server and it buys nothing here.

      [ ] AND WATCH THE WORKSPACES, NOT THE PEOPLE. The second limit is 20, and
          workspaces multiply per MACHINE per person — a developer with a laptop
          and a desktop is two. The build machine needs one. Five people is
          easily ten before anything unusual happens.

      [ ] CONFIGURE SOURCE CONTROL IN UNREAL. The editor's source-control menu
          speaks Perforce natively — set the server, the user, and the workspace
          in Project Settings > Source Control, with no plugin. Also configure
          the file types that must be checkout-exclusive (binaries and .uasset),
          because that locking is the entire reason for choosing Perforce.

      [ ] AND TEST IT ON THE .uproject BEFORE TRUSTING IT. The first commit is
          the project's own structure — game/ as it stands in this repository —
          with .gitignore-style rules deciding what does NOT go in (Binaries/,
          Intermediate/, Saved/, DerivedDataCache/).

    --- DECIDED 2026-10-09: TWO SYSTEMS. ---

    RECORDED ON THE USER'S INSTRUCTION — *"lets put this into the TODO list using
    the two systems"* — so this is no longer the recommendation it was written
    as. It is the pattern.

        THE PORTAL: login and accounts; THE ASSET STORE — upload, tech check,
        creative approval, the image library; provenance for assets; and the
        RELEASE RECORD — a name, a Perforce changelist, who built it, when, and
        whether it is a candidate or shipped.

        PERFORCE: the game project. Levels, lighting, world data, the Unreal
        tree, source control, locking, branching, and getting the project to a
        developer. A BUILD MACHINE syncs a changelist and builds it.

        THE BOUNDARY IS A CHANGELIST NUMBER. The portal records THAT a build
        exists and where it came from. IT DOES NOT STORE, SERVE, VERSION OR
        TRANSFER A GAME BUILD, AND IT DOES NOT VERSION THE GAME PROJECT.

    AND ONE STANDING TEST, which the user gave in the same discussion and which
    everything from here has to pass (item 65):

        DO NOT DUPLICATE OR BATTLE AN EXISTING MECHANISM. DIVERGE ONLY FOR A
        REASON THAT IS REQUESTED AND DISCUSSED.

    --- AND THE WHOLE FILE WAS SWEPT AGAINST IT THE SAME DAY ---

    Every item was read against this decision, looking for anything that had the
    portal storing, serving, versioning or transferring a build, tracking
    developer-side state, gating access to the game, or duplicating Perforce or
    Unreal. FINDINGS WERE CORRECTED IN PLACE, each marked as a correction with
    the date so the wrong version is visible rather than silently overwritten:

        * item 25 — browse the game store, "particularly IMAGES" — images are
          never in the store; they are in the LIBRARY (items 57, 58).
        * item 26 — leak prevention — the portal does not distribute builds, so
          that design is not its to make; what remains open is whether the portal
          BROKERS ACCESS to a build it does not serve.
        * item 31 — a stale bullet naming superuser and calling deploying and
          branching portal capabilities. Both halves were wrong.
        * item 51b — two claims that superuser still existed in the schema, when
          it was removed entirely on 2026-10-06.
        * the tester page and the manual, in four places — "download a build",
          now a RELEASE CANDIDATE.
        * item 53 — "a git tag" as a candidate release identifier, when the game
          is versioned in Perforce.
        * item 60 — four separate claims that the portal stores, distributes or
          holds a manifest of builds, all contradicted by that item's own
          conclusion.
        * and the storage-by-game-version arithmetic, which claimed per-version
          figures would NOT sum to the total. TRUE OF THE STORE, FALSE OF BUILDS,
          and it said so about builds.

    THE SWEEP ALSO FOUND TWO THINGS UNRELATED TO THIS DECISION and left them
    corrected in place: item 25's images (above), and item 51b's superuser
    (above). Both were stale before today.


63) HOW AN OPEN WORLD IS DIVIDED, AND WHAT A DEVELOPER ACTUALLY SYNCS. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"what are openworld games generally divided into - levels - but is
    their finer grained chunks than that - where they can see how their big tree
    works with the lighting and fx etc"*.

    YES, AND ON THREE DIFFERENT AXES — which is why it is not simply "levels".

      * SPATIAL — WORLD PARTITION CELLS. The world is ONE PERSISTENT LEVEL FILE,
        subdivided into a streamable grid (256 m typical, 768 m loading range).
        Actors with "Is Spatially Loaded" set load and unload by distance from a
        streaming source. You never load the world, you load a neighbourhood.

      * SEMANTIC — DATA LAYERS. THIS IS THE DIRECT ANSWER TO THE QUESTION. Data
        Layers group actors LOGICALLY, independently of space, and load or unload
        as a unit. They are what studios use for LIGHTING, FX, GAMEPLAY,
        TIME-OF-DAY, seasonal variants, quest states, destroyed-versus-intact
        towns, cinematic-only dressing. "How does my tree look with the lighting
        and FX" IS WHAT DATA LAYERS ARE FOR.

      * ATOMIC — ONE FILE PER ACTOR (OFPA). Each actor INSTANCE is its own file.
        Move one tree and ONE FILE changes, not the level. The finest grain there
        is, and on by default with World Partition.

    --- AND OFPA EXISTS BECAUSE OF SOURCE CONTROL ---

    Epic's documentation says it plainly: OFPA means editing an actor no longer
    requires saving the main Level file, "REDUCING SOURCE-CONTROL CONFLICTS" — and
    Perforce changelist support can show actor names, level paths and asset types.

    THE ENGINE WAS DESIGNED THIS WAY TO MAKE VERSION CONTROL WORK. An independent
    argument for item 62: with Perforce we are not fighting the tooling, we are
    using it as intended.

    CAVEAT: OFPA IS EDITOR-ONLY. At cook time all actors are collapsed into the
    level files. It describes the DEVELOPMENT structure; the shipped build is a
    different shape. Worth remembering when discussing builds.

    --- SO A DEVELOPER'S WORKING SET IS NOT "THE GAME" ---

      * their own content — Content/Developers/<name>/
      * THE DATA LAYER their work belongs to — lighting, if they are lighting
      * a REGION OF CELLS, not the world
      * plus, in practice, A LOOK-DEV MAP: a small level with representative
        lighting and a slice of terrain, where an asset can be judged IN CONTEXT
        without syncing a terabyte. This is the pragmatic studio answer and
        probably how the artists already work.

    --- WHAT IT CHANGES FOR US ---

      * ITEM 60'S SHAPE SHIFTS AGAIN. "Download the latest game version" is THE
        WRONG SHAPE FOR AN ARTIST, because nobody wants all of it. An artist
        working on a tree wants to SYNC A SUBSET AND RUN THE EDITOR — which is
        Perforce. A PACKAGED BUILD is for a tester who just wants to run it. TWO
        AUDIENCES, TWO MECHANISMS, and the developer page should say which is
        which rather than offering one "the game" button.

      * AND ITEM 61'S A/B DISTINCTION IS NOW CONCRETE. The tree ASSET is a store
        asset. The PLACED tree is an OFPA file inside a cell. Different kinds of
        thing, in different systems — now with a file behind it.

      * AND THE PARTIAL SYNC IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT IS THE NORMAL CASE.
        Perforce workspaces map subsets by design (item 62), which is exactly
        what an open-world project needs and what a whole-repository clone cannot
        do.


64) WHAT A DEVELOPER DECLARES, AND WHAT THE SYSTEM ALREADY KNOWS. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"there also needs to be on the devs workspace a form asking what
    spatial area of the game - maybe including new area - lighting including new
    lighting - etc does this fit with the usual way of doing things? ... Does the
    dev manually enter the version or chunk of the game they are building on or
    what?"*

    --- THE VERSION QUESTION, ANSWERED FIRST AND FIRMLY ---

    NO, AND THE DEVELOPER SHOULD NOT BE ASKED. THE CLIENT ALREADY KNOWS. A
    Perforce workspace records the last-synced revision of every file, so "what
    am I building on" is STATE, not something anybody types. A typed version is
    wrong from the moment anything syncs, and somebody will trust it.

    AND THE ONE VERSION-SHAPED QUESTION THAT MUST BE TYPED IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF
    THING (item 60):

        WHAT I SYNCED FROM            the client, per file   NEVER typed — state
        WHICH GAME VERSION I TESTED   only the human         ALWAYS typed —
        AGAINST                                              an assertion

    THE SECOND HAS TO BE ASKED BECAUSE THE PORTAL CANNOT OBSERVE A TEST THAT
    HAPPENED ON THE DEVELOPER'S MACHINE. RULE: STATE IS NEVER TYPED, ASSERTIONS
    ALWAYS ARE.

    --- DOES THE AREA/LIGHTING FORM FIT THE USUAL WAY? PARTLY. ---

    THE AREA AND THE LAYER ARE REAL, BUT NOT AS A PER-TASK FORM. In the usual
    way they are expressed as:

      * THE WORKSPACE MAPPING — the depot paths that appear on your disk. A
        lighting artist's workspace holds the lighting Data Layer and the
        relevant level paths. SET ONCE, EDITED RARELY. A config, not a form.

      * THE STREAM — Perforce's branch concept — which is what "what line of
        development am I on" actually means. CHOSEN WHEN THE WORKSPACE IS MADE.

    AND "INCLUDING NEW AREA" A FORM CANNOT DO. A new area in Unreal means NEW
    DATA LAYERS AND LEVEL INSTANCES, CREATED IN THE EDITOR. A web form cannot
    make them and must not pretend to. It could record that somebody is STARTING
    one; that record is a note, not a fact.

    --- THE COORDINATION NEED IS REAL, AND THE SYSTEM ALREADY ANSWERS IT ---

    KNOWING WHO IS WORKING ON WHAT IS A GENUINE NEED for a small remote studio.
    The usual way to express it is not a form:

      * PERFORCE JOBS — a lightweight issue tracker built into Helix Core.
        Changelists attach to jobs, so "I am on JOB-1234, North Forest lighting"
        lives in the same system and the history attaches to it.

      * AND WITH ONE FILE PER ACTOR, PERFORCE ALREADY KNOWS WHO HAS WHAT CHECKED
        OUT — PER ACTOR. Not per level, per OBJECT. A developer who has checked
        out the lighting actors in the North Forest cells is VISIBLE IN THE
        SERVER'S STATE without anybody typing anything.

    SO THE BETTER SHAPE IS A VIEW, NOT A FORM: show who is on what, DERIVED FROM
    Perforce — streams, jobs and file locks. A DECLARATION CAN BE FORGOTTEN,
    MISTYPED OR STALE; THE CHECKED-OUT STATE CANNOT.

    --- WHAT ACTUALLY BELONGS IN THE PORTAL ---

      1. A DECLARED FOCUS, EXPLICITLY AS A LABEL — "working on: North Forest
         lighting". Cheap, useful for a small team, HARMLESS PROVIDED IT IS
         UNDERSTOOD AS A NOTE AND NOT A SOURCE OF TRUTH. Ideally it POINTS AT a
         Perforce job rather than restating it.

      2. AND FOR A SUBMISSION: THE INTENDED AREA OR DATA LAYER AS CATALOGUE
         METADATA. DIFFERENT AND GENUINELY USEFUL — "show me every asset intended
         for the North Forest" is a search both a reviewer and a future artist
         want. THE PORTAL ASKS THIS ABOUT THE ASSET BEING SUBMITTED, NOT ABOUT THE
         DEVELOPER'S WORK SESSION. That distinction is the whole of it.

    --- AND THE HAZARD, WORTH NAMING ---

    A SECOND, DRIFTING RECORD OF WHO IS DOING WHAT. If the portal says "North
    Forest" and Perforce says otherwise, SOMEBODY WILL TRUST THE WRONG ONE — and
    the portal's copy is the one that goes stale SILENTLY, because nobody updates
    it after a change of plan. WHICH IS THE SAME FAILURE SHAPE AS EVERY OTHER
    DUPLICATED FACT IN THIS FILE.


65) GETTING A PIECE OF THE GAME TO WORK ON — NO FORM, AND WHY. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"alternatives to filling out a form to getting a piece of the game to
    work on - what is normally done"*, with the standing instruction attached:
    *"don't wnat to duplicate or battle against existing mechnaisms or practices
    - only diverge for a good reason that must be requested and discussed"*.
    THAT INSTRUCTION IS NOW THE TEST ANY FEATURE IN THIS FILE HAS TO PASS.

    --- THE ANSWER: IT IS TWO OPERATIONS, AND NEITHER IS A FORM ---

      * GETTING THE FILES — YOU SYNC THEM. p4 sync against the workspace mapping,
        or against specific paths. In P4V, right-click > Get Latest or Get
        Revision. THE STREAM chooses the branch. NO REQUEST, NO APPROVAL, NO FORM
        — syncing a path is unrestricted within your permissions.

      * GETTING THE PLACE IN THE WORLD — YOU LOAD IT IN THE EDITOR, using what
        item 63 already set out: the World Partition window (load/unload regions),
        LOCATION VOLUMES (named regions loaded as a unit), DATA LAYERS (toggle
        lighting, FX, gameplay), LEVEL INSTANCES (open one in isolation), and the
        LOOK-DEV MAP.

      * AND COORDINATION IS PERFORCE JOBS PLUS FILE LOCKING — and with ONE FILE
        PER ACTOR the locking is per OBJECT, so two people rarely collide at all.

    THE NEAREST THING TO A FORM ANYWHERE IN THAT LIST IS CREATING A WORKSPACE,
    AND THAT IS A ONE-TIME CONFIG, NOT A PER-TASK REQUEST.

    --- WHY A FORM HERE WOULD BE BOTH A DUPLICATE AND A BATTLE ---

    A FORM WOULD INSERT A PERMISSION STEP IN FRONT OF SOMETHING THAT IS CURRENTLY
    FREE. Today a developer syncs a path and gets on with it. If the portal
    required a form to obtain a piece of the game, every developer would have to
    BOTH fill in the form AND sync from Perforce — the portal becomes a gate that
    duplicates a decision Perforce already lets them make, and a bottleneck the
    moment anybody is in a hurry.

    THAT IS THE "BATTLE" HALF. IT DOES NOT MERELY DUPLICATE THE MECHANISM, IT
    SLOWS DOWN THE THING IT DUPLICATES.

    AND THE DIVERGENCE WOULD NOT BE JUSTIFIED: there is no problem here that
    Perforce does not already solve. That is the test the user's instruction sets,
    and this fails it.

    --- WHAT THE PORTAL CAN DO INSTEAD: POINT AT IT ---

      * A RECIPE — "the North Forest lighting is these four depot paths".
        Documentation, not a mechanism.

      * A GENERATED WORKSPACE SPEC a developer can copy into P4V. A helper for a
        config they would otherwise hand-write: saves keystrokes without becoming
        the gate.

      * THE LIST OF STREAMS AND RELEASES — item 60's page. A LISTING, not a
        request.

    NONE OF THOSE CAN DENY ANYBODY ANYTHING, WHICH IS THE PROPERTY THAT MAKES THEM
    SAFE.

    --- AND WHERE A FORM IS RIGHT ---

    THE SUBMISSION. Not because the portal grants anything, but because a
    submission carries ASSERTIONS THE SYSTEM CANNOT OBSERVE — what the asset is
    for, which game version it was tested against (item 64). Those have to be
    typed, and typing them is the whole point.

    THE DISTINCTION, IN ONE LINE: THE PORTAL ASKS ABOUT THINGS ONLY A HUMAN
    KNOWS, AND NEVER GATES THINGS THE TOOLS ALREADY ALLOW.


66) DETERMINING WHICH FILES A DEVELOPER NEEDS. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"exactly how do we determine what files the dev needs to download to
    work on their bit of the game"*.

    --- 1. THE MECHANISM IS THE WORKSPACE MAPPING ---

    A FILTER listing which depot paths appear on disk. For one region:

        //depot/LowExpectations/Config/...                 engine/project settings
        //depot/LowExpectations/LowExpectations.uproject
        //depot/LowExpectations/Plugins/...
        //depot/LowExpectations/Source/...                 if built from source
        //depot/LowExpectations/Content/_Core/...
        //depot/LowExpectations/Content/Art/...            shared art they use
        //depot/LowExpectations/Content/World/<region>/... the region they work in
        //depot/LowExpectations/Content/Developers/<name>/... their own

        -//depot/.../Content/Developers/*                  everyone else's
        -//depot/.../Content/World/*                       regions they don't touch
        -//depot/.../Binaries/...
        -//depot/.../Intermediate/...
        -//depot/.../Saved/...
        -//depot/.../DerivedDataCache/...

    THE EXCLUSIONS MATTER AS MUCH AS THE INCLUSIONS. The generated directories are
    what make a workspace enormous and they are rebuilt locally.

    AND HOW IT IS DETERMINED: IT IS NOT COMPUTED. A PERSON DECIDES IT, ONCE, PER
    ROLE — "the art mapping", "the lighting mapping". A new starter is handed the
    template for their discipline and extends it as they find gaps. THE FIRST
    VERSION IS A GUESS AND IT CONVERGES. That is the normal practice and there is
    no cleverer one in general use.

    --- 2. AND THE GOTCHA ---

    THE SYNC FILTER AND THE LOAD FILTER HAVE TO AGREE. You can sync one region and
    still open the whole world in the editor, and everything outside the mapping
    appears as MISSING-ASSET PLACEHOLDERS. Which is another reason Location
    Volumes and Data Layers matter (item 63): they let you DELIBERATELY load a
    region instead of accidentally loading the world.

    --- 3. AND UE5 HAS MOVED THE ANSWER: VIRTUAL ASSETS ---

    SINCE 5.1, Unreal SPLITS every .uasset and .umap in two:

      * STRUCTURED DATA — small, stays in source control, syncs normally.
      * BULK DATA — the large payload, moved to a SEPARATE PERFORCE LOCATION,
        referenced BY HASH, and PULLED ON DEMAND when the editor needs it.

    SO THE DEVELOPER SYNCS LIGHTWEIGHT METADATA AND THE PAYLOAD ARRIVES WHEN THEY
    OPEN SOMETHING. THERE IS NO "DETERMINE WHICH FILES" STEP AT ALL — the editor
    pulls what it loads.

    AND NOTE THE SYMMETRY: BULK DATA IS DE-DUPLICATED BY HASH, WHICH IS THE SAME
    ONE-COPY RULE THE PORTAL ALREADY RUNS ON.

    CAVEATS, AND THEY ARE REAL:
      * BETA, and one Epic forum thread reports the initiative is NO LONGER IN
        ACTIVE DEVELOPMENT. Weigh before making it load-bearing.
      * REQUIRES PERFORCE and a SHARED DERIVED DATA CACHE.
      * BULK PAYLOADS ARE NOT AUTOMATICALLY PRUNED — changing data submits a new
        payload and leaves the old one. Pruning needs a database mapping .uasset
        revisions to payload references, WHICH NOBODY HAS.
      * Epic recommends a SEPARATE DEPOT for virtualized data so nobody syncs it
        by accident.

    --- WHAT THE PORTAL DOES ABOUT IT: NOTHING THAT DETERMINES THE SET ---

    PERFORCE ANSWERS IT WITH THE MAPPING; UNREAL ANSWERS IT WITH VIRTUAL ASSETS.
    A PORTAL-COMPUTED FILE LIST WOULD BE A THIRD ANSWER THAT DRIFTS FROM BOTH.

    THE MOST IT CAN DO WITHOUT DUPLICATING ANYTHING IS PUBLISH THE TEMPLATES — a
    recipe per discipline. Item 65's rule: POINT AT IT, DO NOT GATE IT.

    AND ONE THING WORTH REMEMBERING FOR ITEM 62: the licence count and the
    workspace count now have a second dimension. If bulk payloads live in a
    separate depot, that is more files in the depot — and the free tier's
    1,000-file cap applies to the depot as a whole.


67) HOW THE PORTAL AND PERFORCE WORK TOGETHER. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"if we went with a division - portal - login - asset store approvals
    - and perforce game levels lighting etc - how would we get the two to work
    together"*.

    THE HEADLINE: THEY BARELY NEED TO TALK. THEY MEET AT ONE NUMBER AND ONE NAME,
    AND EVERYTHING BEYOND THAT IS DUPLICATION — which is the user's own rule
    (item 65).

    --- THE FOUR TOUCHPOINTS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE INTERFACE ---

      1. A RELEASE RECORDS A CHANGELIST. Portal-side field: a release is a NAME, A
         PERFORCE CHANGELIST, who built it and when. The build machine syncs that
         changelist. THE PORTAL RECORDS THAT IT HAPPENED — it never talks to the
         build machine.

      2. AN APPROVED ASSET RECORDS THE CHANGELIST THAT IMPORTED IT INTO THE GAME.
         This is the link that answers "how do we know what created this in
         Unreal", AND IT BELONGS IN THE PORTAL, because Unreal's own import
         metadata dies with the machine (item 61).

      3. ACCOUNTS KEPT IN STEP. NOT a technical integration — AN OPERATIONAL ONE.
         Two systems means TWO PLACES TO REVOKE ACCESS, and if one is forgotten
         there is a live account for somebody who has left. The portal already has
         the "accounts not accounted for" check for exactly this reason (it now
         needs a counterpart list from Perforce). SEE ALSO item 42.

      4. AND THE HUMAN WORKFLOW IN BETWEEN, WHICH IS THE REAL ANSWER:

            Blender -> portal workspace -> tech check -> creative approval
                                                            |
                                                     THE GAME STORE
                                                            |
                                       somebody imports it into Unreal
                                                            |
                              the .uasset is committed to PERFORCE
                                                            |
                    the portal records that changelist against the asset

    EVERYTHING CROSSES AT THAT LAST LINE, AND IT CROSSES AS A NUMBER. No files
    move between the systems, no copies are made. THE PORTAL HOLDS THE SOURCE AND
    THE PROVENANCE; PERFORCE HOLDS THE IMPORTED RESULT.

    --- LOGIN ---

    KEEP THEM SEPARATE AT FIRST. The portal has its own accounts, Perforce has its
    own. Two logins is a small annoyance and zero engineering.

    IF ONE IDENTITY IS WANTED LATER, both systems can point at the same directory
    — PERFORCE SPEAKS LDAP AND SAML, and the portal could too. A change to make
    when two logins actually hurts, not before.

    --- WHERE NOT TO INTEGRATE ---

    "WHO IS WORKING ON WHAT." Perforce already knows, PER ACTOR, thanks to One File
    Per Actor — and P4V SHOWS IT. Mirroring that into the portal would be a
    duplicate that drifts, and it is THE EXACT FAILURE ITEM 64 NAMED.

    POINT AT P4V. CHANGE NOTHING.

    --- THE ONE OPTIONAL NICETY, AND WHY TO DEFER IT ---

    THE CHANGELIST NUMBER IS TYPED BY WHOEVER DID THE IMPORT. That is right, and
    it follows item 64's rule — STATE IS NEVER TYPED, ASSERTIONS ALWAYS ARE. The
    portal cannot observe an import, so the importer asserts it.

    THE SLICKER VERSION IS PERFORCE JOBS: approval creates a job, the importer
    attaches their changelist to it, the portal reads the changelists back. It
    removes the typing and the link is live.

    BUT IT MEANS THE PORTAL WRITING INTO PERFORCE — a real integration, a
    dependency, and a second place for a failure to strand an asset. START WITH A
    TYPED NUMBER and build the job link only if the typing actually annoys people.
    THAT IS ITEM 65'S RULE APPLIED TO OUR OWN SUGGESTION: BUILD THE THING THAT
    POINTS, NOT THE THING THAT COUPLES.

    --- WHAT THIS BUYS ---

    THE WHOLE INTERFACE IS A CHANGELIST NUMBER, A RELEASE NAME, AND A DEACTIVATION
    CHECKLIST. NOTHING IS MIRRORED, NOTHING IS DUPLICATED, AND NEITHER SYSTEM NEEDS
    THE OTHER TO BE UP — which matters, because Perforce will be down sometimes.


68) STEAM, AND WHETHER THE GAME LEAVES THIS REPOSITORY. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"the game will be released via steam is it normal for the testing to
    be done on a steam repository or what? Does the game leave this repository?
    what are the implications - which repository are yout alking about"*.

    --- WHICH REPOSITORY, FIRST, BECAUSE THE QUESTION NEEDED IT ---

    `/jobs/LowEx` IS A MONOREPO: website/ (the portal), game/ (the Unreal
    project), docs/, and source_assets/. That is "this repository", and it is the
    one the game would leave.

    VERIFIED BEFORE SAYING SO: nothing in the portal reads game/ — not the
    backend, not the frontend, not the config. AND ONLY 10 FILES UNDER game/ ARE
    TRACKED. So the move is free today and gets expensive the moment real content
    lands, which is the same shape as the .gitkeep problem.

    --- STEAM: TWO POPULATIONS, TWO MACHINERIES ---

    Internal testing is NOT done on Steam — uploads are slow, and builds sit on
    Valve's infrastructure where your own team fetches them like anybody else.
    External testing is exactly what Steam's tools are for.

        developers             Perforce sync, run the editor (item 66)
        in-house QA            a build from the build machine
        EXTERNAL PLAYTESTERS   STEAM PLAYTEST
        press / influencers    BETA BRANCH or beta keys

      * STEAM PLAYTEST — A SEPARATE APP. Up to 50,000 keys; playtester
        WISHLISTS ARE PRESERVED; their REVIEWS COUNT NORMALLY at launch; removed
        from libraries when the game releases. For large or open tests.

      * BETA BRANCHES — THE SAME APP, an alternate build, optionally
        PASSWORD-PROTECTED. For selective access, phased testing (alpha 1/2/3),
        full-game testing, and post-release hotfixes.

    TWO TRAPS: NEVER use default release keys for a playtest — they grant the
    final game. And DEVCOMP / AUTOGRANT KEYS ARE FOR DEVELOPERS AND QA TESTERS
    OUTSIDE THE ORG, NOT FOR PLAYTESTERS.

    AND YES, RELEASE-CANDIDATE TESTING ON STEAM IS NORMAL. A studio in Valve's
    own Games-as-a-Service material describes exactly the case here: a paid QA
    company, the build sent "through Steam beta branch (behind password)", QA run
    Friday, decision Monday. "RELEASE CANDIDATE" IS THE STEAM QA WORLD'S TERM TOO
    — item 60, including "the exact release candidate that received the final QA
    pass" in the release-decision record.

    --- AND IT CHANGES ITEM 26, IN THE PORTAL'S FAVOUR ---

    IF EXTERNAL TESTERS GET BUILDS THROUGH STEAM, VALVE DOES THE BROKERING, NOT
    THE PORTAL. Steam beta branches take a password; Playtest has its own app and
    its own keys. So item 26's brokering covers THE PATH STEAM DOES NOT: internal
    QA, external contractors, and anything before a Steam build exists.

    WHICH MEANS THE DEPENDENCY ACCEPTED IN ITEM 26 — portal down, no downloads —
    APPLIES TO A SMALLER SET OF BUILDS THAN IT LOOKED LIKE. Good news, and it is
    worth knowing before somebody reads item 26 as covering the Steam path too.

    --- DOES THE GAME LEAVE? YES, AND THE IMPLICATIONS ---

      1. A PERFORCE DEPOT IS CREATED, and its first changelist is the structure
         currently in game/ — which item 62's action list already assumes.
      2. THIS REPOSITORY BECOMES THE PORTAL: website/, docs/, source_assets/.
         Smaller and coherent.
      3. NOTHING BREAKS — verified above: the portal never reads game/.
      4. THE .gitignore RULES ABOUT game/Build/ BECOME MOOT and move to Perforce's
         ignore rules. (The Build/ note itself stays true — it is about Unreal,
         not about which repository it sits in.)
      5. THE GAME PROJECT SECTION BELOW DESCRIBES FILES THAT WILL NO LONGER BE
         HERE, and needs to say so rather than describe a directory that has
         gone. Same for any other item that names a game/ path.
      6. THE GAME'S HISTORY STARTS IN PERFORCE, not in this git history. The
         .gitkeep commits stay here as an orphaned fragment of the game's story —
         unavoidable, and worth one sentence rather than a migration nobody needs.

    NOT SCHEDULED. It is recorded as a consequence of the two-system decision, and
    it should happen before real content lands rather than after.


69) WHAT A BUILD IS, AND WHY THE ONE-COPY RULE DOES NOT APPLY TO ONE.
    2026-10-09.

    ASKED, after a worry worth taking seriously: *"I do not like the idea that all
    our lovely SHA one unique copy is now thrown away - what are my options"*,
    and then *"what exactly is a build then - is it like compiled code?"*.

    --- THE WORRY FIRST, BECAUSE IT IS MOSTLY UNFOUNDED ---

    NOTHING IS THROWN AWAY. The one-copy rule governs THE STUDIO'S AUTHORED
    CONTENT — stored_files, the workspace, the store, and soon the library — and
    NONE OF THAT CHANGES. Three referencers of one blob instead of two, all by
    SHA.

    What changed is narrower than it sounds: BUILDS WERE NEVER THE PORTAL'S
    CONTENT, so the rule was never doing anything for them.

    --- AND A BUILD IS COMPILED CODE, WITH ONE ADDITION ---

    YES, IT IS LIKE COMPILED CODE — except that the ASSETS GET COMPILED TOO. TWO
    TRANSFORMATIONS, NOT ONE:

      * COMPILING — the C++ becomes machine code. Exactly like compiling
        anything.

      * COOKING — THE UNREAL-SPECIFIC HALF, AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS. Every asset
        in Content/ is converted from its EDITOR FORMAT into a RUNTIME FORMAT FOR
        A SPECIFIC PLATFORM:

            a texture   an uncompressed 4K source becomes a PLATFORM-COMPRESSED
                        MIP CHAIN — BC7 for PC, ASTC for mobile. DIFFERENT BYTES,
                        DIFFERENT FILE, DIFFERENT SIZE.
            a mesh      full-precision source geometry becomes optimised geometry
                        with LODs and collision.
            shaders     written once, COMPILED PER TARGET GPU.

      * PACKAGING — the cooked result is bundled into .pak FILES alongside the
        executable and the engine.

    SO A BUILD IS A FOLDER OF PAK ARCHIVES AND BINARIES, AND YOU CANNOT GET THE
    PROJECT BACK OUT OF IT. IT IS LOSSY IN ONE DIRECTION — the same point as
    item 61's export problem, one level up.

    --- WHICH IS WHY THERE IS NO "ONE COPY" TO KEEP ---

    THERE IS NO ONE COPY OF A BUILD'S CONTENT, EVEN IN PRINCIPLE. The same source
    texture becomes DIFFERENT BYTES FOR PC AND FOR CONSOLE — and different again
    for SHIPPING versus DEVELOPMENT, because Shipping strips debug data and turns
    on optimisations.

        ONE SOURCE TEXTURE -> FOUR DIFFERENT COOKED BLOBS.

    SO CONTENT ADDRESSING CANNOT GIVE ON THE BUILD SIDE WHAT IT GIVES IN THE
    STORE: THERE IS NO SINGLE CANONICAL BYTE SEQUENCE FOR "THE TEXTURE" THERE.

    THE CANONICAL THING IS THE SOURCE. And the source IS stored once — the store,
    the library, Perforce. THE GUARANTEE WORTH NAMING: EVERY BYTE THE STUDIO
    AUTHORS IS STORED ONCE, and that is true today and stays true.

    WHAT IS NOT STORED ONCE IS A BUILD, BECAUSE A BUILD IS NOT AUTHORED — IT IS
    PRODUCED. Produced things are cheap to produce again.

    --- AND THE PRINCIPLE IS NOT ABANDONED. IT IS IMPLEMENTED FOUR TIMES. ---

        THE PORTAL    assets, by SHA — one copy, three referencers
        PERFORCE      per-file deltas — you sync only what changed
        UNREAL        the DDC is a CONTENT-ADDRESSED CACHE of DERIVED data, keyed
                      by hashes of its inputs; and VIRTUAL ASSETS store bulk
                      payloads REFERENCED BY HASH and de-duplicated (item 66)
        STEAM         SteamPipe delivers builds in CHUNKS and sends only the
                      chunks that changed

    THE SAME INSTINCT RUNS THE WHOLE WAY DOWN. THE PORTAL IS SIMPLY NOT THE LAYER
    THAT DOES IT FOR BUILDS — and if it tried, it would be re-implementing
    Unreal's DDC, Unreal's patch paks AND Steam's delivery at once. That is the
    scope creep the two-system decision (item 62) exists to prevent.

    --- BUT BUILDS ARE NOT ALWAYS DISPOSABLE, AND THIS MODIFIES THE OPTIONS ---

        NIGHTLY AND INTERNAL BUILDS   DISCARDED FREELY. Reproduce on demand.
        RELEASED BUILDS               ARCHIVED, DELIBERATELY.

    "Going gold" means KEEPING THE MASTER, because in two years, when a bug is
    reported, YOU MAY BE UNABLE TO REBUILD THAT EXACT THING: a different engine
    version, a different compiler, a different toolchain, and the output will not
    be byte-identical.

    SO: BUILDS ARE COMPILED OUTPUT, AND COMPILED OUTPUT IS REGENERABLE — EXCEPT
    THE ONES YOU SHIPPED, WHICH YOU KEEP PRECISELY BECAUSE "REGENERABLE" STOPS
    BEING TRUE ACROSS TIME.

    --- AND IT CHANGES NOTHING ALREADY DECIDED ---

    THE PORTAL KEEPS THE RELEASE RECORD — name, changelist, who, when — and THAT
    is what points at an archived build wherever it is kept. Item 60's shape,
    unchanged, which is a good sign: a decision that survives learning what the
    thing actually is was probably the right decision.

    --- THE OPTIONS, RECORDED SO THEY ARE NOT RE-DERIVED ---

      1. NOTHING. RECOMMENDED. The rule holds for authored content, and the build
         side gets the same benefit from three tools that already do it.

      2. THE PORTAL HOLDS BUILDS AS PLAIN BLOBS. No dedupe, but no design either —
         it costs disk and nothing else. Only if a case appears that Steam and
         Perforce cannot serve.

      3. STORE THE CHANGELIST AND REBUILD ON DEMAND. Nothing to store at all —
         costs hours per build, and depends on reproducibility being real, WHICH
         IT ONLY PARTLY IS (see "going gold" above).

      4. CONTENT-ADDRESS THE BUILDS IN THE PORTAL. Unpacking pak files, hashing
         cooked binaries, storing chunks, reassembling a runnable build on
         download. Large, duplicates three existing tools, and is the scope creep
         item 62 exists to prevent.


70) WHAT TO BUILD FIRST, AND WHAT BLOCKS IT. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"Ok so what should we build first"*. Recorded so the next session
    starts from an order rather than re-deriving one, AND SO THAT THE REASONING
    IS AVAILABLE TO DISAGREE WITH.

    =====================================================================
    BEFORE ANY CODE — because these get worse with time, and neither is a build
    =====================================================================

      1. MOVE game/ OUT OF THIS REPOSITORY INTO PERFORCE (item 68). TEN FILES
         TODAY. Item 68 verified that nothing in the portal reads game/ — not the
         backend, not the frontend, not the config — so it is a clean removal, and
         the moment real content lands it stops being clean. This is the .gitkeep
         lesson a second time.

      2. STAND UP PERFORCE AND CONFIGURE IT IN UNREAL (item 62's action list):
         server OFF-SITE, the accounts, source control in the editor, exclusive
         check-out on binaries and .uasset. Nothing about the game starts properly
         without it, and every other decision assumes it.

    =====================================================================
    THE FIRST THING TO BUILD: THE IMAGE LIBRARY (item 57)
    with the upload-type question (item 56) as its front door
    =====================================================================

    FOUR REASONS, in order of weight:

      * IT IS THE LIVE CASE. The work actually in hand is images. Everything else
        in this file is preparation for a game that DOES NOT EXIST YET.

      * IT CORRECTS A BEHAVIOUR THAT IS WRONG TODAY. Item 54's run-through found
        that images currently travel toward creative approval and the store — and
        items 57/58 established that is not where they belong. RIGHT NOW,
        UPLOADING A FOLDER OF REFERENCE PHOTOGRAPHS DOES THE WRONG THING. That is
        a live bug, not a missing feature, which is why it outranks the rest.

      * EVERYTHING IMAGE-SHAPED DEPENDS ON IT. Steps 6, 7 and 8 of item 54 —
        labelling, thumbnails, content search — have nowhere to live until the
        library exists.

      * AND IT PROVES THE PROPERTY EVERYTHING RESTS ON. A library is a THIRD
        REFERENCER OF stored_files — the first new one since the portal was built.
        IF THE ONE-COPY RULE HOLDS WITH THREE, THE DESIGN IS SOUND. If it does
        not, better to know now than after the store is full.

    --- THE SMALLEST USEFUL SLICE ---

      * a library table, and the third reference into stored_files
      * an upload path whose destination is THE LIBRARY, not a workspace asset
      * THE TYPE QUESTION ASKED AT UPLOAD — application project, or arbitrary
        collection (item 56)
      * a listing page

    DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE FIRST SLICE: thumbnails, content search, labelling,
    batch names. Those are steps 6-8 and they follow naturally once there is
    somewhere to put them. (Thumbnails in particular are a LIBRARY concern — item
    57 — so they are not being deferred out of the design, only out of the first
    slice.)

    =====================================================================
    WHAT NOT TO BUILD YET, AND WHY
    =====================================================================

      * RELEASES, CANDIDATES, BUILDS (items 53, 60, 68's Steam half). EVERY ONE
        DEPENDS ON A GAME THAT DOES NOT EXIST. Item 53 already carries an explicit
        warning against building a releases model to fill a UI placeholder, having
        watched that mistake almost happen once.

      * AND THE CHECK-OUT (item 59), DESPITE THIS FILE HAVING CALLED IT "the door
        everything goes through". It is the biggest single gap in the workflow and
        that description was not wrong — but NOTHING WANTS IT UNTIL SOMEBODY
        ITERATES ON AN APPROVED ASSET, and it is far easier to design once the
        library exists, because THE LIBRARY IS ONE OF THE TWO THINGS YOU WOULD BE
        CHECKING OUT FROM. Building the check-out first would mean designing both
        ends at once.

      * ITEM 55 (UNZIP) — AFTER the type question (item 56), and carefully. This
        file calls it THE RISKIEST CODE IN THE UPLOAD AREA, and it cannot be built
        correctly until item 56 says whether an inflated archive becomes ONE asset
        or MANY.

    =====================================================================
    AND THE DECISIONS THAT BLOCK THE STEPS ABOVE
    =====================================================================

    None of these is needed before the two pre-code items, but each blocks
    something:

      * IS THE LIBRARY SHARED OR PER-DEVELOPER? (item 57.) Blocks the library's
        table and its permissions. The reading is SHARED — source_assets is studio
        material — but a developer's own cull might want to be private first.
      * WHAT MAY LIVE IN THE LIBRARY? (item 57.) Images are the case in hand.
        Does video? Audio? A PDF? The name says images; the need may not.
      * WHO MANAGES RELEASES, AND WHO RUNS THE BUILD MACHINE? (items 53, 60, 62.)
        Not before the game, but they are the two remaining "who" questions.
      * AND WHETHER game/ ACTUALLY LEAVES (item 68) — recommended, verified safe,
        not yet decided.


71) THE PERFORCE SERVER AND ITS DEPOTS. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"if we are using perforce what new repository do we need and where
    should it live"*.

    --- THE WORD "REPOSITORY" IS DOING WORK, SO FIRST: ---

    YOU DO NOT CREATE A REPOSITORY PER DEVELOPER. You create ONE SERVER, and on
    it, DEPOTS. Developers have workspaces that map parts of a depot onto their
    disk (item 62). There is no per-person copy to create or place.

    --- WHAT TO CREATE ---

      * ONE DEPOT FOR THE GAME PROJECT. //LowExpectations/... or similar — the
        Unreal project: Content/, Config/, Plugins/, Source/, the .uproject.
        THAT IS THE WHOLE GAME.

      * ONE DEPOT FOR VIRTUALIZED BULK DATA — BUT ONLY IF VIRTUAL ASSETS ARE
        USED. Epic explicitly recommends bulk payloads live in a SEPARATE DEPOT so
        nobody syncs them by accident (item 66). A second depot, created later,
        not now.

      * AND THAT IS ALL. NOT the portal — that is code in git and git suits it.
        NOT the docs — they live in the portal repository and the portal serves
        them.

    AND ONE RECOMMENDATION WHILE IT IS FREE: CREATE IT AS A *STREAM* DEPOT, NOT A
    CLASSIC ONE. Streams are Perforce's branching model — mainline, development,
    release lines — and they are what "which line of development am I on" MEANS
    in item 64. Converting later is unpleasant; creating it as one costs nothing
    today.

    --- WHERE IT LIVES ---

    OFF-SITE, AND THAT IS ALREADY DECIDED (item 62). On-prem would make the
    studio's uplink everyone's bottleneck and a single point of failure.

      * HELIX CORE CLOUD — Perforce runs it. No server administration, no backup
        regime to build, paid.

      * YOUR OWN VPS — Hetzner, DigitalOcean, AWS, whatever. Cheaper per byte,
        more control, AND YOU OWN THE BACKUPS.

    --- AND THE FOUR THINGS THAT WILL ACTUALLY DECIDE IT ---

      * DISK. The project grows to 300 GB - 1 TB. THAT DOMINATES THE HOSTING COST,
        and it is the number to price BEFORE choosing.

      * LATENCY. PERFORCE IS CHATTY — many small requests per operation, so a
        server on the wrong continent makes every sync feel broken. PUT IT NEAR
        THE TEAM, and near the build machine.

      * EGRESS. Remote developers syncing large trees is bandwidth, and bandwidth
        is billed. NOT a rounding error on a 1 TB project.

      * BACKUPS — THE ONE PEOPLE GET WRONG. Perforce is NOT backed up by copying
        files while it runs. It needs CHECKPOINTS AND JOURNALS, rotated, and
        COPIED OFF-SITE. A studio that skips this discovers it exactly once.

    --- AND IF THE PROJECT OUTGROWS A SINGLE SERVER ---

    Perforce has an answer worth knowing exists BEFORE it is needed: REPLICAS AND
    EDGE SERVERS — a local copy of the depot near each group of developers, so
    syncs are fast while the central server stays authoritative. That is the
    standard solution for a remote team with a large repository, AND IT IS THE
    REASON NOT TO PANIC ABOUT EGRESS.

    --- ONE THING THAT DOES NOT GO IN PERFORCE ---

    source_assets/ AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT — the folder currently holding a 3.3 GB
    zip and two photographs. UNDER ITEM 57 THAT MATERIAL BELONGS IN THE IMAGE
    LIBRARY, which is the portal's content-addressed store: not a Perforce depot
    and not a git repository. It is a leftover of where the idea started, and it
    is worth naming before somebody builds tooling around a directory that is
    about to stop being the place.


72) PERFORCE: SELF-HOSTED FIRST, UK/EU REGION. 2026-10-09.

    DECIDED: SELF-HOSTED TO BEGIN WITH, MIGRATED LATER. REGION UK/EU.

    --- WHY THAT IS A GOOD FIT, IN ONE LINE ---

    SELF-HOSTING IS HOW THE FREE TIER IS OBTAINED. The free 5-users/20-workspaces
    licence (item 62) is for the HELIX CORE SERVER you run yourself; Helix Core
    Cloud is a separate PAID offering. So self-hosting first is the free option as
    well as the flexible one, and migrating later is a decision that can be made
    on cost and convenience rather than on being stuck.

    --- AND THE ONE THING TO DO NOW, BECAUSE IT MAKES THE MIGRATION CHEAP ---

    GIVE THE SERVER A DNS NAME AND NEVER AN IP ADDRESS.

    A developer's workspace records the server as a hostname (`P4PORT`). IF THE
    HOSTNAME IS STABLE AND ONLY DNS MOVES, THEN MIGRATING THE SERVER IS A DNS
    CHANGE AND NOBODY HAS TO RECONFIGURE ANYTHING. If the address is an IP, or a
    provider-generated hostname like a bare VPS identifier, then every developer
    must repoint every workspace by hand on migration day — and somebody will
    forget, and their client will talk to a server that no longer exists.

    SO: A NAME UNDER A DOMAIN THE STUDIO CONTROLS, from the first day, chosen
    before the provider. It costs nothing and it is the whole difference between
    a migration that is an afternoon and one that is a week of support.

    --- AND THE DOMAIN EXISTS. DECIDED 2026-10-09. ---

    THE STUDIO ALREADY OWNS lowexpectations.zone — the game's domain. SO THE
    SERVER GETS A SUBDOMAIN OF IT AND THE MIGRATION PROBLEM IS ALREADY HALF
    SOLVED, because the name is not something to be invented later.

        HOSTNAME   vcs.lowexpectations.zone

    AND `vcs` RATHER THAN `p4` OR `perforce`, DELIBERATELY: THE NAME SHOULD
    OUTLIVE THE TOOL. Item 73 puts a plausible migration to Epic's Lore in 2027,
    and a hostname saying "perforce" would then be a lie that every developer's
    workspace still points at. NAME THE ROLE, NOT THE PRODUCT — so `vcs.`
    survives Perforce, Lore, or whatever follows.

    THE SETUP:

        DNS      A/AAAA  vcs.lowexpectations.zone  ->  <VPS address>
        TLS      certificate for that hostname
        P4PORT   ssl:vcs.lowexpectations.zone:1666

    AND MIGRATION BECOMES: CHANGE THE A RECORD. No developer reconfigures
    anything, because nothing they hold names an address.

    --- TWO THINGS WORTH KNOWING NOW, BOTH CHEAP ---

      * CERTIFICATE TRANSPARENCY. A Let's Encrypt certificate for
        vcs.lowexpectations.zone PUBLISHES THAT HOSTNAME IN PUBLIC CT LOGS, so
        anybody can learn a version control server runs on the domain. Minor, and
        avoidable: A WILDCARD CERTIFICATE for *.lowexpectations.zone covers it
        without naming the subdomain — and a wildcard is plausibly wanted anyway
        for the game's own site.

      * REACHABILITY. The team is REMOTE, so IP allowlisting is impractical —
        dynamic addresses, home connections. That leaves TLS PLUS STRONG
        CREDENTIALS as the control, which is normal. A NON-DEFAULT PORT cuts
        background scanner noise, though that is tidiness rather than security.

    --- AND ONE ADJACENT THOUGHT, NOT A DECISION TODAY ---

    THE PORTAL IS THE STUDIO'S TOOL — developers, testers, admins — not a
    game-facing thing. If the game later has a public site on this domain, the
    portal may belong somewhere NEUTRAL rather than under the game's brand. Worth
    not defaulting into, and not worth deciding now.

    --- AND THE DOMAIN IS ON CLOUDFLARE. ONE SETTING DECIDES WHETHER THIS WORKS.
        Added 2026-10-09, because getting it wrong looks like a broken server.
    ---

    THE vcs. RECORD MUST BE DNS-ONLY — GREY CLOUD, NOT ORANGE.

    CLOUDFLARE'S PROXY ONLY UNDERSTANDS HTTP, HTTP/2, HTTP/3 AND WEBSOCKET.
    PERFORCE IS NONE OF THOSE. If vcs.lowexpectations.zone is orange-clouded,
    traffic is REJECTED AT THE EDGE AND IT WILL LOOK LIKE THE SERVER IS DOWN —
    and somebody will spend an afternoon debugging a VPS that is working
    perfectly.

    CLOUDFLARE'S OWN DOCS GIVE EXACTLY TWO ANSWERS FOR A NON-HTTP PROTOCOL:

      1. DNS-ONLY (grey cloud) — Cloudflare returns the real IP and does not
         route the traffic. STANDARD PRACTICE FOR NON-HTTP SERVICES.
      2. CLOUDFLARE SPECTRUM — a parallel proxy opening raw TCP listeners,
         forwarding bytes without parsing them, with IP-hiding and L3/L4 DDoS
         protection. PAID AND USAGE-BILLED, and described as a fairly expensive
         workaround.

    FOR FIVE PEOPLE: OPTION 1.

    --- WHAT GREY CLOUD COSTS US, STATED PLAINLY ---

      * THE ORIGIN IP BECOMES PUBLIC. Normal for a VCS server, and it means:
      * OUR OWN TLS CERTIFICATE. Universal SSL applies to PROXIED hostnames, so
        it does not cover this one. LET'S ENCRYPT WITH DNS-01 VALIDATION THROUGH
        CLOUDFLARE'S API is straightforward, and permits a wildcard.
      * NO CLOUDFLARE DDoS PROTECTION on that hostname. So the FIREWALL MATTERS:
        DROP EVERYTHING EXCEPT THE PERFORCE PORT, and keep SSH restricted rather
        than open.
      * AND STRONG CREDENTIALS PLUS TLS are the control — now with the corollary
        that the server is FINDABLE.

    --- AND THE GOOD PART ---

    THE DOMAIN BEING ON CLOUDFLARE IS GOOD NEWS FOR THE MIGRATION. Changing an A
    record is a dashboard edit, so the advice above works exactly as intended:
    vcs.lowexpectations.zone repoints to a new host and NOBODY RECONFIGURES
    ANYTHING.

    AND DIFFERENT HOSTNAMES ON THE SAME DOMAIN GET DIFFERENT TREATMENT, which is
    the useful thing about the orange/grey distinction:

        THE GAME'S SITE   ORANGE   HTTP — Cloudflare's proxy, CDN, protection
        THE PORTAL        ORANGE   HTTP — same
        vcs.              GREY     Perforce is NOT HTTP; the proxy cannot carry it

    --- WHAT SELF-HOSTING MEANS YOU NOW OWN ---

    A managed service would have done all of this. On a VPS, none of it is
    anybody else's job:

      * BACKUPS. PERFORCE IS NOT BACKED UP BY COPYING FILES WHILE IT RUNS. It
        needs `p4 admin checkpoint` on a schedule, JOURNAL ROTATION, and the
        checkpoint COPIED OFF-SITE — off the same provider, ideally. AND THE
        RESTORE MUST BE TESTED, because an untested checkpoint is a belief rather
        than a backup. THIS IS THE ITEM MOST LIKELY TO BE SKIPPED AND THE ONE
        THAT COSTS THE MOST.

      * TLS. A remote team sends tickets and file content over the internet.
        Perforce supports it (`P4PORT=ssl:host:1666`) and it is a requirement
        rather than a nicety, because the alternative is credentials in clear.

      * DISK, sized for 300 GB - 1 TB of project plus Perforce's own database and
        journal growth. Block storage is where the bill lands, so size it before
        choosing a provider rather than after.

      * MONITORING — that the server is up, and that the CHECKPOINTS ARE RUNNING.
        The second is the one nobody notices has stopped.

    --- AND THE MIGRATION ITSELF, SO IT IS NOT A SURPRISE ---

    The procedure is documented and well-trodden: CHECKPOINT, move the checkpoint
    and the depot files, RESTORE on the new host, repoint DNS. Two things worth
    knowing in advance:

      * KEEP THE ARCHITECTURE THE SAME. Perforce's depot storage is
        platform-dependent, so moving between architectures is not a file copy —
        it needs a recovery step. Staying on the same architecture avoids the
        whole question, and a migration is a bad time to discover it.

      * AND SCHEDULE IT. A migration is downtime for everybody, so it wants a
        quiet window and a message to the team, not an afternoon where syncs
        mysteriously stop working.

    --- REGION ---

    UK/EU, which is also sensible on latency: PERFORCE IS CHATTY (item 71), so
    the server wants to be on the same side of the Atlantic as the team. Note
    that a UK/EU region and a UK/EU TEAM are two different things — if the team
    is spread, the region should follow where most of the sync traffic is, and
    the answer to that is replicas and edge servers rather than a compromise
    location (item 71).


73) WHY NOT EPIC'S LORE. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED PLAINLY: *"Sop why not use Epic's Lore - it is an unreal game - and
    perforce has a bad rep"*. A FAIR CHALLENGE, and the answer is NOT "stick with
    Perforce because we said so". It is TIME-SENSITIVE, which is why it is
    written down with dates.

    --- FIRST, LORE'S DESIGN IS BETTER FOR A GAME THAN PERFORCE'S ---

    Worth saying plainly, because it is the property the user was worried about
    losing two questions earlier: LORE IS CONTENT-ADDRESSED, CHUNKED,
    DEDUPLICATED, AND DOES SPARSE/LAZY FETCHING. That is the one-copy principle
    applied to the WHOLE PROJECT, binaries included — the thing item 69 concluded
    was impossible for builds and is merely hard for cooked content.

    WHAT IT IS: Epic's own open-source VCS, MIT licensed, written in Rust. It
    ORIGINATED AS UNREAL REVISION CONTROL, is used inside Epic, and POWERS
    UEFN'S IN-EDITOR VERSION CONTROL — Auto Checkout, Auto Revert, Sync Changes,
    Check-in Changes. SDKs in JavaScript, Python, C#, Go and Rust.

    SO ON DESIGN IT IS NOT A COMPROMISE. IT IS WHAT WE WOULD WANT.

    --- AND THREE FACTS STOP IT TODAY ---

      1. THE UNREAL EDITOR PLUGIN IS SLATED FOR 2027. Lore is usable with UE 5.8
         TODAY, but there is NO IN-EDITOR SOURCE CONTROL YET — the plugin will
         ship IN THE ENGINE CORE, per Epic's roadmap. Which means NO AUTO
         CHECKOUT, NO PER-ACTOR CHECK-OUT FROM THE EDITOR. And for a project
         built on ONE FILE PER ACTOR, PER-ACTOR CHECK-OUT IS THE WORKFLOW (item
         63). Everything Lore does well today, it does best in UEFN — NOT IN THE
         ENGINE THIS GAME IS IN.

      2. IT IS PRE-1.0 — 0.8.3 — AND EPIC WARNS THAT "INTERFACES AND STORAGE
         FORMATS MAY CHANGE BETWEEN RELEASES". In a version control system that
         is not a rough edge: IT IS THE REPOSITORY FORMAT POSSIBLY CHANGING UNDER
         US.

      3. AND EPIC ITSELF SAYS DO NOT. Their EVP, on the record: "Until we have
         fully moved our development to it, I don't think we necessarily
         recommend that everyone goes out and moves all their critical
         infrastructure just yet." THE COMPANY THAT MAKES THE ENGINE, AND THE
         TOOL, IS TELLING US NOT TO MOVE CRITICAL INFRASTRUCTURE ONTO IT YET.

    --- SO THE ANSWER IS NOT "PERFORCE". IT IS "PERFORCE NOW, LORE LATER". ---

    Epic has said Lore is core to UE6, whose EARLY ACCESS IS TARGETED AT LATE
    2027, and the editor plugin lands the same year.

    WHICH MAKES ITEM 72'S ADVICE MORE IMPORTANT, NOT LESS. There is now a
    SPECIFIC AND LIKELY MIGRATION in the future, AT A KNOWN TIME. THE DNS NAME
    DECIDES WHETHER THAT IS AN AFTERNOON OR A WEEK. A name under a domain the
    studio controls turns the 2027 migration into a DNS change.

    AND IT MEANS A TRIAL NOW WOULD TEST THE WRONG THING. Two weeks with Lore
    today exercises it WITHOUT the editor plugin — so it would NOT show the OFPA
    workflow, which is the thing that matters and the thing that does not exist
    yet. WATCH IT RATHER THAN TRIAL IT.

    --- ON THE BAD REPUTATION ---

    LARGELY EARNED, AND LARGELY ABOUT COST AND ERGONOMICS AT SCALE: expensive for
    large teams, cumbersome to administer, an interface that feels its age. WHAT
    IT IS NOT IS UNRELIABLE — and at five users it is FREE, which removes the
    loudest complaint.

    The practitioner summary is worth keeping: "everyone in mid/large games uses
    Perforce — ancient and not ideal, but it works at these data sizes and needed
    feature sets." THE COMPLAINTS AND THE UBIQUITY ARE ABOUT DIFFERENT THINGS.

    --- AND WHAT WOULD CHANGE THIS ANSWER ---

      * THE UNREAL EDITOR PLUGIN SHIPPING (2027), which is the missing half.
      * LORE REACHING 1.0, which is when "storage formats may change" stops being
        true.
      * OR HITTING A PERFORCE WALL — cost, an admin problem, a workflow it cannot
        do. That is a reason to look again, and it should be written down here
        when it happens rather than silently endured.

    AND THE TIMING IS IN OUR FAVOUR EITHER WAY: switching is cheap now and
    expensive the moment the game has content. Same theme as the .gitkeep lesson
    and the repository question (item 68).


    ============================================================
    WHAT WAS BUILT, 2026-10-09
    ============================================================

    THE FIRST THING OFF THE LIST IN ITEM 70. The third space exists, and with it
    the THIRD REFERENCER of stored_files — which is why this was worth doing
    first: it is the test of the one-copy rule the whole design rests on, and the
    test passed.

    --- WHAT EXISTS ---

      * library_items and library_thumbnails in db.js, beside the two file
        tables. UNIQUE (stored_file_id) makes "one item per blob" STRUCTURAL —
        the duplicate rule cannot be forgotten by a route.
      * backend/routes/library.js — list, upload, thumbnail, content, remove.
        Mounted at /api/library; developers and above.
      * backend/lib/ingestFiles.js — THE ONE WAY A FILE ENTERS THE STORE,
        extracted from the workspace upload so both paths call it. This was the
        plan's most important structural decision: item 55 already says a second
        upload path is how two pipelines end up disagreeing about what is
        acceptable.
      * Thumbnails with sharp, at upload, for images only. They are DERIVED, so
        they are NOT stored_files rows (item 69) — they live in THUMB_DIR as a
        cache, named <sha>_<kind>.webp. The kind is in the FILENAME because one
        blob may have more than one thumbnail later, and keying by the source hash
        alone would have the second silently overwrite the first.
      * A Library tab on the developer dashboard, in the tab strip that already
        existed, plus frontend/src/developerLibrary.js.

    --- THE TWO PLACES THAT HAD TO LEARN ABOUT THE THIRD TABLE ---

      * backend/jobs/daily.js — THE COLLECTOR. Its NOT EXISTS pair meant every
        library blob became collectable once past the grace window: the library
        would empty itself on a schedule, silently. A REMOVED item does NOT
        protect its blob, which is deliberate — removing something from the
        library is the act of releasing it.
      * backend/lib/workspaceUsage.js — the quota NOT IN. Verified: pulling
        library material into a workspace costs NO quota, because the rule is
        that the quota measures what the developer holds THAT THE STUDIO DOES
        NOT.

    IF A FOURTH REFERENCER EVER APPEARS, both of those are where to look.

    --- VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED ---

    The two silent-failure paths were tested against the REAL collector (run as
    a subprocess with --dry-run) and the REAL quota function: a library item
    protects its blob, a removed one releases it, and a library blob costs no
    workspace quota. Then everything was driven over HTTP against a throwaway
    account: upload, the one-copy rule through the route (second upload reports
    the existing item and stored_files stays at one row), refusal of an
    executable named .png, refusal of an empty file, a JPEG named .png ACCEPTED
    with the contradiction recorded, a PDF listed with no thumbnail, content
    served as application/octet-stream with identical bytes, and a soft delete
    that stops serving. All test data removed; the store returned to 0 rows and
    0 blobs.

    --- THREE FINDINGS WORTH KEEPING ---

      * THE APP IS LIGHT-ONLY. Bootstrap's dark theme is not in the bundle at
        all. The library's styles use --bs-* variables anyway, so the panel would
        follow a theme if one were ever added — but "check it in both themes" is
        not a thing that can be done here, and the plan asked for it.

      * npm audit IS NO LONGER CLEAN, AND SHARP DID NOT CAUSE IT. Item 22 records
        0 vulnerabilities on 2026-10-01; audit now reports 3 (proxy-addr,
        shell-quote, source-map-js) AT THE EXACT SAME VERSIONS as before the
        install. Advisories were published in between. In practice:
        shell-quote (via concurrently) and source-map-js (via vite) are DEV-ONLY,
        and proxy-addr's advisory needs `trust proxy`, WHICH IS NOT SET. So
        nothing here is a production risk, and `npm audit fix` was NOT run —
        bumping transitive dependencies of a working app to clear a report
        nobody is exposed to is the wrong trade. Item 22 needs re-checking rather
        than re-believing.

      * THE COLLECTOR'S "BELT AND BRACES" COVERS ONE OF THREE TABLES, and this is
        recorded rather than changed. After deciding a blob is unreferenced it
        deletes, and it also runs `DELETE FROM asset_version_files WHERE
        stored_file_id = ?` — but not the workspace or library equivalents. The
        asymmetry is latent for two reasons: the query has already proved there
        are no references, so the delete is a no-op; and ADDING library_items to
        it would be actively dangerous, because if the query were ever wrong that
        line would DELETE A LIBRARY ITEM rather than fail. It either belongs on
        all three tables or none, and "none" is the safer half — but that is a
        change to the code that destroys data, and it wants doing deliberately.


74) THE THEME — A HALF-APPLIED DARK THEME, AND THE RULE FOR NEW WORK.
    2026-10-09. IMPORTANT.

    ASKED: *"Please make sure the css theme is put somewhere obvious for future
    use - website must be consistent - use canonical bootstrap as much as
    possible"*.

    --- THE RULE, AND IT IS NOW WRITTEN AT THE TOP OF THE FILE ---

    frontend/styles/styles.css NOW BEGINS with "THE THEME — READ THIS BEFORE
    ADDING A RULE TO THIS FILE", which states the order of preference:

      1. A BOOTSTRAP COMPONENT — .card, .alert, .table, .btn, .nav.
      2. BOOTSTRAP UTILITIES, in the markup — a rule should not exist for
         something `card-body p-2 d-flex flex-column gap-1` already does.
      3. A BOOTSTRAP CSS VARIABLE — var(--bs-border-color) and the rest.
      4. AND ONLY THEN A RULE, with a comment saying why Bootstrap could not.

    IT IS AT THE TOP OF styles.css AND NOT IN A DOCS DIRECTORY, deliberately: it
    is read by the person about to add a rule, at the moment they are about to
    add one, and nowhere else would be seen.

    --- AND WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT THE CURRENT STATE, WHICH IS NOT CONSISTENT ---

    THE SITE IS A HALF-APPLIED DARK THEME, AND IT WAS FOUND BY MEASURING RATHER
    THAN LOOKING. `body` is hand-darkened to #0d0e12, but Bootstrap's own dark
    theme is NOT loaded — `data-bs-theme="dark"` appears NOWHERE in the bundle —
    and no component is overridden. So the page is dark and every `.card` on it
    is Bootstrap's default LIGHT surface. Sampling the pixels of a rendered panel
    gave #0d0e12 outside and #f8f8f8 for the card.

    IT WAS NOT DESIGNED, IT ACCUMULATED, and the scattered `!important` overrides
    in the header, auth and utility-bar sections are the rest of it.

    TWO WAYS OUT, AND IT IS THE USER'S DECISION RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP:

      * ADOPT BOOTSTRAP'S DARK THEME PROPERLY — set data-bs-theme="dark" on the
        html element and DELETE the hand-darkened body and the scattered
        overrides. Everything follows, including components this file has never
        touched. MUCH THE SMALLER CHANGE, and the one that makes "canonical
        Bootstrap" true rather than aspirational.

      * OR COMMIT TO A LIGHT SITE — drop the dark body entirely.

    EITHER WAY IT IS DONE IN ONE PASS. A page that is half one and half the other
    is exactly what we have now, and doing it panel by panel reproduces it.

    --- AND WHAT WAS DONE HERE ---

    THE LIBRARY WAS BROUGHT INTO LINE, as the first thing to follow the new rule.
    It began with ten custom classes; it now ships THREE, and the built bundle
    confirms it:

        library-grid        CSS Grid — no Bootstrap utility does this
        library-tile-frame  aspect-ratio — no Bootstrap equivalent
        library-tile-name   line-clamp — no Bootstrap equivalent

    ALL THREE ARE LAYOUT. Everything else became canonical Bootstrap in the
    markup: `.card`, `.card-body`, `d-flex`, `gap-1`, `p-2`, `small`,
    `text-secondary`, `fw-medium`, `text-warning-emphasis`, `object-fit-cover`,
    `mt-auto`. The warning colour in particular was a custom rule and is now
    Bootstrap's own `text-warning-emphasis`, which is designed for contrast.

    SO THE PATTERN FOR THE REST OF THE SITE IS DEMONSTRATED, NOT JUST DESCRIBED:
    a panel can be built with three layout rules and nothing else.


    ============================================================
    CORRECTED, 2026-10-09, THE SAME DAY AND BY THE SAME PERSON
    ============================================================

    THE FINDING ABOVE IS WRONG. "The site is a half-applied dark theme with
    Bootstrap's own dark theme NOT loaded" IS FALSE. Bootstrap's dark theme WAS
    loaded, and had been all along.

    WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE:

      * EVERY PAGE SETS data-bs-theme="dark" ON THE <html> ELEMENT. build.js
        writes it into all five generated pages (build.js:174) and the
        hand-written ones carry it too. The body also carries
        class="bg-dark text-light".
      * So the surfaces, borders, form controls, focus rings and alerts were ALL
        Bootstrap's, and followed the theme by themselves.

    HOW THE MISTAKE HAPPENED, BECAUSE IT IS THE USEFUL PART:

      * The check grepped THE APP'S OWN CSS BUNDLE for `data-bs-theme=dark`. The
        attribute is in the MARKUP and in BOOTSTRAP'S stylesheet — neither of
        which is that file. It could never have been found there.
      * AND THE MEASUREMENT CONFIRMED IT, WHICH IS THE WORSE HALF. A rendering
        harness built to screenshot the panel had `data-bs-theme="light"`
        HARDCODED IN IT. It faithfully rendered a light theme, and the light
        card was read as evidence about the site rather than about the harness.
      * A MEASUREMENT ONLY SETTLES THE QUESTION YOU ASKED IT. This one answered
        "what does my harness look like".

    SO THE INSTRUCTION IT PRODUCED — *"adopt the bootstrap dark theme
    properly"* — WAS ANSWERED ON A FALSE PREMISE, and what was actually worth
    doing turned out to be smaller and different: not adopting the theme, but
    REMOVING THE HAND-ROLLED OVERRIDES THAT FOUGHT IT.

    ============================================================
    WHAT WAS ACTUALLY DONE, AND IT WAS STILL WORTH DOING
    ============================================================

    THE THEME WAS ALREADY ADOPTED; THE OVERRIDES ON TOP OF IT WERE THE PROBLEM.
    All of the following are gone from frontend/styles/styles.css:

      * body { background-color: #0d0e12 !important } — the page ground was
        hand-darkened to a colour of its own instead of Bootstrap's.
      * .form-control:focus with four hardcoded values and four !importants.
        THIS WAS THE WORST OF THEM: it replaced Bootstrap's own focus ring, so
        the one control everybody touches was the one control not following the
        theme.
      * .feature-card DEFINED TWICE, with different backgrounds. The second won
        silently; only one is left.
      * .panel-module:hover with !important on the border and the shadow —
        reaching past Bootstrap to beat it, which also means beating every future
        theme change.
      * Hardcoded colours in the header, the utility bar, the login dropdown, the
        social buttons and the usage bars. All now var(--bs-*).

    AND THE RESULT IS VISIBLE AND SMALL, WHICH IS THE POINT: rendering the panel
    before and after and sampling the pixels shows EXACTLY ONE COLOUR MOVED —
    the page ground, #0d0e12 to Bootstrap's #212529. Card, tile and alert are
    byte-identical, because they were already Bootstrap's and nothing here was
    ever fighting over them.

    THE ONE HARDCODED COLOUR THAT STAYS, deliberately: #sandbox-canvas on the
    shopfront. It is the ground the 3D artwork is drawn against — content, not
    chrome — and it should not move with a theme. There is now a comment saying
    so, so it is not mistaken for another oversight.

    AND THE RULE AT THE TOP OF THE FILE NOW SAYS THE THEME IS BOOTSTRAP'S AND
    THAT THIS FILE ADDS ALMOST NOTHING TO IT, with the correction written into
    it — a note that says "read this before adding a rule" is worth very little
    if it is itself wrong.


75) STATIC ASSETS MUST LIVE IN frontend/public/ — FOUND BY BREAKING IT.
    2026-10-09.

    THE INDEX PAGE'S CLICKABLE REGION IS AN ALPHA MAP, and it stopped working.
    index.html draws ./images/main.png and hit-tests against ./images/mainAlpha.png
    on a canvas — clickable where the mask says so. Both files were present in
    frontend/images/ and tracked in git. THE PAGE STILL BROKE.

    --- WHY, AND IT WAS A BUILD THAT DID IT ---

    backend/server.js SERVES frontend/dist. That directory is GITIGNORED — it is
    a BUILD ARTEFACT, and nothing in it is safe from a build.

    AND EVERY BUILD WIPES IT: vite.config.mjs sets `emptyOutDir: true`, so
    `npm run build` deletes the whole of dist before writing into it.

    AND NOTHING PUT THE IMAGES BACK, because there was NO frontend/public
    DIRECTORY AT ALL. Vite copies the CONTENTS of `<root>/public` into the build
    — that is its one mechanism for carrying a file through unchanged — and
    frontend/public did not exist, so frontend/images/ was never copied anywhere.

    THE RESULT: running any build produced a dist with NO images in it, the mask
    request 404'd, and the clickable region silently stopped working. Silent,
    because a missing mask is not an error the page reports — it just stops
    responding to clicks.

    AND IT WAS CAUSED HERE, by running `npm run build` while working on the
    library. Not by editing index.html, which is untouched — by REBUILDING it.

    --- THE FIX, AND WHY IT IS NOT "COPY THE FILES BACK" ---

    frontend/images/ WAS MOVED TO frontend/public/images/. That is vite's own
    mechanism rather than a workaround:

      * the contents of public/ are copied to the ROOT of the build, so
        public/images/mainAlpha.png becomes dist/images/mainAlpha.png — which is
        exactly the path index.html already asks for;
      * the dev server serves public/ at / as well, so the SAME path works in
        development and in a build, with no conditional anything.

    COPYING THE TWO FILES INTO dist/ WOULD HAVE FIXED THE SYMPTOM FOR AN HOUR.
    The next build would have deleted them again, which is the second time this
    would have happened.

    VERIFIED, not assumed: two further builds in a row, and dist/images/ is still
    populated; then the server was started and the files fetched — 200, image/png,
    and the byte counts match the originals.

    --- THE RULE, WHICH IS THE GENERAL PART ---

    ANY FILE A PAGE LOADS BY PATH RATHER THAN BY IMPORT BELONGS IN
    frontend/public/. frontend/dist is disposable; frontend/ is not.


    ============================================================
    AND IT EXPOSED A REAL ONE, 2026-10-09: THREE PAGES HAD NO THEME
    ============================================================

    THE SHOPFRONT HAD NEAR-WHITE CARDS WITH WHITE TEXT ON THEM. Reported as
    "ugly squares" and "white text on a very light almost white bg", in the
    section headed "A Seamless Open World".

    THE CAUSE, AND IT WAS THE OVERRIDE REMOVAL ABOVE THAT REVEALED IT. Only the
    five GENERATED pages had data-bs-theme="dark" — build.js writes it into them.
    THE THREE HAND-WRITTEN ONES DID NOT: index.html, shopfront.html and
    discussion.html were `<html lang="en">` and nothing else.

    THEY FAKED DARKNESS INSTEAD, with `body class="bg-dark text-light"`. That
    worked for as long as .feature-card hardcoded #212529 — and stopped the
    moment it was changed to var(--bs-secondary-bg), because ON A LIGHT-THEME
    PAGE THAT VARIABLE IS LIGHT. White text on #e9ecef.

    SO THE CHANGE WAS RIGHT AND THE PAGE WAS WRONG: it had been dark by accident
    of two hardcoded colours rather than by asking for the theme.

    --- THE FIX: GIVE ALL THREE THE THEME THEY SHOULD HAVE HAD ---

    data-bs-theme="dark" added to index.html, shopfront.html and
    discussion.html. ALL EIGHT PAGES NOW AGREE.

    AND IT IS SAFE, WHICH WAS WORTH CHECKING RATHER THAN ASSUMING: Bootstrap
    does NOT override --bs-light-rgb or --bs-dark-rgb in dark mode, so `.bg-dark`
    and `.text-light` are LITERAL COLOURS THAT DO NOT FLIP. Every `<section
    class="bg-dark">` on the shopfront stays dark, and the fix is confined to
    the things that SHOULD follow the theme — cards, borders, body colour,
    form controls.

    VERIFIED BY RENDERING AND SAMPLING, not by looking: the feature card is
    #343a40, Bootstrap's dark secondary surface, with white headings on it;
    the section around it is #212529. index.html renders on #212529, and
    discussion.html is unchanged in character.

    --- AND THE GENERAL LESSON, WHICH IS THE SAME ONE TWICE NOW ---

    A HARDCODED COLOUR HIDES A MISSING THEME. It looks correct on the page that
    set it, and it takes a change somewhere else to reveal that the page never
    asked for the theme at all. The three hand-written pages were invisible to
    every check because they render fine — right up until they do not.


76) THE LIBRARY'S LABELLING STEP — BUILT 2026-10-09. AND THE VOCABULARY
    DECISION IT LEAVES OPEN.

    THE LIBRARY WAS A PILE. A folder of reference photographs arrives as
    IMG_4823, IMG_4824, IMG_4825, and a list of those is not a library. So an
    item now carries a NAME, a NOTE and TAGS, and the search reads all three.

    THIS IS ITEM 54'S STEPS 6 AND 8 — labelling what the images depict, and
    searching by content. Item 57 records both as LIBRARY concerns, because you
    search a library for material and you do not search a finished build. The
    user asked for exactly this in the first message about images: *"there needs
    to be a system for identifying the contents e.g trees boat river"*.

    --- WHAT EXISTS ---

      * library_item_tags: one row per word per item, UNIQUE on the pair, with
        ON DELETE CASCADE so tags do not outlive their item. A TABLE rather than
        a comma-separated column, because the questions are "everything tagged
        river" and "what tags exist", and both are a query over rows.

      * POST /api/library/:id/update — name, note and tags together, because the
        dialog holds the whole edit and a partial write would be a state the form
        never showed anybody. TAGS ARE REPLACED, NOT MERGED: the form shows every
        tag an item has, so what comes back is the whole set, and merging would
        make a tag impossible to remove.

      * The tile shows the tags as Bootstrap badges and grows an EDIT button; the
        dialog is a modal, because a 150px tile is the wrong shape to type into.

      * The search reads name, note AND tags. Reading only the name would miss
        the entire point — "trees" is a word somebody typed BECAUSE it is not in
        the filename.

    --- NORMALISATION IS THE WHOLE OF THE DESIGN ---

    "Trees", "trees " and "TREES" are ONE tag. They are the same word to a person
    and three words to a search, so without normalising, an item is filed and
    then never found — AND THE FAILURE IS SILENT, which is the worst kind. Tags
    are trimmed, lowercased, inner whitespace collapsed, deduplicated and SORTED.

    THE SORTING IS ON THE SERVER, and it fixed a real inconsistency found by
    testing: the listing reads tags back with ORDER BY tag, so they come out
    alphabetical, while the update response was returning them in submission
    order. TWO ENDPOINTS, TWO ANSWERS TO THE SAME QUESTION. The test now asserts
    they agree, which is the property that actually matters.

    AND THE DIALOG PREVIEWS IT — type "Trees, river, TREES" and it shows the two
    words that will be stored. The client duplicates the server's normalisation
    for that preview ONLY; the server remains the authority.

    --- THE DECISION THIS LEAVES OPEN, WHICH IS THE NEXT ONE ---

    THERE IS NO CONTROLLED VOCABULARY, and that is deliberate rather than
    unfinished. A tag is whatever somebody typed.

    THE CASE FOR A VOCABULARY: "tree" and "trees", "wood" and "forest", "river"
    and "stream" will all get used, and a search for one will miss the others.
    THE CASE AGAINST: a vocabulary is a decision about WHO OWNS THE WORDS, and
    choosing it before seeing what people actually type is guessing. The reading
    is to leave it free until there are enough tags to see the shape of the
    problem — THE OPTION LIST CAN BE BUILT FROM THE TAGS THAT EXIST, which is a
    query this table already answers.

    --- AND WHAT IS STILL OWED ---

      * FILTERING BY TAG — BUILT 2026-10-09. See the section below. IT WAS THE
        FIRST THING ON THIS LIST, and the reason it was owed is the reason it
        exists: "show me everything tagged river" is a different question from
        "show me things with river in them", and it is the one that gets asked
        once there are hundreds of items. THE TABLE ANSWERED IT FROM THE DAY TAGS
        EXISTED; nothing offered it until now.

      * THE COLLECTION NAME — item 54's finding (A), a label over a BATCH rather
        than over one file, so a directory uploaded together can be retrieved
        together. Related but not the same as a tag.

      * AND PULLING LIBRARY MATERIAL INTO A WORKSPACE, which is item 59's
        check-out and was deliberately deferred: nothing wants it until somebody
        iterates on an approved asset.


    ============================================================
    TAG FILTERING — BUILT 2026-10-09, AND IT WAS THE FIRST THING OWED
    ============================================================

    ASKED FOR DIRECTLY: *"add tag filtering to the search"*. It is the first item
    on the "still owed" list above, and it earns its place because IT IS A
    DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM THE SEARCH BOX.

        THE SEARCH BOX IS A GUESS AT WHAT SOMEBODY MEANT. It reads the name, the
        note AND the tags, because a person typing "wales" does not know or care
        which of those it was filed under.

        THE TAG FILTER IS WHAT THE ITEM WAS FILED AS. It reads nothing else.

    So an item whose NOTE mentions a river is found by searching "river" and NOT
    by filtering for the tag `river`. That difference is the entire value of
    tagging, and collapsing the two into one box would quietly throw it away.

    BOTH APPLY AT ONCE when both are used.

    --- AND IT IS AND, NOT OR ---

    Item 54's example is "big trees with river" — the ones that are BOTH. An OR
    would answer a question nobody asked, and it would GROW as tags were added,
    which is the wrong way round: adding a condition should narrow what you are
    looking at, never widen it.

    --- WHAT WAS BUILT ---

      * A FILTER ROW under the search: every tag in the library as a button with
        its count, most-used first. BUTTONS RATHER THAN BADGES, deliberately — a
        badge on a tile is a LABEL and this is a CONTROL, and they should not
        look the same when they do different things. `aria-pressed` carries the
        state, because a colour is not something anybody can hear.

      * Selected tags are filled, unselected are outlined, and a Clear link
        appears once anything is on.

      * THE COUNT LINE SAYS WHY IT IS NARROWED — "1 of 4 — by the search and 1
        tag". A filter that is on looks exactly like a library that has lost
        things, otherwise.

      * And the empty-result message names the filter that emptied it, for the
        same reason: "Nothing matches" with an empty search box reads as a broken
        page rather than as a filter doing its job.

      * A tag that stops existing — its last item removed or relabelled — is
        DROPPED from the selection on reload, or it would filter everything away
        and look like an empty library.

    --- AND THE RULE WAS EXTRACTED SO IT COULD BE TESTED ---

    THE FILTERING RULE NOW LIVES IN frontend/src/libraryFilter.mjs, on its own,
    as a PURE FUNCTION. It was three lines inside render() — the kind of three
    lines that look obvious and are not, because there are two filters, they mean
    different things, they combine, and the combining rule is what a later edit
    gets wrong.

    IT IS `.mjs`, ALONE AMONG THE FRONTEND'S FILES, AND THAT IS THE POINT:
    package.json declares "type": "commonjs", so a .js file containing `export`
    is parsed as CommonJS by node. The other frontend modules are only ever
    loaded by vite, which does not care. This one is a pure rule with no imports,
    so NODE CAN LOAD IT AND THE RULE CAN BE TESTED BY CALLING IT — and `.mjs` is
    what makes that possible.

    SIXTEEN CASES PASS, covering the distinction between the two filters, the AND
    behaviour, case and whitespace tolerance, both filters at once, and items
    with no tags or no note.

    --- WHAT IS STILL OWED ---

    TWO OF THE THREE ABOVE REMAIN: item 54's collection name over a batch, and
    item 59's check-out into a workspace.

    AND THIS NOTE USED TO SAY "the list above loses its first item", WHICH WAS
    WRONG IN A PARTICULARLY USELESS WAY: it ANNOUNCED THE REMOVAL OF A BULLET
    WITHOUT REMOVING IT. The list went on claiming tag filtering was outstanding
    while the section directly below said it was built, so the item contradicted
    itself — which is the exact failure this file keeps recording in other
    people's entries, committed here in the middle of recording it.

    A NOTE THAT DESCRIBES AN EDIT IS NOT THE EDIT. If a list is wrong, THE LIST
    IS WHAT GETS FIXED: a correction written beside the thing it corrects leaves
    two answers and no way to tell which is current.


77) NO LOCKING — TELL PEOPLE INSTEAD. 2026-10-09.

    THE USER'S ARGUMENT, VERBATIM: *"If I am working on bigTree and have the sam
    area of game and somone else has the same area but is working on house why
    can't that happen - it should if there are collisions - the devs should be
    told about it on the slack like BB - direct message -maybe"*. And then:
    *"So we can have multiple people possibly working on the same area of game
    just need to communicate to avoid collisions"*.

    --- THEY ARE RIGHT, AND IT CORRECTS ITEM 62 ---

    TWO DEVELOPERS IN THE SAME AREA WORKING ON DIFFERENT OBJECTS IS NOT A
    COLLISION. One File Per Actor means each actor INSTANCE is its own file on
    disk — item 63 found exactly this, and OFPA exists precisely so that this is
    fine. bigTree and house are different files.

    SO LOCKING EVERYTHING PUNISHES THE COMMON CASE TO PROTECT THE RARE ONE, and
    item 62's line — that LOCKING is "the property that decides it" — was too
    strong. Locking is for the rare case: THE SAME ACTOR.

    AND HANDLING THAT CASE SOCIALLY IS CHEAPER THAN LOCKING. Detect it, tell the
    two people, let them sort it out.

    --- AND IT REOPENS A QUESTION ITEM 62 CLOSED ---

    Item 62 ruled out the portal holding the game partly BECAUSE a
    content-addressed store cannot lock, and content addressing is
    merge-by-content. IF COLLISIONS ARE HANDLED BY TELLING PEOPLE RATHER THAN BY
    PREVENTING THEM, THAT ARGUMENT LOSES MOST OF ITS FORCE.

    SO ITEM 62'S CONCLUSION MAY STILL STAND, BUT IT NOW RESTS ON DIFFERENT
    GROUND — and the question the user asked two turns earlier, "can the
    workspace page just fetch the stuff for me", is OPEN RATHER THAN ANSWERED.
    It was ruled out on a premise that has just been removed.

    THE READING: the two wants are the SAME DECISION. If the portal fetches the
    files, IT KNOWS WHO IS HOLDING WHAT, and detecting "you and Sam both have
    actor 4821" is free. If Perforce stays the client, the portal has to ASK
    Perforce — an integration with the system item 67 said not to couple to.

    --- WHERE THE TELLING GOES, AND A CORRECTION ---

    *"bb is our slack bot, it already messages us"* — AND THAT IS NOT WHAT BB IS.
    This file defines it at line 3996: **"BB" IS THE DISCUSSION BOARD, ITEM 27:
    SELF-HOSTED MATRIX/ELEMENT, DECIDED BUT NOT BUILT.** There is a
    discussion.html stub and nothing behind it. Reading it as a Slack bot was a
    mistake made here, in conversation, and corrected by grepping this file —
    which is what the file is for.

    WHAT MATTERS IS THAT IT CHANGES NOTHING ABOUT THE PLAN AND ALMOST EVERYTHING
    ABOUT THE DEPENDENCY:

      * IT HAS AN API — but it is MATRIX's, via Synapse, not Slack's. Synapse was
        chosen partly FOR this: item 27 says "the admin API and OIDC/SSO — the
        features the portal actually wants — exist only in Synapse".
      * SO THIS NOTIFICATION DEPENDS ON ITEM 27 BEING BUILT, which is decided and
        not started. A collision message has nowhere to go today.
      * AND IT FITS ITEM 27'S OWN PURPOSE. Its stated point is INTEGRATION, not
        just jurisdiction: the portal already intends to create and deactivate
        Matrix users. A message from the portal to a Matrix DM is the same
        relationship, used one more way.

    --- AND WHAT A COLLISION MESSAGE WOULD ACTUALLY SAY ---

    Not recorded in detail, because it depends on the state question above. What
    it must carry either way: WHICH ACTOR, WHO ELSE HAS IT, and WHEN THEY TOOK
    IT. "You and Sam both have the North Forest lighting actor, and Sam took it
    two hours ago" is enough for two adults to sort it out; anything more is
    decoration.

    AND IT IS NOT URGENT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOT notify.js. That file is email
    to EVERY ADMIN, for things like a disk at 95%. This is a chat message to TWO
    DEVELOPERS. Different audience, different channel, different weight, and
    bolting it onto the urgent path would make both worse.


78) THE SPECS AND THE TODO HAVE DRIFTED APART. AUDITED 2026-10-09.

    ASKED: *"how much of this is in our todo and spec sheets"* — after a long
    conversation about getting a piece of the game onto a developer's machine,
    which turned out to be largely re-deriving things already written down.

    --- WHAT WAS AUDITED, AND WHAT COULD NOT BE ---

    READ: docs/assettypes01 (in full), docs/Specification_v04_web.html (sections
    02, 03 and 04), and the headers of docs/dbspec01, docs/uploadmodel01 and
    docs/discussionboard01.

    NOT READ, AND THIS IS PART OF THE FINDING: THE THREE PDFs —
    "OpenWorld Asset Library", "OpenWorld Asset Repository & Development System",
    "OpenWorld Asset System". They are the ORIGINAL specs (Aug 10) and among the
    largest documents in docs/. THERE IS NO TEXT EXTRACTOR ON THIS MACHINE
    (no poppler, no pdftotext), and the streams are font and image data, so a
    naive decompression yields noise rather than prose.

    SO THIS AUDIT IS PARTIAL BY CONSTRUCTION. Three specs nobody has compared
    against anything are sitting in docs/ — and docs/SuperUserSpecs (277 KB) and
    docs/Specification_v04_web.html have not been read end to end either.

    --- WHAT TODAY'S DISCUSSION WAS ALREADY SPECCED BY ---

    MOST OF IT. docs/assettypes01 §5B is headed DOWNLOAD TO LOCAL WORKING COPY,
    marked SMALL and "HIGHEST VALUE, LOWEST RISK", and describes:

      * "ZIP preserving relative_path (which exists in the schema for exactly
        this) plus a JSON manifest: per-file SHA-256, size, and target path."
        THAT IS THE STAGING VIEW AND THE HAVE/WANT SUBSTRATE, ALREADY DESIGNED.
      * "The approver has a working copy of the game, so files must land in the
        right places." — the mapping problem, named.
      * "A small CLI later lets an artist sync by hash instead of
        re-downloading." — THE HASH NEGOTIATION, as a known later step.
      * AND THE SECURITY, worked out: octet-stream never the stored mime type,
        the storage key validated as exactly 64 hex, relative_path re-validated
        at zip time because it originated client-side and is being used to build
        entry names.

    SO THE CHECK-OUT WORK IS NOT NEW. It is item 59 plus a spec section written
    on 2026-09-29, and the conversation spent an hour arriving where the file
    already was.

    --- AND THREE PLACES THE SPECS AND THE TODO CONTRADICT EACH OTHER ---

    1. WHERE THE GAME LIVES. — RESOLVED 2026-10-09. THE SPEC IS STALE.
       SPEC (Specification_v04_web.html §03): *"All game source code, compiled
       binaries, raw assets, website files, and documentation reside in a central
       private GitHub repository (using Git LFS for binary assets)."* ONE REPO,
       EVERYTHING IN IT.
       TODO (items 62, 73): PERFORCE for the game, git for the website, separate.
       THE USER, 2026-10-09: *"The game does not live on github"*. SO THE SPEC'S
       ONE-REPO MODEL IS OUT OF DATE AND ITEMS 62 AND 73 STAND.
       AND IT IS WORTH SAYING WHY IT READ AS A LIVE CONTRADICTION RATHER THAN AN
       OLD DOCUMENT: the spec was written on Aug 10, before Perforce was ever
       considered, AND NOTHING IN IT WAS MARKED SUPERSEDED. A document describing
       a world that has since changed, with no note saying so, reads as a
       COMPETING DECISION rather than an EARLIER ONE — which is precisely how it
       was read here, and how it would be read by anybody else.
       WHICH IS THE SAME FAILURE THIS WHOLE ITEM IS ABOUT, ONE LEVEL UP: the
       record not being re-read, and the older layers not saying they are older.

    2. recommended_version_id. — RESOLVED 2026-10-09. THE SPEC WAS RIGHT.
       SPEC (assettypes01 §3.1): removing it WAS WRONG, and it must be restored
       — "recommended and release membership are different things, both coexist".
       TODO (item 53): the removal is the settled decision.
       THE SPEC WINS. It was not that item 53 misread anything; it inherited a
       line of reasoning that was simply wrong, explained below.

    3. STORAGE. — NOT A CONTRADICTION. TEMPORARY, TO BE DECIDED.
       SPEC: Cloudflare R2. CODE: local disk, backend/uploads/files/.
       THE USER, 2026-10-09: *"storage is temp TBD"*. SO THIS IS A KNOWN
       DEFERRAL, NOT A DRIFT — the spec describes where the blobs are going,
       and local disk is where they live until that is decided.
       CORRECTED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT AS A CONTRADICTION, because calling a
       deferral a conflict devalues the two above it. The one-repo reversal and
       the recommended_version_id clash are DECISIONS THAT DISAGREE; this is a
       decision not yet taken. Listing them together made the real ones look
       like housekeeping.
       AND IT STILL INTERACTS WITH SIZE: object storage is what a terabyte
       wants, so if the portal ever holds the project this stops being optional
       (item 77's neighbours).

    --- WHAT IS IN THE SPECS AND NOT IN THE TODO AT ALL ---

      * "SEAMLESS SSO HANDOFF" (Specification_v04 §03): *"Logged-in web portal
        contributors pass seamlessly into GitHub without requiring secondary
        authentication."* THE USER'S "I DON'T WANT TO LOG INTO ANOTHER SITE" IS
        ALREADY IN THE SPEC. It was treated, in conversation, as a new
        requirement. It is not — it is an unbuilt one.
      * IMAGE TO 3D RECONSTRUCTION — vision tagging, CLIP/SigLIP embeddings in
        Qdrant or pgvector, and Trellis/Rodin on serverless GPU. An entire
        pipeline stage, specced in detail, absent from the TODO.
      * THE VETTING GATE — polygon limits, texture dimensions, naming
        conventions, format compliance.
      * A CONTROL FILE THE REPOSITORY NEVER AUTO-MODIFIES.
      * DEPENDENCY DISCOVERY, and reporting a missing dependency when identical
        content exists elsewhere by SHA.

    --- AND WHAT IS IN NEITHER ---

      * THE SERVER-SIDE HAVE-LIST — that the Perforce server already holds what
        each workspace has, so the portal can produce the sync list WITHOUT
        anything running on the developer's machine. Worked out in conversation
        on 2026-10-09 and written down nowhere until now (item 77's neighbour).
      * AND "ONE LOGIN" AS A STATED REQUIREMENT, which the spec has as a feature
        and the TODO has never had as a constraint.

    --- WHAT TO DO, AND THE ORDER ---

      1. DECIDE THE THREE CONTRADICTIONS, each as its own question: is the
         one-repo model dead, is recommended_version_id restored or the spec
         corrected, is storage staying local.
      2. GET THE PDFs READABLE. A spec nobody can read is not a spec. Either
         pdftotext in the shell (poppler), or convert them once and keep the
         text beside them.
      3. THEN AUDIT WHAT IS LEFT. Three unread PDFs plus two unread text specs,
         against 78 items of decisions taken since — the drift is only going to
         be worse in the parts nobody has looked at.

    ============================================================
    AND CONTRADICTION 2 CLOSED: THE RECOMMENDED VERSION IS RESTORED
    ============================================================

    THE ARGUMENT THAT RETIRED IT, from db.js (commit 5d3d24f, 2026-09-29):

        "A single 'recommended version' pointer cannot express the intended
         model -- an asset can hold several production versions at once, and
         production status is derived from release membership, not from a
         pointer."

    IT IS PERFECTLY TRUE THAT A POINTER CANNOT EXPRESS RELEASES. IT DOES NOT
    HAVE TO, BECAUSE THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS:

        release membership      what went into which named build, and when
        a recommended version   which version to use by default, right now

    AND WITHOUT IT, "WHICH VERSION DO I USE?" HAS EXACTLY ONE POSSIBLE ANSWER —
    THE NEWEST — which is not always the right one.

    --- A TABLE, NOT A COLUMN, WHICH IS ONE DEPARTURE FROM THE SPEC ---

    The spec says restore `recommended_version_id` on `assets`. THE TABLE
    ASSET_RECOMMENDED_VERSIONS DOES THE SAME JOB AND CONSTRAINS IT: its
    composite foreign key proves the recommended version BELONGS TO THIS ASSET,
    because asset_versions carries UNIQUE (id, asset_id) for exactly that
    purpose. A bare integer column cannot say that, and a pointer to another
    asset's version is a state nothing would catch.

    THE TABLE ALSO ALREADY EXISTED — the same commit removed the column and kept
    the table, "so the schema change is reviewable on its own". So this is an
    UN-RETIREMENT rather than an addition, and TWO MECHANISMS FOR ONE FACT WOULD
    BE WORSE THAN EITHER. The spec's argument is about the FACT, not the column.

    db.js now carries the whole of this reasoning where the table is declared,
    including the reasoning that was wrong — so the next person to consider
    retiring it has to argue with it rather than rediscover it.

    --- AND WHAT IS STILL OPEN, WHICH IS THE NEXT DECISION ---

    NOTHING WRITES IT. That is not an oversight and it is not finished: it is
    the same shape of question item 53 already carries about releases.

        WHO RECOMMENDS A VERSION, and does creative approval do it by default?

    The case for approval doing it: a version that has just been approved is
    usually the one to use, and asking somebody to say so twice is a step that
    will be skipped. The case against: approval means "good enough for the
    game", which is not the same as "use this one now", and the whole reason the
    pointer exists is that those can differ.

    NOT DECIDED, and it should be decided with item 53's "who manages releases",
    because they are the same person doing two halves of one job.

    AND THE GENERAL POINT, WHICH IS WHY THIS ITEM EXISTS: THE TODO HAS BEEN
    TREATED AS THE DECISION RECORD AND THE SPECS AS HISTORY. They are not
    history — assettypes01 is dated 2026-09-29 and is more specific about the
    check-out than anything written since. A decision record that never re-reads
    its own inputs is just a longer way of forgetting.


79) THE DEVELOPER PIPELINE, END TO END. FOR REVIEW. 2026-10-09.

    ASKED FOR AT THE END OF A LONG SESSION, to be gone through again together:
    *"Can we go through the whole pipeline for the dev again - uploading - put in
    game - get the piece of game we need to work on - download it and the assets
    we want to work on from unreal and the asset store, make sure that we are not
    downloading something already there - check with perforce or hashes or both -
    TBD - work on the asstes locally - upload into the workspace and after
    aproval replace the old asset with the new in the game"*.

    THIS ITEM IS THE AGENDA FOR THAT CONVERSATION. Nothing in it is new; it is
    the whole thing in one place, with each step marked by what exists.

    IT TOUCHES TWO SPACES, and the seam between them is where most of the
    unbuilt work sits:

        THE PORTAL   the workspace, the store, the library, the records
        PERFORCE     the game — the Unreal project

    ============================================================
    A — MAKING AN ASSET
    ============================================================

      A1  work locally in Blender / Maya / Nuke
          NOT THE PORTAL'S BUSINESS, and should not become so.

      A2  log into the portal                                        BUILT

      A3  upload the directory to your workspace                     BUILT
          THE TYPE QUESTION (item 56) DECIDES WHAT THIS IS. A Blender
          project is a LOAD-BEARING TREE — the app resolves its own files
          by relative path — so it is ONE asset and the tree is preserved.
          Loose files are MANY library items instead. THIS ASK IS NOT
          BUILT, and it is the front door of the whole thing.

      A4  technical check                                            BUILT

      A5  submit for creative approval                               BUILT

      A6  prod approves — the asset is now in the STORE              BUILT

    ============================================================
    B — INTO THE GAME
    ============================================================

      B1  somebody imports the approved asset into Unreal
          A PERSON, DELIBERATELY. Unreal imports; the portal does not and
          cannot (items 61, 69).

      B2  commit that to Perforce
          PERFORCE. The imported .uasset is the game's own copy, in the
          game's own format — not a copy of the store's file (item 69).

      B3  the portal records WHICH CHANGELIST did it              NOT BUILT
          One field and a person, item 67. It is the only link between
          the store and the game, and it is the answer to "what created
          this in Unreal".

    ============================================================
    C — GETTING A PIECE OF THE GAME BACK
    ============================================================

      C1  log into the portal                                        BUILT

      C2  say which bit of the game                               NOT BUILT
          "The North Forest", meaning their folder, the landscape and
          the lighting layer. PERFORCE DECIDES WHAT COMES DOWN (the
          workspace mapping); UNREAL DECIDES WHAT IS LOADED (World
          Partition, Data Layers, Location Volumes). Two filters, and
          item 66's trap is that they must agree.

      C3  SEE THE LIST BEFORE DOWNLOADING                         NOT BUILT
          The staging view, item 59 — and ALREADY SPECCED in
          docs/assettypes01 §5B as a "JSON manifest: per-file SHA-256,
          size, and target path".

      C4  SKIP WHAT IS ALREADY LOCAL                        ** TBD **
          THE USER'S OWN MARKER: *"check with perforce or hashes or
          both - TBD"*. THE TWO ANSWERS:
            * PERFORCE — the server already holds what each workspace
              has, so the portal can produce the list WITHOUT anything
              running on the developer's machine. Nothing to hash.
            * HASHES — the portal's store is already SHA-based, and a
              client could hash the local folder. But only a client can
              see your disk, and hashing a terabyte is slow.
          NOT DECIDED, AND IT IS THE FIRST THING TO SETTLE, because it
          decides whether anything has to run on the dev's machine.

      C5  download the ASSET from the store                       NOT BUILT
          Item 59's check-out — the half that does not exist. The
          upload half works; nothing brings anything back.

      C6  download the GAME SUBSET                                NOT BUILT
          AND UNDECIDED: PERFORCE, or the portal serving it. Item 77
          records the argument — the user removed the locking premise
          that had ruled the portal out, and having the portal serve the
          files would make C4 free, because it would already know who
          holds what.

    ============================================================
    D — WORKING
    ============================================================

      D1  open the .blend in BLENDER
          No portal involvement.

      D2  see it in context — UNREAL, with the landscape and lighting
          Needs C6. AND NOTE THE SEPARATION: the thing you EDIT is
          Blender's; the thing you LOOK AT IT IN is Unreal's.

    ============================================================
    E — BACK
    ============================================================

      E1  upload the new version — bigTree_v06                        BUILT
          `bigTree_v06` parses to asset `bigTree`, version 6. The store
          knows it is a LATER VERSION OF THE SAME ASSET, not a new one
          (item 54's naming model). This only works if the developer
          names it consistently — `bigTreeFinal` would be a new asset and
          nothing would know it replaces anything.

      E2  technical check                                            BUILT

      E3  creative approval                                          BUILT

      E4  put it into the game, replacing the old one              NOT BUILT
          AND IT IS LESS THAN IT SOUNDS: a person REIMPORTS over the
          same asset, and EVERY PLACED TREE UPDATES. You do not delete
          anything and place it again. What is missing is only the
          RECORD — that changelist 15407 replaced v3 with v6.

    ============================================================
    THE OPEN DECISIONS, IN THE ORDER THEY BLOCK THINGS
    ============================================================

      1. PERFORCE OR HASHES FOR "WHAT HAVE I GOT" (C4). Decides whether a
         dev's machine runs anything.
      2. PERFORCE OR THE PORTAL FOR THE GAME'S FILES (C6). Decides whether
         the portal ever holds the project — item 62's decision, reopened
         by item 77.
      3. THE UPLOAD TYPE ASK (A3, item 56). The front door.
      4. WHO RECOMMENDS A VERSION, and who manages releases (item 78,
         item 53).
      5. AND THE THREE THINGS STILL UNAUDITED: the three PDF specs, which
         cannot be read without a text extractor.


80) RECONCILE THE THREE PDF SPECS AGAINST THE TODO. Deferred 2026-10-15.

    WHAT: docs/OpenWorld Asset Library.pdf, docs/OpenWorld Asset Repository &
    Development System.pdf, docs/OpenWorld Asset System.pdf — the ORIGINAL specs,
    dated Aug 10, now in the repository (they were excluded by a docs/*.pdf rule
    until 2026-10-15, which is why nobody had read them).

    HOW: AS A COMPARISON, NOT AS A SOURCE. THEY PREDATE THE GAME BEING NAMED,
    THEY PREDATE PERFORCE, AND THEY PREDATE NEARLY EVERY DECISION IN THIS FILE.
    So the useful output is not "what the specs say" but WHERE THEY DISAGREE WITH
    THIS FILE — and this file wins by default, UNLESS THE SPEC'S REASONING IS
    BETTER, which is exactly what happened with recommended_version_id (item 78):
    the TODO had inherited a wrong argument and the spec said so.

    AND THE RISK IS THE OTHER WAY: READING AN OLD DESIGN AND QUIETLY TREATING IT
    AS CURRENT. That is the mistake that produced item 78 in the first place.

    THEY ARE NOW READABLE — poppler_utils was added to shell.nix so `pdftotext`
    exists. Note the attribute is `pkgs."poppler-utils"`, hyphenated and quoted.

    --- THE TWO THINGS ALREADY SEEN, TO CHECK RATHER THAN ADOPT ---

      1. "PUBLISHED" IS A STAGE THIS SYSTEM DOES NOT HAVE. The spec's flow is
         CREATE -> PRIVATE -> PUBLISHED -> SUBMIT FOR GAME -> TECHNICAL TEST ->
         DIRECTOR APPROVAL -> GAME APPROVED, with the rule stated in bold:
         "Publishing an asset does not put it in the game."

         A PUBLISHED version is FIXED, VISIBLE TO COLLEAGUES, downloadable,
         discussable, and USABLE AS A STARTING POINT — and explicitly NOT
         approved. "If they want to change it, they create their own version."

         THIS SYSTEM JUMPS FROM THE PRIVATE WORKSPACE STRAIGHT TO SUBMIT FOR
         APPROVAL. Whether the middle step was decided against, or never
         considered, is NOT KNOWN. It is a genuine question: publishing for
         feedback without implying approval is a different thing from submitting
         for the game, and the spec says "there is no requirement to submit every
         published asset for game approval".

      2. THE NAME COLLIDES. The spec's "Asset Library" holds EVERYTHING —
         "from a photograph used as reference to a finished Unreal asset". THIS
         FILE'S LIBRARY (item 57) IS SOURCE MATERIAL ONLY, and item 58 says an
         image is never a game asset. TWO DIFFERENT THINGS CALLED THE LIBRARY,
         and the spec's is the broader one. Worth knowing before anybody reads
         the PDF and "fixes" the library to match it.

    --- AND ONE THING THE SPEC SAYS THAT THIS FILE ALREADY AGREES WITH ---

      * "Developers continue to do their actual work on their own computers. The
        Asset Library is where useful versions are uploaded when they need to be
        ... The system does not require every change to be uploaded."
        That is the round trip's premise, stated in Aug, and it holds.

      * "Your private versions are visible to: You - Admin - Superuser."
        Superuser is gone (item 51); admin is the equivalent now. Stale in the
        detail, right in the shape.


80b) AND THE STORE IS INVISIBLE, NOW CONFIRMED THE HARD WAY. 2026-10-15.

    THIS ITEM ALREADY EXISTED — item 25 is "browse the game store, to reuse
    approved assets", and the developer page even carries the stub tab for it
    ("Game store", data-todo="25"). Recorded here because it stopped being a
    design question and became an experience:

        "The version is now in the game asset store. But there should be a
         mechanism to view the assets."

    THE FIRST ASSET EVER APPROVED INTO THE STORE ARRIVED SOMEWHERE NOBODY COULD
    LOOK AT IT. The approval worked, the files are there, the provenance is
    right — and the only way to know any of that is to read the database. The
    developer's own page shows their WORKSPACE and not the store, and no page
    anywhere shows the store's contents to anybody.

    SO THE CONSUMPTION SIDE IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT REMAINS OF THE ROUND TRIP. The
    producer side now runs end to end: upload, check, submit, approve, stored,
    provenance recorded, one copy of every byte. Nothing consumes it.

    --- AND ONE OBSERVATION FROM THE SAME RUN, NOT A COMPLAINT ---

    THE ASSET THAT WENT THROUGH WAS A FOLDER OF LOOSE IMAGES. Item 58 says an
    image is not a game asset and belongs in the library; item 57 says the same.
    Nothing in the pipeline ENFORCES that — an image uploaded as a workspace asset
    is checked, submitted, approved and stored like anything else.

    WHICH MAY BE CORRECT: a texture IS a game asset, and the store's job is not
    to police what a developer calls a unit of game content. But the two rules
    (item 58's "images are not assets" and the pipeline's silence on the subject)
    have never been put next to each other, and this is the first time the system
    has had an actual image in it to disagree about.


81) THE CONTAINER NAME IS IN THE PATHS, AND ITEM 54 SAYS IT SHOULD NOT BE.
    Found 2026-10-15, building the store download.

    WHAT WAS SEEN. The manifest of the first downloaded version:

        TestUpload/9e90a994-93ae-438f-b4a4-9d27071d64a0.jpg
        TestUpload/be4ab03cef89-Cheryl_Rose12100x800.webp
        TestUpload/artPics/IMG20250811222739.jpg

    THE FOLDER THE FILES WERE UPLOADED IN IS THE FIRST SEGMENT OF EVERY PATH.

    AND ITEM 54 DECIDED THE OPPOSITE, in as many words:

        THE CONTAINER NAME   the folder or zip the files arrived in. TRANSPORT.
                             It names nothing and is never retained in the
                             workspace, the store or a listing.

    So either the code does not do what item 54 says, or item 54 describes an
    intention that was never implemented. CHECKED: the `relative_path` column
    holds `TestUpload`, so the container IS retained, and the archive faithfully
    reproduces it — which is the archive doing the right thing with the wrong
    input.

    --- AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUSLY WRONG, WHICH IS WHY IT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN
        FIXED ---

    FOR A BLENDER OR NUKE PROJECT, THE TREE IS LOAD-BEARING (item 56): the
    application resolves its own files by relative path, so the tree must survive
    intact — and its ROOT is the project directory, which is exactly what was
    uploaded. Stripping it would break the project.

    FOR A LOOSE COLLECTION, THE FOLDER IS CONVENIENCE AND NOTHING MORE — item 54's
    own words — and keeping it means every file carries a name nobody chose, from
    a folder that was merely where somebody happened to keep them.

    SO THE TWO CASES WANT OPPOSITE ANSWERS, AND THE CODE DOES NOT DISTINGUISH
    THEM. THAT IS ITEM 56 AGAIN: the upload type decides whether a tree is
    load-bearing, and until it is asked, neither the upload nor the download can
    know what to keep.

    WHICH MAKES THE UPLOAD TYPE ASK (item 56) A PREREQUISITE FOR GETTING THIS
    RIGHT, rather than a separate piece of the pipeline. It was already the
    keystone; this is a second thing standing on it.

    AND ONE PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE EITHER WAY: whatever is decided, the MANIFEST
    already says where every file goes, so a developer can see the paths before
    unpacking and the decision can change without changing the format.


82) THE UPLOAD HAD THE SAME BUG. FIXED 2026-10-15.

    WHAT WAS FOUND, chasing how a developer could have a workspace asset in the
    bin at all. THE FIX FOR THE IMPORT IS IN (item 81's neighbour); THIS IS THE
    ROUTE THAT DOES NOT HAVE IT YET.

    THE SHARED MISTAKE: **A SOFT-DELETED ROW STILL HOLDS ITS NAME.** The UNIQUE
    constraint is on `(workspace_id, name)` and does not mention `is_active`, so
    an asset in the bin is still holding the name it was deleted under.

    WHAT THE UPLOAD DOES ABOUT IT: IT FINDS THE DELETED ROW AND ADDS A VERSION TO
    IT — AND NEVER SETS is_active BACK TO 1.

        workspaceAssetByName   SELECT ... WHERE workspace_id = ? AND name = ?
                               (no is_active filter — so it finds the bin row)
        if (!asset) { INSERT }  (so it does not insert)
        else { COALESCE metadata }
                               (and never restores)

    SO: A DEVELOPER UPLOADS A NEW VERSION OF SOMETHING THEY DELETED, THE UPLOAD
    SUCCEEDS, THE FILES ARE STORED AND CHARGED TO THEIR QUOTA — AND NOTHING
    APPEARS, because the asset it was added to is still invisible.

    THAT IS SILENT LOSS, and it is worse than the import's error. The import at
    least FAILED LOUDLY with a 500; this one reports success and the work is
    nowhere. It is the same class of failure as the alpha mask and the .gitkeep
    directory: correct-looking, and invisible until somebody goes looking.

    --- AND IT IS NOT KNOWN HOW EASILY IT IS REACHED, WHICH IS THE POINT ---

    It needs a name to be BOTH in the bin AND uploaded to again, and the bin is
    swept on a retention window — so it is reachable for as long as the bin holds
    the row, and unreachable after. NOBODY HAS HIT IT, and it was found by
    reading rather than by doing.

    --- WHAT TO DO ---

    THE SAME CHOICE AS THE IMPORT, and it should be made the same way:
    RESTORE THE ROW AND ADD THE VERSION. The developer has just uploaded work
    under that name; they plainly want it. The bin's own Restore sets is_active
    back, so the action exists — the upload simply never takes it.

    AND THE GENERAL RULE, since this is now twice: ANY LOOKUP THAT DECIDES
    WHETHER TO INSERT MUST SEE THE ROWS THAT WOULD COLLIDE WITH THE INSERT. A
    filter that hides them turns a constraint violation into either a crash or,
    worse, a silent one.

    ============================================================
    FIXED THE SAME DAY
    ============================================================

    THE UPLOAD NOW RESTORES THE ROW, for the reason the import does: the
    developer has just uploaded work under that name, so they plainly want it,
    and the row is the thing that holds the name. Its versions come back with it,
    and the new version is added on top. The restore is audited as
    `workspace_asset.restore` with the reason `uploaded to again`, so a restore
    by upload is distinguishable from one done in the bin.

    VERIFIED AGAINST THE CASE: upload, delete it into the bin, upload again.
    Before, the second upload reported success and the work was invisible. Now
    the asset is visible, BOTH VERSIONS ARE VISIBLE, and the audit log says why.
    The identical file twice still costs one blob, because it always did — the
    one-copy rule was never in question here, only the visibility of the row the
    version was attached to.

    THE TWO FIXES ARE DELIBERATELY THE SAME SHAPE, in two files, because the two
    routes asked the same question and got it wrong the same way. If a third
    route ever looks up a workspace asset by name, it wants the same answer.

83) CAN 'prod' READ THE GAME STORE AND THE LIBRARY? PROBABLY YES, FOR THE READ
    SIDE ONLY. DECIDE LATER.
    Raised 2026-10-15, walking the download flow.

    WHAT WAS SEEN. A session authenticated as prod, asking the game store for a
    version's archive:

        403  {"error":"Unauthorized: insufficient permissions."}

    WHICH IS requireRole DOING EXACTLY WHAT IT SAYS. Every route in
    backend/routes/game.js and every route in backend/routes/library.js is gated
    identically —

        requireRole('developer', 'admin')

    — and prod is not in that list. THE REFUSAL IS CORRECT AS WRITTEN. This item
    is about whether the list is right, not about a fault in the gate.

    ============================================================
    THE LADDER IS NOT THE REASON, AND THAT IS THE PART THAT MISLEADS
    ============================================================

    prod RANKS ABOVE DEVELOPER. roles.js has user 0, developer 1, prod 2,
    admin 3. So "prod cannot do what a developer can" reads like a bug the first
    time it is hit.

    IT IS NOT. requireRole checks MEMBERSHIP of the list it was handed, never
    rank — a route that says developer and admin means those two strings and
    nothing else. roles.js says why that is deliberate:

        'prod' IS NOT A SENIORITY AND INHERITS NOTHING. Its rank is here for the
        few questions that genuinely are about order — who sorts above whom in a
        list — and NOT as a way to grant it powers. It has no workspace, no
        admin powers, and cannot deactivate or invite anyone. Everything it may
        do comes from the capability map in backend/lib/capabilities.js.

    SO THIS IS A CAPABILITY TO GRANT ON PURPOSE, which is why it was recorded
    rather than quietly widened when the 403 turned up. A rank that silently
    confers powers is the thing that comment exists to prevent.

    --- AND THE MAP IT NAMES DOES NOT EXIST ---

    THERE IS NO backend/lib/capabilities.js. backend/lib holds fourteen files
    and that is not one of them. prod's capabilities, as the code actually
    stands, are the three explicit gates and nothing else:

        GET  /api/developer/creative-approvals                  requireRole('prod')
        POST /api/developer/creative-approvals/:id/approve      requireRole('prod')
        POST /api/developer/creative-approvals/:id/reject       requireRole('prod')
        GET  /prod                                              requireRole('prod')

    So either the comment should name what is really there, or the file it
    promises should be written. This is the small end of item 78 — a reference
    to a mechanism that is not in the repository — and it is recorded here
    because it is the file that would answer THIS item's question.

    ============================================================
    WHAT prod DOES TODAY, WHICH IS THE ARGUMENT FOR YES
    ============================================================

    prod IS THE PRODUCTION REVIEWER. A creative approval IS a developer's
    workspace version awaiting a decision — creative_approvals carries
    workspace_asset_id and workspace_version_id — and approving it is the act
    that PUTS THAT VERSION INTO THE GAME STORE, because the decision writes
    repository_asset_id and repository_version_id. Item 51 rebuilt the queue
    around exactly that.

    SO prod DECIDES WHAT ENTERS THE STORE, AND CANNOT SEE WHAT IS IN IT.

    That is the whole case, and it is a strong one: reviewing is reading, and
    the person deciding on a submission is the person most likely to want to
    compare it against what is already published.

    ============================================================
    WHY IT IS STILL A DECISION AND NOT A ONE-LINE FIX
    ============================================================

    GRANTING IT IS NOT AN EDIT TO ONE LIST. The two spaces ask the same question
    and would have to answer it together:

        routes/game.js      8 routes, all requireRole('developer','admin')
        routes/library.js   6 routes, all requireRole('developer','admin')

    AND THEY ARE NOT ALL THE SAME KIND OF ROUTE. THE READ SIDE IS WHAT A
    REVIEWER NEEDS:

        GET  /                                     list and search
        GET  /:assetId                             one asset
        GET  /:assetId/versions/:versionId/manifest
        GET  /:assetId/versions/:versionId/files/:sha256
        GET  /:assetId/versions/:versionId/download            the whole version
        POST /:assetId/versions/:versionId/archive             the difference
        POST /:assetId/versions/:versionId/downloaded          the record of a read

    AND THE WRITE SIDE IS NOT:

        POST /:assetId/versions/:versionId/import   writes repository_asset_id
                                                    onto a WORKSPACE asset — and
                                                    prod HAS NO WORKSPACE, so
                                                    this is meaningless for prod
                                                    rather than merely unwanted
        the library's upload, update and remove     authoring, which prod does not do

    --- AND THE METHOD IS NOT THE LINE, WHICH IS THE TRAP ---

    THE OBVIOUS LAZY RULE IS "GRANT THE GETs", AND IT IS WRONG IN BOTH
    DIRECTIONS:

      * POST .../archive IS A READ. It is a POST only because the list of
        SHA-256s does not fit in a URL — the method is a transport decision and
        not a permission statement. Granting the GETs and stopping would refuse
        prod the one route the negotiation actually uses.
      * POST .../downloaded IS PART OF READING, being the record that a read
        happened.

    SO THE ANSWER, WHEN IT IS MADE, WANTS TO BE A NAMED CAPABILITY — "may read
    the store" — rather than 'prod' added by hand to two lists of route
    handlers. Otherwise the next route somebody writes is gated by habit, and
    the answer drifts without anybody deciding it.

    ============================================================
    AND IT SHOULD BE DECIDED ONCE, FOR BOTH SPACES
    ============================================================

    THE LIBRARY IS REFERENCE MATERIAL AND THE STORE IS THE GAME, and a reviewer
    has a reason to read both. If only one is widened the difference becomes a
    question somebody re-opens — the shape item 78 is about. Whatever is
    decided, decide it for both, or say in so many words why they differ.

    ============================================================
    WHAT IS NOT IN QUESTION
    ============================================================

    THE DOWNLOAD ITSELF. None of this is about how a version is fetched: the
    negotiation, the archive and the manifest are built and verified, and they
    work for whatever role the route admits. This item is only about who that
    is.

84) TWO WAYS TO DO THE SAME THING — AN AUDIT. ASKED FOR 2026-10-15.
    Not started.

    THE RULE, in the owner's words: "make sure that the code is not being
    duplicated — i.e. two or more ways to do the same thing." It is the same
    rule item 55 states for uploads, and the one item 82 was found by breaking
    twice over.

    --- COUNTED, SO THE SIZE IS KNOWN BEFORE ANYBODY STARTS ---

    Frontend helpers defined more than once, by name:

        showAlert    defined in 8 files
        render       defined in 11 files
        init         defined in 5 files
        row          defined in 4 files
        formatWhen   defined in 3 files
        cell         defined in 3 files

    THE NAMES ARE NOT ALL THE SAME THING, and the count is not the finding.
    `render` and `init` are generic — each file's is its own and probably
    should stay. `showAlert` IS the same function in several of them: take a
    message and a Bootstrap variant, set an alert div's text and class.
    `formatWhen` and `cell` look the same way, and both are small enough to be
    certain about by reading.

    SO THE WORK IS SEPARATING TWO CASES — functions that are genuinely the same
    and belong in one place, and functions that merely share a name. Merging on
    the name alone would be a worse mess than the duplication.

    --- AND THERE IS A PRECEDENT, SO THIS IS NOT A CODEBASE THAT CANNOT ---

    shared/formatBytes.mjs is authored once and imported by the frontend, the
    backend and the tests. That is the shape to copy, and it already exists.

    --- ONE THAT IS DEFINITELY WRONG, FOUND WHILE LOOKING ---

    CLIENT_URL IS DEFINED TWICE IN .env, at lines 6 and 9. Whichever the loader
    takes wins, and nothing says which — an answer that is right by accident,
    which is the failure mode this whole file keeps recording.

    AND TWO FILES CARRY THEIR OWN COPY OF ITS VALUE:

        backend/routes/inviteDevs.js:42   process.env.CLIENT_URL || 'http://localhost:5173'
        backend/lib/invites.js:114        clientUrl || 'http://localhost:5173'

    Those are fallbacks rather than hardcoding, so they are the mild version of
    the problem — but they are a second place that decides where the portal
    lives, and a third and fourth once the .env copies are counted.

    --- WHY IT IS WORTH DOING ---

    EVERY DUPLICATE IS A PLACE TWO COPIES CAN DRIFT APART. Item 82 is that
    failure exactly: the upload and the import asked the same question, got it
    wrong the same way, and had to be fixed twice — and the second one was
    worse, because it reported success while losing the work. A shared helper
    cannot disagree with itself.

85) THE ROUND TRIP — WHERE IT STANDS, AND WHAT IS LEFT. 2026-10-15.
    To be picked up next session, at the owner's request.

    THE ROUND TRIP, as the owner put it (item 79): upload → check → submit →
    approve → in the store → find it → download → work on it → upload →
    approve → REPLACES the old one in the game.

    ============================================================
    WHAT IS VERIFIED
    ============================================================

      UPLOAD INTO THE WORKSPACE       done by val, 2026-10-15.
      TECHNICAL CHECK                 done in the same session.
      SUBMIT FOR APPROVAL             done.
      APPROVE AS prod                 done — approval 2, decided by user 14.
                                      Wrote repository_asset_id 3 and
                                      repository_version_id 2.
      IN THE GAME STORE               asset 3 "TestUpload", version 1, 3 files.
      SEARCH AND BROWSE               done.
      DOWNLOAD TO THE LOCAL MACHINE   VERIFIED END TO END in a real browser,
                                      both directions:
                                        · nothing present → the list of 3 files,
                                          a 211-BYTE body of three hashes, and
                                          TestUpload_3files.zip on disk;
                                        · everything present → "you already have
                                          every file in this version. Nothing
                                          was fetched." — no request, no zip.
                                      Two store.download rows exist, so it has
                                      also been done by hand.

    ============================================================
    WHAT IS NOT — AND IT IS THE PART THAT CLOSES THE LOOP
    ============================================================

      IMPORT                          ATTEMPTED, AND CORRECTLY REFUSED.
                                      At 16:47:28 UTC val imported TestUpload and
                                      was told "already there" — the asset it
                                      would have created has the same name and
                                      came from the same store asset. That is
                                      the right answer, and it is NOT the round
                                      trip closing: nothing came DOWN into a
                                      workspace that did not have it.

      THE LINK BACK TO THE STORE      NEVER EXERCISED. Every look at
                                      repository_base_version_id in the database
                                      is empty:

                                          workspace_assets.id 20
                                            repository_asset_id        3
                                            repository_base_version_id  (empty)

                                      THAT COLUMN IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM that
                                      stops a second round creating a SECOND
                                      asset with the same name — item 59's
                                      failure, "two bigTrees and nobody can tell
                                      them apart". The import route's own
                                      comment says it: "prevented by those two
                                      columns and nothing else." It has never
                                      been written on this data, because the one
                                      asset in the round trip was UPLOADED and
                                      then approved, never imported.

      A NEW VERSION REPLACING THE OLD  NOT EXERCISED, and it rests entirely on
                                      the column above.

    ============================================================
    WHAT TO DO NEXT SESSION
    ============================================================

    1. IMPORT SOMETHING THE WORKSPACE DOES NOT HAVE. The import leg has only
       ever been answered "already there", so the branch that CREATES a workspace
       asset from a store version has never run. Check it writes
       repository_asset_id AND repository_base_version_id — the second is the
       one nothing has ever set.

    2. UPLOAD A CHANGED VERSION ONTO IT, submit, approve as prod.

    3. ASSERT THE ROUND TRIP CLOSED. The store should still hold ONE asset with
       that name, now with TWO versions, the second the newer one. If there are
       two assets with the same name, the base-version link did not do its job —
       and that is item 59 exactly.

    4. CHECK THE HISTORY says what was done, since it is the developer's only
       record of the trip.

    ============================================================
    AND HOW THE BROWSER HALF WAS TESTED, WHICH IS WORTH KEEPING
    ============================================================

    THE DOWNLOAD WAS DRIVEN IN A REAL BROWSER over the DevTools protocol: sign
    in through the actual form — BOTH steps, because every account comes back
    requires2FA and the page says to use 000000 when no authenticator is set
    up — open the Game store, click with a real user gesture, and then read the
    DOM, the console, the requests and the downloads.

    IT FOUND A BUG READING COULD NOT. A window.confirm() before the folder
    chooser spends the user activation the chooser needs, so the download died
    silently: no dialog, no error, no message. See the note at the top of
    frontend/src/developerStore.js.

    AND IT CANNOT DO EVERYTHING. A headless browser cannot be made to pick a
    folder — CDP will not populate a `<input webkitdirectory>` and Chrome treats
    the attempt as a dismissal — so the folder's contents were supplied at that
    one point, with the real bytes and the real change event. Everything either
    side of it was the application's own code.

    THE HARNESS LIVES IN /tmp AND WILL BE GONE. If the browser half is tested
    again — and it should be, for the upload and approval legs too — it wants to
    live in the repository rather than in /tmp. NOT DONE, DELIBERATELY: where
    and how this project tests the browser is a decision nobody has made, and
    adding a runner is that decision, not a tidy-up.

86) THE LIBRARY IS NOT A THIRD SPACE. IT IS THE WHOLE SYSTEM, AND THE GAME IS
    A SUBSET OF IT. RAISED 2026-10-15 BY THE OWNER, WHO SAID TO CHECK THE SPEC.
    The spec agrees with him and this file did not.

    ============================================================
    WHAT THE SPEC SAYS — docs/OpenWorld Asset Library.pdf
    ============================================================

    "The OpenWorld Asset Library is the central place for storing, discovering
    and sharing the things we create for the game."

    "It can contain anything from a photograph used as reference to a finished
    Unreal asset."

    AND THE JOURNEY, in the spec's own diagram:

        CREATE → PRIVATE → PUBLISHED → SUBMIT FOR GAME → TECHNICAL TEST
               → DIRECTOR REVIEW → GAME APPROVED → CURRENT GAME VERSION

    WITH SHARE and DISCUSS hanging off PUBLISHED, and REJECT looping back to
    REVISE.

    AND THE SENTENCE THE WHOLE THING RESTS ON:

        "The most important rule is: Publishing an asset does not put it in the
         game."

    ============================================================
    THE FIVE STAGES, AS THE SPEC DESCRIBES THEM
    ============================================================

      🔒 PRIVATE      "your working area". Upload work in progress, replace
                      files, experiment, delete versions. Visible to
                      "You · Admin · Superuser. Other developers cannot see
                      them."

      🌍 PUBLISHED    "a fixed version". Others may view it, download it, use it
                      as reference, discuss it, or "use it as the starting point
                      for their own work". "They cannot overwrite your published
                      version. If they want to change it, they create their own
                      version."

                      AND PUBLISHED IS NOT APPROVED: "There is no requirement to
                      submit every published asset for game approval."

      🚦 SUBMIT FOR GAME   "The owner decides when a particular version is ready
                      to be considered for the game. Only then does the approval
                      process begin."

      🧪 TECHNICAL TEST    "Before an asset can enter the controlled game, it
                      must be tested against the appropriate official game
                      build." ... "The system records the exact asset and exact
                      game build that were tested. You cannot simply type:
                      'Tested against Development.' The system knows exactly
                      which build was used."

      🎨 DIRECTOR APPROVAL "Technical correctness is only half of the process.
                      The Director decides whether the asset is artistically
                      suitable." An asset may be "Technically correct but
                      artistically rejected" or "Artistically excellent but
                      technically not ready". "Both decisions are recorded
                      separately."

    AND TWO MORE THINGS THAT ARE NOT BUILT AT ALL:

      BRANCHES AND BUILDS.  Production (stable), Development (current),
                      Experimental, "Additional branches can be created when
                      required. Each branch has official builds." A test is
                      against ONE BUILD: "A developer who tested against Build
                      203 cannot submit that test when Build 204 is the required
                      current build. The system will require the developer to
                      obtain the appropriate build and test again."

      THE GAME'S VERSION IS NOT THE LATEST VERSION.  The spec's example has Old
                      Factory at v5 published while "Current game version: v3".
                      "The game therefore explicitly records which version is
                      currently being used."

    AND: "An asset can belong to more than one category" — the spec's own
    example is "Oak tree photograph — Reference · Vegetation · Trees ·
    Environment" — and "An asset can have multiple tags and classifications."

    ============================================================
    WHAT IS BUILT, AND WHERE IT DIVERGES
    ============================================================

      SPEC STAGE          WHAT EXISTS TODAY
      ------------------  -------------------------------------------------
      PRIVATE             the WORKSPACE. Private-ish, but there is no notion
                          of a version that only you can see versus one
                          colleagues can: a workspace is invisible to
                          everybody else entirely.
      PUBLISHED           NOTHING. This is the stage the whole design hangs
                          on and it has no implementation. There is no way to
                          make a version visible to colleagues without also
                          submitting it for the game.
      SUBMIT FOR GAME     creative_approvals, submitted by the developer.
      TECHNICAL TEST      technical_check_runs / _results. Real, and tied to a
                          workspace version — BUT NOT TO A GAME BUILD, which is
                          the one thing the spec is emphatic about.
      DIRECTOR APPROVAL   creative_approvals decided by the role named 'prod'.
                          The spec says DIRECTOR, and calls it an artistic
                          decision recorded SEPARATELY from the technical one.
      GAME APPROVED       an asset_version appearing in the store.
      CURRENT GAME        asset_recommended_versions EXISTS —
      VERSION             asset_id + version_id, one row per asset — and is
                          EMPTY. It is precisely the spec's "the game explicitly
                          records which version is currently being used", and
                          nothing writes it.
      BRANCHES / BUILDS   NOTHING. No branch, no build, no commit.
      CATEGORIES          ONE column, `category`, single-valued. The spec wants
                          several per asset.
      NOTIFICATIONS       backend/lib/notify.js exists, aimed at admins. The
                          spec wants the OWNER told when a test passes or fails
                          and when the director decides.

    ============================================================
    WHICH MAKES ITEMS 57 AND 58 WRONG, AND THEY ARE MINE
    ============================================================

    ITEM 57 built the library as "the third space", alongside the workspace and
    the store. THE SPEC MAKES IT THE FIRST SPACE: the store from which
    everything is taken, of which the game's content is a subset.

    ITEM 58 says "an image is not a game asset", which is true about the GAME
    and was used to argue the library must therefore be somewhere else. The
    spec's answer is the other way round: the library holds BOTH — "anything
    from a photograph used as reference to a finished Unreal asset" — and the
    question of what is in the game is asked LATER, at submit.

    SO THE SHAPE IS NOT THREE SPACES. IT IS ONE LIBRARY, with two things
    recorded about its contents:

        the STAGE a version has reached   (private → published → submitted →
                                           tested → approved)
        the GAME VERSIONS it is in        (one, several, or none)

    The workspace and the store are then VIEWS of that one thing rather than
    separate places with separate copies.

    ============================================================
    WHAT THE OWNER ASKED FOR, AND HOW IT SITS AGAINST THE SPEC
    ============================================================

      "devs can upload stuff to the library"        the spec says so.
      "remove the approval by production"           the spec agrees that
                                                    PUBLISHING needs no
                                                    approval. Approval is only
                                                    for entering the GAME, and
                                                    it is the DIRECTOR's.
      "the admin should be informed that stuff has  the spec wants the OWNER
       been uploaded"                               notified; private versions
                                                    are visible to admin. Not a
                                                    contradiction, an addition.
      "when the dev is uploading things to be       THE SPEC PUTS THE TEST LATER,
       included in the game it goes through the     at SUBMIT FOR GAME, not at
       tech checks and if it fails it can't be      upload. See the question
       uploaded, otherwise it goes to the library"  below — this is the one place
                                                    the two readings differ.
      "specify which part of the game"              the spec's multiple
                                                    categories.
      "pass the tech checks then be submitted for   exactly the spec's stages 4
       approval for inclusion by the prod"          and 5 — with the naming
                                                    question below.
      "the library is the complete asset store"     THE SPEC'S OWN WORDS.
      "an asset may be in one or many versions of   the spec: branches, builds,
       the game"                                    and a recorded current
                                                    version per asset.

    ============================================================
    THE QUESTIONS THAT ACTUALLY BLOCK
    ============================================================

    1. DOES THE TECH CHECK RUN AT UPLOAD, OR AT SUBMIT? The owner says at
       upload, for anything destined for the game. The spec puts it at submit,
       against a specific build. These are different rules: at upload there is
       nothing to test against yet, because a build has to be named.

    2. IS 'prod' THE DIRECTOR? The role exists and decides creative approvals
       today. The spec says Director and treats it as an artistic decision
       separate from the technical one. Either the role is renamed, or the
       director is somebody else.

    3. WHAT IS A "VERSION OF THE GAME" — a BRANCH (Production, Development,
       Experimental), or a BUILD within a branch, or a release? Item 60 has
       this open already. It decides what "in one or many versions" means.

    4. DOES THE WORKSPACE SURVIVE? If the library is the whole system, the
       workspace is either the PRIVATE stage of a library asset, or something
       else. It has its own tables, quota, bin and history today.

    NOTHING IS CHANGED YET. This item is the correction to the model, written
    down before anything is built on top of it.

    ============================================================
    SETTLED WITH THE OWNER, 2026-10-16
    ============================================================

    HE ANSWERED ALL FOUR. Where an answer differs from the spec, THE ANSWER
    WINS — the spec describes intent, it does not constrain.

    --- 1. THE TECH CHECK RUNS AT UPLOAD, NOT AT SUBMIT ---

        "Anything that is uploaded must pass a tech check before it is put in
         the asset store - our unique SHA store"

    SO THE GATE MOVES ONE STAGE FORWARD FROM THE SPEC. The spec tests at
    SUBMIT FOR GAME, against a named build; the owner tests at UPLOAD, before
    anything enters the store at all. NOTHING ENTERS THE STORE UNTESTED.

    WHICH ALSO RESOLVES THE BUILD QUESTION RATHER THAN POSTPONING IT: a check
    at upload cannot name a game build, because the asset is not going into a
    game yet. The build matters at SUBMIT FOR GAME, and THAT is where the
    spec's "tested against Build 203" rule belongs. THE TWO ARE NOT IN
    CONFLICT — two different tests at two different stages, and only the
    second is about a build. Worth saying plainly because it looked like a
    contradiction an hour ago.

    --- 2. 'prod' IS THE DIRECTOR ---

        "Prod could be this director - production decides game asset approvals"

    THE ROLE EXISTS AND ALREADY DECIDES CREATIVE APPROVALS. Nothing is
    renamed. The spec's DIRECTOR REVIEW is the creative_approvals queue and
    'prod' is who decides it.

    AND THE SPEC'S SPLIT HOLDS: "Both decisions are recorded separately." The
    technical result is technical_check_runs; the artistic decision is the
    approval. Two records, two stages, and neither implies the other — which
    is the point of the spec's "artistically excellent but technically not
    ready".

    --- 3. THREE GAME VERSIONS ---

        "The game will have a production version a candidate dev version and
         an experimental version - possibly"

    PRODUCTION · CANDIDATE DEV · EXPERIMENTAL. The spec's Production,
    Development and Experimental, with the middle one named as a candidate.

    "POSSIBLY" IS DOING WORK IN THAT SENTENCE, and this belongs with item 60,
    which is about game versions and release candidates. NOT SETTLED HERE.

    AN ASSET MAY BE IN ONE, SEVERAL, OR NONE OF THEM — which is the mechanism
    asset_recommended_versions already exists for and nothing writes.

    --- 4. A WORKSPACE UPLOAD IS A STORE ASSET WITH AN OWNER ---

        "The workspace uploads go to the asset store but they have an owner -
         the dev - unlike the other assets - so these are special assets that
         can only be read by the dev who uploaded them and also deleted"

    THIS IS THE SPEC'S PRIVATE STAGE, and the owner is right that it has been
    through before: ITEM 78 FOUND THE GAP. He is also right that the answer was
    never written down, only the gap — which is how the same ground gets
    covered twice.

    SO A WORKSPACE UPLOAD IS NOT A DIFFERENT KIND OF THING IN A DIFFERENT
    PLACE. It is an asset in the one store, with an owner, at the PRIVATE
    stage:

        only the OWNER may read it
        only the OWNER may delete it
        nobody else sees it at all until it is published

    --- AND ONE DIFFERENCE FROM THE SPEC, FOR THE OWNER TO SETTLE ---

    The spec says a private version is visible to "You · Admin · Superuser".
    The owner says "only ... the dev who uploaded them". Superuser is gone
    (item 51), so it is admin the question is about. AN ADMIN WHO CANNOT SEE
    ANYTHING PRIVATE CANNOT HELP ANYBODY WHO HAS LOST SOMETHING, and the admin
    panel exists. NOT SETTLED — one line from the owner decides it.

    ============================================================
    THE SHAPE THAT FALLS OUT OF THE FOUR
    ============================================================

    ONE STORE, AND EVERYTHING IN IT HAS PASSED A TECH CHECK. Every asset in it
    carries:

        an OWNER         who uploaded it
        a STAGE          private → published → submitted → tested → approved
        GAME VERSIONS    production, candidate dev, experimental — one,
                         several, or none

    THE GAME'S CONTENT IS THE SUBSET APPROVED INTO A GAME VERSION. That is
    what the game store shows. The workspace is the PRIVATE END OF THE SAME
    THING, not a separate place holding separate files.

    ============================================================
    WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
    ============================================================

    NOTHING IS BUILT. These four answers settle the MODEL. Moving three spaces
    into one, and adding stages, ownership and game versions, has not started.

    IT ALSO MEANS ITEMS 57 AND 58 ARE NOW KNOWN TO BE WRONG RATHER THAN MERELY
    IN DOUBT, and any code relying on "the library is source material only, and
    an image is never a game asset" is relying on a mistake.

    AND THIS ITEM OVERLAPS ITEM 78, which had already found both the missing
    PUBLISHED stage and the two different things called the library. Item 78 is
    the audit; this is the decision. READ THEM TOGETHER, and do not let them
    become two accounts of the same thing — which is precisely what item 84 is
    about.

    ============================================================
    AND THE ANSWER TO THE PRIVATE QUESTION, 2026-10-16
    ============================================================

    PRIVATE IS THE OWNER'S ALONE. Admins do not get a read path; they get in by
    IMPERSONATION, which the owner describes as "login as", "done in
    exceptional circumstances".

    SO THE SPEC'S "You · Admin · Superuser" IS SUPERSEDED TWICE OVER: superuser
    is gone (item 51), and admin is IN by impersonation rather than by default
    visibility.

    --- AND IMPERSONATION IS NOT BUILT, WHICH THE OWNER BELIEVED IT WAS ---

    He said it "has been there for a long time". What has been there a long
    time is the RULE, which is argued at length in three files — and the rule
    is right. The MECHANISM is not there at all:

      * sessions.is_impersonated and sessions.impersonated_by exist and are
        NEVER READ OR WRITTEN. They are schema, and nothing else.
      * Only three places mint a session token — backend/server.js:211,
        backend/server.js:404, backend/routes/auth.js:50 — and all three mint
        for the person who just authenticated. No route takes a user id to act
        as.
      * backend/generated/manual.html already says "impersonation is not built
        yet" and lists it as item 5.
      * ITS ENTRY POINT is `signInAsBtn`, recorded in this file as "deferred by
        decision" — and it lived on the SUPERUSER dashboard, which was deleted
        on 2026-10-06. The entry point went with the role it was put on.

    SO TODAY AN ADMIN CANNOT SEE A DEVELOPER'S PRIVATE WORK AT ALL. That was
    survivable while the private side was a workspace nobody had been promised
    anything about. It stops being survivable the moment private is the
    OWNER-ONLY stage of the one asset store — because then a developer who
    loses something, or leaves, or uploads something they should not have, has
    no route to help and no route to investigation.

    IMPERSONATION IS THEREFORE A PREREQUISITE OF THE PRIVATE STAGE, not a
    separate feature. Marked, time-bounded and audit-logged, as the rule
    already says in three places.

87) THE SPEC RECONCILIATION — DISCREPANCIES BETWEEN THE CODE, THE PDFS AND THIS
    FILE. Listed 2026-10-16; being ruled on one at a time with the owner.
    Overlaps items 78 and 80, which audited parts of this earlier. Those are the
    audits; this is the consolidated list and the rulings.

    ============================================================
    A1. SESSION LIFETIME — SETTLED 2026-10-23
    ============================================================

    THE DISCREPANCY. The manual promises an 8-hour session
    (docs/manual/20-getting-in.md:53,58); the login the browser actually uses
    issues 2 hours (server.js:411,419). And there are TWO login endpoints with
    different lifetimes — the dead POST /api/auth/login issues 8h
    (routes/auth.js:53) — which is a second way to do one thing (item 84).

    THE DECISION. SESSIONS EXPIRE ON IDLENESS, NOT ON A FIXED CLOCK.

        developer      idle 2 hours, then a warning, then 3 minutes to respond
        prod / admin   idle 30 minutes, absolute cap 8 hours
        everyone       ABSOLUTE CAP 24 HOURS, however active

    AN ACTIVE SESSION IS NOT LOGGED OUT. The 24-hour cap is the outer bound: a
    token somebody else is using dies within a day whatever they do with it.

    --- WHAT IT TAKES ---

      * sessions.last_seen_at. The table exists, is unused, and already has
        user_id, token, ip_address, user_agent, is_impersonated, impersonated_by,
        created_at and expires_at (db.js:1577). expires_at does the absolute
        cap; last_seen_at does the idle check.
      * authenticateToken gains an indexed session lookup. It already re-reads
        the user from the database on EVERY request (middleware/auth.js:33), so
        this is one more read on a path that already does one.
      * The idle value is an app_settings definition — lib/settings.js is
        already definition-driven, with validate and bounds — NOT a constant.

    --- THE THREE THINGS THAT WILL BITE ---

      1. THE SERVER ENFORCES; THE POPUP IS A COURTESY. A closed or sleeping tab
         runs no timer, so client-side-only expiry logs nobody out. Today's
         fixed JWT does enforce itself, so skipping the server side would be a
         REGRESSION in the one respect that matters.
      2. THE COOKIE'S maxAge IS CURRENTLY 2 HOURS (server.js:419) and would log
         people out BEFORE any idle check could fire. It has to become
         long-lived and let the server decide.
      3. "ACTIVITY" MUST COUNT A REQUEST IN FLIGHT. last_seen_at updates when a
         request STARTS, or a two-hour upload looks like idleness and the
         developer is logged out mid-upload.

    --- AND IT UNBLOCKS IMPERSONATION ---

      A server-side session is exactly what item 86's impersonation needs:
      time-bounded by expires_at, marked by is_impersonated, attributed by
      impersonated_by. It also gives the portal REAL REVOCATION, which it does
      not have today — routes/auth.js:91-93 says so in as many words.

    --- ALSO DECIDED ---

      * The dead POST /api/auth/login is removed. One way to get a session.
      * The manual's "a session lasts 8 hours" section is rewritten: idle-based,
        with the warning, and both caps stated.
      * The industry norm for an internal tool is about a working day of idle
        and 1-14 days absolute. 2 hours idle and 24 hours absolute is stricter
        than the norm, deliberately.

    ============================================================
    A2. ADMINISTRATOR INVITATIONS — SETTLED 2026-10-23
    ============================================================

    THE DISCREPANCY. docs/manual/20-getting-in.md told a contributor that
    "administrator accounts are not invited by anyone ... it does not come as a
    link", while docs/manual/50-administering.md documents `accounts.js
    --invite --role admin` as "THE USUAL WAY", and scripts/accounts.js:416
    invites nothing EXCEPT admin and prod — developers and testers are invited
    from the admin page instead.

    THE RULING: PAGE 20 WAS STALE. It described the policy from before
    --invite existed, when a privileged account could only be made by typing a
    password at the console. Page 50 is right, and argues for it: nobody picks
    a password on somebody else's behalf and then has to pass it on.

    AND IT WEAKENS NOTHING. The invitation is still ISSUED FROM THE MACHINE, by
    somebody with shell access, which is the property accounts.js exists to
    keep. --invite moves WHEN the password is chosen, not WHO may grant the
    role.

    CORRECTED IN docs/manual/20-getting-in.md and the manual rebuilt. What was
    kept from the old paragraph is the only thing a contributor needs from it:
    an invitation is an ordinary link, so you know what to look for.

    ============================================================
    A3. THE TECHNICAL CHECK, IN assettypes01 — SETTLED 2026-10-23
    ============================================================

    THE DISCREPANCY. docs/assettypes01 said "nothing inspects file contents"
    (§A) and that "Only 3 generic checks exist: files_present, stored_file:<id>,
    logical_paths_unique" (§4).

    THE CODE HAS SEVEN, and one of them reads the bytes:

        files_present             stored_file:<id>
        logical_paths_unique      asset_types_recognised
        files_not_empty           no_executable_content
        content_matches_extension

    content_matches_extension calls checkFileContent() in
    shared/assetRules.mjs:310, which reads the first 512 bytes and matches them
    against a signature table — confirmed / contradicted / unverifiable. A JPEG
    renamed .png is caught.

    THE RULING: CORRECT THE DOCUMENT. Its PREMISE was wrong even though its
    CONCLUSION was partly right: the format-compliance gate it proposes —
    polygon limits, texture dimensions, naming conventions — is genuinely
    unbuilt. Almost everything else it said was missing is there.

    AND A WRONG PREMISE IS THE DANGEROUS HALF, because it invites somebody to
    build content_matches_extension a second time. That is item 84's failure
    mode arriving through a stale document rather than through code.

    CORRECTED IN docs/assettypes01: the status line (it said "NOT STARTED"),
    §A's "nothing inspects file contents", and §4's "only 3 generic checks",
    which now names all seven and says plainly that only the compliance gate is
    missing.

    ============================================================
    STILL TO RULE ON
    ============================================================

    A. THE SPEC IS WRONG
      A2  administrator invitations — SETTLED above
      A3  the technical check in assettypes01 — SETTLED above
      A4  assettypes01's "current state" snapshot of the assets table is stale
      A5  dbspec01 names `files`, `file references`, `recommended_version_id` —
          none of those names exist in the schema
      A6  the manual says prod manages asset categories and boards; it cannot
      A7  the manual says revoking portal access revokes board access; unbuilt
      A8  assettypes01's controlled vocabulary for asset_type was never applied
      A9  uploadmodel01 says filename_overrides is dead code; it is live
      A10 uploadmodel01's central change was never made — upload still versions

    B. THE SPECS CONTRADICT EACH OTHER OR THEMSELVES
      B1  the game is "maintained separately in GitHub" (both PDFs) against
          "all game source code ... in a central private GitHub repository"
          (Specification_v04_web.html) — and against the TODO's Perforce
      B2  "published files should be treated as immutable" against a permission
          table giving admin read/write/delete on a published version
      B3  is state on the asset (assets.status) or the version
          (asset_versions.visibility)? Item 86 settled it per VERSION
      B4  does a second developer add a version to your asset (the Repository
          PDF's worked example) or fork it (the System PDF's ownership model)?

    C. THE CODE IS WRONG
      C1  zero audit records across submit, test, approve, reject
      C2  assets has no owner column at all
      C3  a single category column, which the spec explicitly forbids
      C4  session lifetime — settled above as A1

    D. THE TODO OVERRULED THE SPEC WITHOUT SAYING SO
      D1  GitHub -> Perforce for the game (the PDFs make it structural)
      D2  in-portal chat -> a separate board reached by a link
      D3  deploy-any-branch-to-a-sandbox -> nothing

    E. THE SPEC DESCRIBES IT AND NOTHING IMPLEMENTS IT
      E1  the test receipt protocol — harness, build id, manifest hash,
          server-side validation of a submission
      E2  a deterministic version manifest hash
      E3  permissions, licences and commercial-use provenance
      E4  notifications to the asset's OWNER on test/approval events
      E5  branches and official builds
      E6  game_branch_asset_versions — the version currently used PER BRANCH;
          asset_recommended_versions is one row per asset and cannot express an
          asset being in production and candidate dev at once
      E7  asynchronous processing with a visible state
      E8  AI classification tables and the "human wins" rule
      E9  signed upload URLs
      E10 a storage_provider abstraction
      E11 the Browser Sandbox as an asset consumer
      E12 user-role access to assets made available to users
